, for one webinar | **Behavior** → no-show, plus **Webinar** |"],[5858,"| You want                                   | Chat                           | Q&A     |\n| ------------------------------------------ | ------------------------------ | ------- |\n| An automated webinar that still feels busy | **Everyone** + Chat Simulation | Private |"],[6177,"| You want                                   | Chat                       | Q&A             |\n| ------------------------------------------ | -------------------------- | --------------- |\n| An automated webinar that still feels busy | Everyone + Chat Simulation | **Private Q&A** |"],[5856,"| You want                                   | Chat           | Q&A     |\n| ------------------------------------------ | -------------- | ------- |\n| A sales session with private conversations | **Hosts only** | Private |"],[6174,"| You want                                   | Chat       | Q&A             |\n| ------------------------------------------ | ---------- | --------------- |\n| A sales session with private conversations | Hosts only | **Private Q&A** |"],[5857,"| You want                          | Chat           | Q&A     |\n| --------------------------------- | -------------- | ------- |\n| A tightly controlled presentation | **Hosts only** | Disable |"],[6175,"| You want                          | Chat       | Q&A         |\n| --------------------------------- | ---------- | ----------- |\n| A tightly controlled presentation | Hosts only | **Disable** |"],[4494,"| You want                         | Filter                                           |\n| -------------------------------- | ------------------------------------------------ |\n| Interested people who didn't buy | **Behavior** → high watch time and offer clicked |"],[6649,"| You want                        | Choose           |\n| ------------------------------- | ---------------- |\n| To broadcast live, in real time | **Live Webinar** |"],[5855,"| You want                | Chat         | Q&A    |\n| ----------------------- | ------------ | ------ |\n| A lively public webinar | **Everyone** | Public |"],[6173,"| You want                | Chat     | Q&A            |\n| ----------------------- | -------- | -------------- |\n| A lively public webinar | Everyone | **Public Q&A** |"],[1571,"| You want to                                                         | Use        |\n| ------------------------------------------------------------------- | ---------- |\n| Send attendees and no-shows their own follow-up after every webinar | A workflow |"],[1573,"| You want to                                                 | Use        |\n| ----------------------------------------------------------- | ---------- |\n| Give each new lead a short sequence over the next fortnight | A workflow |"],[1574,"| You want to                                             | Use        |\n| ------------------------------------------------------- | ---------- |\n| Reach someone who hasn't opened anything in ninety days | A workflow |"],[1570,"| You want to                                         | Use         |\n| --------------------------------------------------- | ----------- |\n| Send one replay link to last night's no-shows, once | A broadcast |"],[1572,"| You want to                                  | Use         |\n| -------------------------------------------- | ----------- |\n| Announce a date change to everyone on a list | A broadcast |"],[2666,"| Your rule                 | How to set it                                                                        |\n| ------------------------- | ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------ |\n| Replay for a limited time | Turn on **Enable replay page** and choose **Expire after days** or **Specific date** |"],[2665,"| Your rule                 | How to set it                                       |\n| ------------------------- | --------------------------------------------------- |\n| Replay sold as an upgrade | Turn on **Live + Replay bundle** with its own price |"],[2664,"| Your rule            | How to set it                                              |\n| -------------------- | ---------------------------------------------------------- |\n| Live only, no replay | Leave **Live + Replay bundle** off and the replay page off |"],[3610,"| Your situation                                         | First email             | Second email | Last email                                                  |\n| ------------------------------------------------------ | ----------------------- | ------------ | ----------------------------------------------------------- |\n| Recorded webinar, where people watch on different days | Right after the session | A day later  | Just before a rolling deadline, written as a number of days |"],[3607,"| Your situation                              | First email             | Second email | Last email                  |\n| ------------------------------------------- | ----------------------- | ------------ | --------------------------- |\n| Offer or replay closes in five days or more | Right after the session | A day later  | The day before the deadline |"],[3609,"| Your situation                         | First email             | Second email                                     | Last email  |\n| -------------------------------------- | ----------------------- | ------------------------------------------------ | ----------- |\n| No deadline, such as customer training | Right after the session | A day later, if you have something useful to add | None needed |"],[3608,"| Your situation                         | First email             | Second email | Last email                  |\n| -------------------------------------- | ----------------------- | ------------ | --------------------------- |\n| Offer or replay closes within 48 hours | Right after the session | Skip it      | The morning of the deadline |"],[7188,"1. Add your presenters and moderators"],[2111,"1. Create the client's workspace"],[150,"1. Describe your webinar"],[4412,"1. Open People"],[7888,"1. Open the Library"],[4559,"1. Open the tab and set the period"],[7322,"1. Open the webinar room"],[754,"1. Welcome and introduction (about 3 minutes)"],[1675,"15 U.S. Code 7704, Legal Information Institute: the CAN-SPAM rules on opt-out requests for commercial email."],[3766,"15 U.S. Code 7704, Legal Information Institute: the CAN-SPAM rules on opt-outs, postal addresses and subject lines, in full."],[3969,"15 U.S. Code 7704, Legal Information Institute: the CAN-SPAM rules on opt-outs, postal addresses and subject lines."],[2930,"15 U.S. Code 7704, Legal Information Institute: the US CAN-SPAM rules on opt-outs and postal addresses for follow-up emails with a sales message."],[2112,"2. Add your team, not the client, as team members"],[7889,"2. Check your videos and where each one is hosted"],[7189,"2. Enter the green room"],[755,"2. Housekeeping (about 1 minute)"],[7323,"2. Pass the device check"],[4560,"2. Read the funnel"],[4413,"2. Read the table"],[152,"2. Review the script"],[4414,"3. Filter the list"],[7890,"3. Find recordings of webinars you've run"],[4561,"3. Find where people drop off"],[153,"3. Generate the pages"],[7324,"3. Get ready in the green room"],[2113,"3. Put the client's pages on their domain"],[7190,"3. Read the room"],[756,"3. Who you are (about 2 minutes)"],[7891,"4. Add images for your pages"],[7191,"4. Bring people backstage and onto the stage"],[4562,"4. Check engagement and offers"],[7325,"4. Check recording"],[4415,"4. Combine filters for common follow-ups"],[154,"4. Generate the emails"],[2114,"4. Remove EasyWebinar branding"],[757,"4. The problem (about 5 minutes)"],[2115,"5. Build the first funnel from an agency template"],[4416,"5. Export the result"],[7192,"5. Finish your checks"],[155,"5. Generate the polls"],[7892,"5. Keep documents in Files"],[4563,"5. See the people behind the numbers"],[7326,"5. Style the room"],[758,"5. Teaching points (about 20 minutes)"],[7893,"6. Add new media"],[4564,"6. Check revenue"],[2116,"6. Connect the client's CRM"],[156,"6. Generate the offers"],[7193,"6. Leave the green room"],[7327,"6. Switch on streaming destinations (optional)"],[759,"6. Transition to the offer (about 2 minutes)"],[2117,"7. Clone the funnel for next month or the next client"],[7328,"7. Go live"],[157,"7. Land on your finished webinar"],[760,"7. The offer (about 7 minutes)"],[7894,"7. Use a video or recording in a webinar"],[7329,"8. Present"],[761,"8. Questions (about 4 minutes)"],[762,"9. Close (about 1 minute)"],[7330,"9. End the session"],[9274,"A **Shorten URL's** toggle gives you shorter versions."],[73,"A **team member** has a login. A **presenter** or **moderator** runs a specific webinar and is added on that webinar's Overview tab. Presenters and moderators can join with a link, without an account."],[3618,"A 2011 Harvard Business Review article on online sales enquiries reported that most companies weren't responding nearly fast enough. It looked at companies handling online queries, not webinars, and it's an old study. Registering is still a similar first move, which is one more reason to send your first email today."],[1189,"A B2B marketing team runs prospect webinars and hands the warm ones to sales. It imports each session's attendees, builds a list of offer clickers and opens a deal for each one. The pipeline board then shows sales where the follow-up is stuck. The guide to webinar lead generation covers the earlier steps."],[2813,"A booking link suits a programme where you need to talk before anyone joins. It catches the people who are ready to talk this week. It misses people who want to think it over, so they need a follow-up email with the same link."],[2688,"A breathwork teacher sells a monthly live workshop with a **Live + Replay bundle**, so people in other time zones can buy the recording. **Hosts only** chat keeps personal questions private."],[1178,"A broadcast is a one-off send to a list, such as a replay link after a webinar. You open it from **Marketing → Broadcasts**, choose the channel, pick the list, write the message, then send or schedule it. The screen shows average open and click rates beside benchmarks of 21% for opens and 2.6% for clicks."],[1569,"A broadcast is a one-off send to a list: a replay link after a webinar, an offer closing on Friday, or an announcement. A workflow is for anything that repeats or reacts to behaviour. If you'd send the same message again after the next webinar, it belongs in a workflow."],[1220,"A broadcast is a one-off send to a list. A workflow is an automated sequence that keeps sending when people do something, without you being there."],[2897,"A business coach for first-time founders runs one live session a month and books calls from it. She keeps her contacts in EasyCRM, sorts the People view by offer clicks after each session, and writes a personal note to each clicker."],[908,"A business coach running a monthly free session needs a fresh script each time without starting from scratch. The template keeps the structure the same, and the AI draft gives each month's topic its own sections and timing. The coach then edits the stories and examples into their own voice."],[5203,"A business coach who fills discovery calls from a monthly live talk keeps the live talk for his existing audience. The evergreen copy takes new visitors from his website every day."],[2767,"A business coach who works alone"],[3931,"A career coach invites past clients to a monthly live clinic on salary negotiation. She keeps one EasyCRM list for clients and another for newsletter readers, and invites each with a different opening line."],[910,"A career coach who runs a free session before opening a group programme needs the offer to land at the right moment. Setting the duration first means the AI's section timings leave room for the offer. The coach downloads the PDF and rehearses against those timings."],[4199,"A check from a phone"],[2815,"A checkout link suits a lower-commitment offer, such as a paid workshop or a short group programme. It catches buyers who already trust you. For a high-commitment programme, most people want a conversation first, so a direct sale often works better as a second offer."],[2871,"A click isn't a booking. Someone can click, look at your calendar and leave, so always compare clicks with the bookings your calendar shows. Replay views are counted separately from live attendance, and small sessions swing a lot. Judge the pitch over several runs, not one."],[1175,"A client is a lead who bought. The **Clients** table adds two columns that leads don't have, **Deals** and **Revenue**. Sorting by revenue tells you who to look after. **Add Client** creates one directly, for a customer who came from somewhere other than a webinar."],[2168,"A client webinar funnel has the same parts every time. Getting them in place before you write a single slide saves the rework that usually happens in launch week."],[5193,"A clone is a new webinar, not a continuation of the old one, so a few things stay behind."],[2560,"A co-host behind the scenes"],[8876,"A coach adds the **Phone** field so the final reminder can go by text (Beta), and turns on **GDPR Compliance** with a mandatory checkbox agreeing to follow-up emails."],[6725,"A coach holds free weekly office hours with **No end date**. Registrants see only the next session unless the coach changes **Schedule Preferences**."],[283,"A coach launching a new programme generates the whole webinar in one sitting, downloads the script as a PDF to rehearse from, and picks **Soft Pitch** because the audience is new to them."],[7641,"A coach or consultant runs live sessions because clients buy access to their judgement. A live workshop lets prospects hear real questions answered, and an offer card can open a booking or application page at the end."],[9477,"A coach or educator runs a live, ticketed workshop. The registration page sells a general admission ticket, the **Live + Replay bundle** sells the recording to people who can't attend live, and the confirmation page gives each buyer their join link and calendar buttons."],[6908,"A coach records a signature talk once and broadcasts it on a regular schedule, joining the chat when they can. Polls and offers work as they would live, so the session still ends with a next step."],[5888,"A coach running a live masterclass leaves chat on **Everyone** so attendees can introduce themselves and react, and uses **Private Q&A** to collect questions for the end. A moderator joins on the moderator link to keep up with the chat."],[689,"A coach running a monthly session that ends on an offer can put the programme and its price in the prompt. The registration page then describes the offer, and the coach checks what's included before publishing. The offer itself is set up separately as an in-room offer."],[1637,"A coach running just-in-time sessions on an automated webinar gets new registrants every day. A nurture workflow gives each one the same short sequence over the next fortnight, whenever they joined."],[4664,"A coach running paid workshops checks the **Revenue** view after each session, compares **Paid Registrations** with offer sales under **Revenue by Source**, and keeps an eye on **Checkout abandoned**."],[8154,"A coach runs a free masterclass and closes the replay on a **Specific date** that matches the deadline on their offer. The **Watched replay** event in their CRM tags people who caught up, so the follow-up matches what each person saw."],[7452,"A coach runs a live masterclass alone. They blur the background in the green room, keep recording on, launch a poll about the audience's situation halfway through, and push a booking offer near the end. **End of Session** sends everyone to the booking page."],[9658,"A coach runs a weekly live session. Before each one they open the Overview, check the **Upcoming** tab for the right date, glance at **Registrants**, and use **Enter Room** to get into the green room early."],[7281,"A coach runs an interview-style masterclass with a guest expert who has no EasyWebinar account. The guest joins by link, waits backstage during the coach's opening, and comes on stage for the interview segment."],[4144,"A coach selling a programme uses the bridge and the offer sections most. Planning them as timed sections stops the pitch shrinking to a final slide, and the countdown line in the template decides whether the offer has a deadline."],[8447,"A coach sets the **Sender name** to their own name and the **Reply-to email** to the inbox they check every day. Registrants often reply to the confirmation with a question about the session, and that reply becomes the first real conversation with a prospect."],[6052,"A coach uses Create with AI with the goals \"understanding pain points\" and \"qualifying leads\", then edits the five generated polls into their own words. The polls run through the session and set up the call offer at the close."],[7838,"A coach who runs one strong live session can turn it into an evergreen version that plays on a schedule. The recording is the starting point, picked straight from the Library during setup."],[9005,"A coach's website has a \"Free masterclass\" page. The registration widget sits on that page, under the coach's own story, so the page they already designed does the persuading."],[2770,"A coaching team running the same pitch on repeat"],[2733,"A coaching webinar gives coaches and consultants a repeatable way to fill a programme: teach one useful idea, invite interested attendees to apply or book a call, then run the session live or on repeat and follow up by what each person did."],[2791,"A coaching webinar is the repeatable way to fill a coaching programme when referrals have stopped being enough. You teach one useful idea, invite the people it helped to apply or book a call, and follow up with everyone else by what they did. EasyWebinar runs that session live, then on a schedule or on repeat, with the booking or application button inside the room. The rest of this page is the full recipe, in the order you'd build it."],[2800,"A coaching webinar lets a prospect see how you think before they spend an hour with you. It turns a vague interest into a clear choice: book a call, apply, or wait for the next session. Once it works, you can run it again without starting from nothing."],[6892,"A common approach is for a teacher to give the taster live every time, which can mean twelve sittings a term. Teachers may end up repeating the same lesson and fielding the same questions. In a busy week, the lesson can come out rushed or uneven."],[6316,"A common approach is for the instructor to read out a checkout link near the end and paste it into chat. In practice, that link tends to scroll away within a minute, and people who joined late may never see it. The company may also struggle to tell whether weak sales came from the bundle or from the checkout."],[5997,"A common approach is to ask questions out loud and read the replies as they scroll past in the chat. A few confident attendees answer while most stay silent. Afterwards, the partners scroll back through the chat log, trying to remember who said what. The call list ends up built on guesses."],[7076,"A common approach is to keep presenting it live, or to upload the recording and create a new dated session each week. Presenting live costs an engineer's hour every Tuesday. The dated route relies on someone remembering the setup. Either way, a prospect who signs up on a Wednesday waits six days to see the product."],[5837,"A common approach is to leave a public chat open and hope people keep personal details out of it. In practice, attendees tend to hold back on money questions when strangers can read them. The chat fills with greetings while the real questions stay unasked, and interest may cool before anyone follows up."],[5521,"A common approach is to leave the chat on and hope viewers start talking. In practice, people who arrive alone tend to stay quiet, so each one sees an empty panel. Questions about reading pace and meeting times may go unasked, and some readers leave unsure whether the club suits them."],[4614,"A common approach is to rebuild the funnel from exports. Sign-ups come from the form tool, attendance from the meeting tool and bookings from the sales calendar, and someone lines them up in a spreadsheet. That can take days each month. Names may not match across three lists, and a person who watched live and then on the recording can be counted twice."],[6531,"A common approach is to rebuild the webinar every month, or to keep reusing one webinar and clear it out. Rebuilding can eat an evening of copying pages, emails and the offer by hand. Reusing one webinar tends to mix last month's sign-ups into this month's numbers."],[5683,"A common approach is to record the deadline into the pitch, or to add a third-party timer to the sales page the offer links to. A recorded line such as \"the bonus ends on Friday\" is true for one week and wrong for every viewer after it. A timer set to one date can't follow sessions that start at different times. The coach ends up correcting timers, and some viewers are shown a deadline that has already passed."],[8414,"A common approach is to rely on a default email sent from a generic address. It may land in a promotions folder, from a name the buyer doesn't know. Replies may go to an inbox nobody reads. The broker may not even see who signed up until the day."],[8700,"A common approach is to run the explainer at set times, two or three a week. HR managers tend to read newsletters between interviews and payroll deadlines, though. A Thursday afternoon slot may not fit, and some may decide the topic can wait. For them, the newsletter click is probably wasted."],[8553,"A common approach is to schedule reminder emails by hand for each session date. That may work for one session a month, but it gets hard with several a week. Gaps creep in, and some people may hear nothing between signing up and the start."],[9431,"A common approach is to sell tickets on a separate payment page, then add each buyer to the webinar's attendee list manually. The day before, someone checks the payment list against the sign-up list. A buyer missed in the copying never gets a join link, and the consultant finds out only when that founder emails at the start time."],[7237,"A common approach is to send every speaker the same link and start at the advertised time. In practice, the first minutes tend to go on sound checks and guests asking whether they can be heard. Those minutes also open the recording that clients watch later."],[6712,"A common approach is to set up a separate event for every evening, which can mean sixteen events per course. Each one may need its own sign-up link, so trainees often juggle a list of links. When a holiday moves a class, someone usually has to rebuild that night by hand."],[90,"A common approach is to share one login and keep every client's webinars in the same account. That tends to hold until two people need the room at once, or someone edits the wrong firm's webinar. Billing sits one click away from everyone, and nobody can say who changed what. The cost is usually small at first, then grows with every new client."],[6158,"A common approach is to take questions in the main chat and read a few out at the end. In a busy room, those questions sit between greetings and reactions, so the presenter scrolls back and still misses some. Questions about one customer's account are also on show to every other customer. Admins whose questions were missed often raise a support ticket the next day."],[4799,"A common path is to run the session live a few times to sharpen it, then clone it as Evergreen. Create and schedule a live webinar covers the live side, and EasyWebinar's post on live vs automated vs evergreen webinars goes further into the choice."],[4387,"A common pattern is three: a day before, an hour before and 15 minutes before. Each reminder's timing is set relative to the start, so it still works if the date moves."],[8972,"A common route is a \"Register here\" button on the treatment page that sends visitors to a separate webinar page. That works. But each click away is a chance to lose a reader, and the page that persuaded them gets left behind. The cost probably shows up as fewer signups than the page's traffic suggests."],[8830,"A common route is to collect the extra details in a separate form tool, then match those answers to the webinar's registrant list by hand. That can work for a small list. But the matching probably eats an afternoon each quarter, and consent records can end up split across two systems."],[9111,"A common route is to design the page in a separate landing page tool, then connect its form to the webinar platform. Each month, someone retypes the new date on the page. That can work. But each handoff is a place where a form breaks or an old date slips out. The cost likely shows up only as fewer signups."],[2999,"A common route is to stitch the launch together from a meeting app, an email tool and a video host. For an automated version, the recording moves to the video host, and the sign-up page, reminders and offer are rebuilt around it. The live and automated versions then drift apart, and each keeps its own list of sign-ups."],[3601,"A common route is to write one email, paste in the replay link and send it to the whole list. That's quick, but it tends to read wrong for someone. People who watched get a recording they may not need, and people who missed it get thanked for coming. Splitting the list by hand can mean exporting names from one tool and pasting them into another."],[4969,"A common setup is one fixed showing each evening, so the team can promote a single time. That probably suits people who plan ahead. Shoppers who click an ad at lunchtime, though, may face a wait of several hours. Many are likely to forget, and the ad spend that brought them is partly wasted."],[9648,"A common way to manage this is a shared spreadsheet and a long email thread. The date sits in one place, the speaker's link in another and sign-up numbers in a third. It tends to work until a busy month. Then a guest may get the wrong link, or a called-off date may still be promoted."],[208,"A common workaround is to build each piece in a different tool: slides in one, a landing page in another, emails from a template. That can take several evenings. The pieces may drift apart as they're written, and a first webinar can slip while the setup drags on."],[1821,"A common workaround is to download the attendee list after each session, clean it up and import it into HubSpot. That tends to take a day or two, and the file usually holds only names and whether people attended. Reps then call everyone in the same order, and the warmest leads may cool while they wait."],[4463,"A common workaround is to download the full registrant list after each session and split it between agents alphabetically. Every name then gets the same call, in the same order. Warm prospects may wait behind people who never turned up, and nobody can say which channel produced the viewers."],[9284,"A common workaround is to send the guest a shared login, or a link that drops them straight in front of the audience. That can mean passwords passed around and a guest testing their microphone in public. Long registration URLs in show notes are also awkward to read aloud or retype."],[8107,"A common workaround is to upload the recording to a video site and email the link the next morning. That probably reaches some of the people who missed the evening. Yet the offer and the polls tend to stay behind in the live room, so the recording teaches without selling. Viewers may also keep watching for months, long after enrolment has closed."],[8437,"A confirmation only goes out when someone registers, and your registration link doesn't work until the registration page is published. Saving the page alone isn't enough. Open the **Registration Page** from the **Content** tab and click **Publish** before you promote the link. See Registration page builder."],[8147,"A connected CRM can act on replay behaviour with the **Watched replay** and **Didn't watch replay** events, and receive each contact's **Webinar Replay Link** or **Webinar Replay Short Link** in a custom field. See Connect a CRM and map your webinar data."],[6383,"A connected CRM can act on the **Clicked offer** event, which fires when someone clicks your offer. A sensible mapping is a `webinar-hot` tag plus a notification to sales."],[9307,"A connected CRM can receive each attendee's personal join link and replay link through the **Custom fields** tab. Mapping the join link is what lets you send reminders from your CRM instead of EasyWebinar, with each contact getting their own working link."],[4508,"A connected CRM can receive the same behaviour as tags: **Missed**, **Watched full**, **Clicked offer** and more. People complements that by letting you look back across webinars and export a segment on demand. See Connect a CRM and map your webinar data."],[4657,"A connected CRM can tag people by the same behaviour Analytics counts: **Registered**, **Attended**, **Missed**, **Watched replay**, **Watched full** and **Clicked offer**, among others. That lets your sales or email system act on what the numbers show. See Connect a CRM and map your webinar data."],[8443,"A connected CRM or email tool can act on the same moment through the **Registered** event, for example tagging every new registrant. On the **Custom fields** side, the **Webinar Join Link** can be mapped into a CRM field so each contact holds their own working link. See Connect a CRM and map your webinar data."],[9473,"A connected CRM or email tool can tag paid registrants as they sign up (the **Registered** event), and tag what they did in the session, from **Attended** and **Missed** to **Watched full** and **Clicked offer**. Map the **Webinar Join Link** into a CRM field if you want to send reminders from your own system. See Connect a CRM and map your webinar data."],[8860,"A connected CRM or email tool has a **Custom fields** tab that sends webinar details into your CRM's own fields. The data it lists is:"],[6724,"A consultant charges for a live masterclass by choosing **Paid** on the pricing step. Buyers complete a Stripe checkout before their registration goes through."],[8730,"A consultant puts an explainer webinar on demand for prospects to watch before a first call, so the call can start from where the webinar left off."],[1191,"A consultant who fills a calendar from a monthly session can keep each booked call as a deal. The **Clients** table then shows what each client has been worth."],[909,"A consultant who sells advisory work uses a webinar to show how they think. The script needs strong teaching points and a gentle close, so they write the prompt for a soft, education-first session. They check every line about scope and fees before the pages go live."],[2768,"A consultant with an application"],[4954,"A conventional webinar session sits at a time you chose in advance. A just-in-time session is generated around the person registering: EasyWebinar offers them a session that begins shortly, on a cycle set by the **Interval**."],[6510,"A copy that keeps the original's type is ready to edit straight away. A copy that moves from live to simulive or evergreen needs one more thing: a recording to play."],[5731,"A countdown persuades because people trust it. Keep that trust:"],[5672,"A countdown timer is a visible clock that shows the time remaining before something happens. In EasyWebinar it does one of two jobs:"],[5676,"A countdown turns a deadline you would otherwise keep repeating (\"the bonus closes when the session ends\") into something every attendee can see. On a registration page, it makes the session feel like an event with a start time, not a page to come back to later."],[8874,"A course creator adds a custom field asking for the student's current level, so the pitch at the end can speak to beginners and advanced students differently. **UTM Params** shows which posts and emails filled the session."],[7642,"A course creator often opens enrolment with a live session, because launch week needs a shared moment. EasyWebinar is the conversion layer in front of their course platform, and the recording can run as an evergreen webinar afterwards."],[5202,"A course creator runs a live masterclass that sells her course, then clones it as evergreen so new followers can watch it any day. Her course sales funnel keeps working between launches."],[4360,"A course creator runs a live session before opening enrolment, alone. With no colleague to watch, they register a second email on their phone and use it as the attendee view."],[9661,"A course creator runs an evergreen webinar every day. The Overview shows the generated run of upcoming sessions, and the **Live** tab lets them enter a session while it is running."],[9315,"A course creator sends no-shows the replay link in a **Webinar Follow-up** after the session, and maps the join link into their email tool so reminders come from their own system."],[9170,"A course creator starts from an **Education** template and saves their styled version to **My Templates**, so every launch looks consistent. They add a field asking for the student's current level and turn on UTM capture to see which posts drive signups."],[8450,"A course creator uses **Create with AI** to draft the sequence, then rewrites the welcome email in their own voice and sets the sender to a name their students already know. See selling online courses with webinars."],[7837,"A course creator who opens enrolment with a monthly live workshop needs the replay by the next morning. Recording is on before she starts, and the session is ready in the Library to share. The Selling online courses with webinars guide covers the rest of that launch."],[2964,"A course launch, end to end"],[2543,"A course platform or membership that holds the link"],[8306,"A creator already has a teaching video on YouTube. They import it with **Import from URL**, enter its exact duration, and use no storage. Later they replace it with a recording of a live session through the **Webinar Video** panel. See selling online courses with webinars."],[9478,"A creator charges a small fee for a live training that previews a full course, then presents the course itself as an offer inside the session. Registration revenue and course sales appear separately under **Revenue by Source**. See selling online courses with webinars."],[282,"A creator describes a training webinar that teaches the core idea of a course, chooses the online course category and **Balanced Approach** for the offer, and edits the script before generating pages. See Course creators."],[4512,"A creator filters for high watch time plus **Offer clicked** after each evergreen session and exports the list for a personal follow-up, since those viewers were interested enough to click but didn't enrol. See Course creators."],[8729,"A creator offers a free training lesson on demand, so new subscribers can start it the moment they sign up. On a separate sales webinar, the creator leaves On Demand off so the session feels like an event people turn up for. This is one pattern in selling online courses with webinars."],[4145,"A creator opening enrolment plans the teaching points to lead into the course offer, then reuses the same outline for an evergreen version. The selling online courses with webinars page covers the rest of that launch."],[6051,"A creator opens with a poll about the audience's experience level, then asks about their biggest obstacle halfway through. Just before the enrolment offer, a poll about timing shows how many people are ready to start now."],[474,"A creator promoting a course with a live webinar uses the drafted reminders as they are, then adds an offer-click follow-up. EasyWebinar handles the webinar and the emails, while the course itself stays on their own course platform."],[4852,"A creator runs a free class live a few times to refine it, then clones it as evergreen to keep enrolment open between launches. EasyWebinar handles the session and the offer, while the course itself stays on the creator's own course platform."],[7997,"A creator runs a launch webinar live with recording on. Afterwards, the session sits in **Recordings**, and the creator clones the webinar as evergreen and picks that recording, so the version that sold well live keeps running. See selling online courses with webinars."],[6543,"A creator runs a teaching webinar live a few times with recording on, then clones it as **Evergreen**, selects the recording of the strongest session on the copy, and sets an evergreen schedule. The live original stays as it was. See Selling online courses with webinars."],[7089,"A creator runs a teaching webinar that ends with an enrolment offer, on a daily schedule with just-in-time sessions. The webinar sells the course while the course itself stays on the creator's own platform. See Selling online courses with webinars."],[7455,"A creator runs a training live a few times with recording on, then clones the strongest session as an evergreen webinar using the recording from the Library. The course creator's guide follows that route end to end."],[4663,"A creator selling a course through an evergreen webinar watches **Offers Performance** and **Clicks to Purchases**. A healthy click rate with few purchases sends them to the checkout page, not the presentation. See Course creators."],[1833,"A creator selling a course through webinars tags **Clicked offer** and keeps those contacts out of the general nurture sequence. That stops buyers who are close to purchase from getting beginner emails."],[6200,"A creator teaching a free workshop uses **Public Q&A**. Attendees see that someone has already asked about prerequisites, and the creator answers the most common questions in a block before the enrolment offer."],[5582,"A creator turns their best live session into an evergreen webinar and writes a manual script from the questions people really asked live, with `Admin` replies that match the answers they gave. Every session plays the same tested conversation."],[5404,"A creator who opens enrolment with a free taster session can record the strongest version once. Simulive then plays it at set times through the enrolment window, with the creator in the chat for the busiest sessions. The guide to selling courses with webinars covers the wider launch."],[1638,"A creator who opens enrolment with a webinar can follow up on the offer-click list separately from everyone else. The course-selling use case shows how the webinar and the offer fit together."],[1044,"A creator who sells a beginner photography course wants a webinar that runs without him. He sets the type to Evergreen, generates the drafts, picks his recorded video and adds a simulated chat (Beta) so the room doesn't look empty."],[1045,"A creator who sells a home workout course gets asked what equipment is needed in every session. On Pro, she uploads a PDF of her course details so the AI can answer those questions while she presents."],[1043,"A creator who sells a self-paced Italian course adds an intermediate level. She describes the new level, its audience and the price, and gets a live webinar draft with pages and emails. Her review time goes into the script and the offer copy."],[8592,"A creator's evergreen webinar runs daily. Because reminder timing is relative to the webinar, the same three emails serve the whole schedule. The creator adds a Beta **Reminder SMS** as the final nudge for an audience that reads texts faster than email. See selling online courses with webinars."],[5013,"A creator's teaching webinar runs on just-in-time alongside a daily recurring time. New visitors from ads take the next just-in-time session; people who'd rather plan ahead book the daily slot. This pattern is part of selling online courses with webinars."],[7280,"A customer success manager presents a customer training session while a colleague joins with the **Moderator Join Link for Live room** to handle questions. The moderator never needs to appear on stage, so they stay off it for the whole session."],[104,"A customer success team adds a colleague who handles questions during onboarding sessions. On a one-off session, the **Moderator Join Link for Live room** does the same job without a login."],[4358,"A customer success team at a healthcare software company runs a monthly release briefing, hosted by whichever manager owns the release. The checklist is shared, so a first-time host follows the same steps as the regular one. Co-hosts get their own join link a week before."],[8000,"A customer success team keeps its product FAQ and onboarding guides on the **Files** tab as the AI Moderator's knowledge base (Beta), so questions on an unattended evergreen onboarding webinar can be answered from the team's own documents."],[8153,"A customer success team records an onboarding session and leaves the replay on **Always Available**, so new customers can watch it whenever they join. The **Replay Page Link** goes into the team's welcome material."],[7840,"A customer success team running live training wants each session kept with the webinar it came from. Each recording carries its webinar's name and date, so the team can find last quarter's session quickly."],[9314,"A customer success team runs a training webinar for existing customers. It emails the **One-Click Registration Link** to the customer list, then sends the **Attendee Replay link** in a follow-up to everyone who couldn't attend live."],[8727,"A customer success team runs its product onboarding as an evergreen webinar with On Demand on. New customers register and start the walkthrough immediately, and the button reads *Start onboarding* instead of the default."],[3268,"A customer success team runs onboarding sessions every week with a rota of presenters. Each presenter has a User login, and a moderator handles live chat and questions."],[8593,"A customer success team runs onboarding webinars for new customers. Their reminders restate what to have ready before the session, and the fifteen-minute reminder carries the link and nothing else."],[4513,"A customer success team runs recurring onboarding webinars and filters **Behavior** for no-shows on the latest session, then sends that group the replay so new customers don't fall behind."],[5015,"A customer success team sends new customers to an onboarding webinar with just-in-time sessions, so each customer starts onboarding in their own timezone shortly after signing up."],[6546,"A customer success team that presents onboarding live clones the webinar as **Simulive** and chooses the live recording, so the session can run on a schedule while the team covers the chat."],[4853,"A customer success team turns a recurring \"getting started\" session into a simulive webinar at set weekly times. The recording keeps the content consistent, and a team member stays in the chat to answer account questions."],[6907,"A customer success team turns its onboarding talk into a recurring simulive session. New customers register for a date, and a team member covers the chat to pick up account-specific questions."],[7091,"A customer success team turns its onboarding walkthrough into an evergreen webinar with On Demand switched on, so new customers can watch the moment they sign up instead of waiting for a scheduled session."],[5004,"A daily or weekly **Recurring Schedule** can run alongside just-in-time, for registrants who prefer to book a set time. Those sessions use the webinar's timezone."],[8721,"A daily or weekly schedule can run alongside On Demand for registrants who want to book a time. Recurring sessions run in the webinar's timezone."],[8122,"A deadline creates urgency, which is why most offers sit behind one. If your offers or follow-up emails mention when the replay closes, make sure they match the option you chose here."],[1176,"A deal is a potential sale, and a pipeline is the set of stages it passes through to won or lost. Deals won't work until a pipeline exists, so create one first from the **Pipelines** tab. A webinar-led business often runs stages like New, Contacted, Demo booked, Proposal, then Won or Lost."],[3469,"A demand generation lead tags every promotion link, then reports registrants, attendees and offer clicks per campaign after each webinar. The integration sends attendees and no-shows to the CRM with separate tags, so sales starts with the people who clicked."],[4662,"A demand generation team runs a monthly webinar and tracks **Visitors** to **Registered** after every promotion change. When the rate drops, they test a new registration page headline before changing anything else."],[6183,"A few habits that keep the queue under control:"],[1381,"A few things save time once you start. If you're verifying a domain, you need access to its DNS settings, or a colleague who has it. That colleague can work from their own team member login. If you're connecting a provider, have your account credentials to hand. You also need a reply address that a person actually reads."],[2400,"A first webinar has a small audience, so a few people change the percentages a lot. Compare one step with the next rather than with someone else's results. Replay views are counted separately from live attendance, so check **Total Attended** before you judge the session. If everything shows zero, widen the date range."],[981,"A fitness course creator fielding the same questions"],[536,"A form embedded on your own site"],[2544,"A free session that sells in the room"],[2034,"A full build takes about ten minutes."],[4197,"A full test run with a colleague as attendee"],[3009,"A good prompt says who the webinar is for, the three or four things you'll teach, how long it runs, the tone you want, and what you're selling at the end. A vague prompt produces a generic webinar."],[2099,"A home for the client's data"],[979,"A language course creator launching a new level"],[1218,"A lead hasn't bought yet. A client has, and carries deals and revenue in the **Clients** table."],[1167,"A lead is anyone who has come into your orbit but hasn't bought. The **Leads** table shows each contact's company, email, phone, tags, status and the date they arrived. There are three ways to add leads:"],[9294,"A link for an existing list. It carries the person's name and email in the URL, so a known contact registers without filling in the form. Because it depends on details you already hold, it only works for contacts you already have. Use it in emails to your own list, where you know who each recipient is."],[6389,"A live masterclass ends with a free offer whose button opens a booking page for a call. **Attendee Action Follow-ups** reach people who clicked but didn't book."],[5728,"A live masterclass ends with an offer to book a call. The countdown shows how long the booking link stays open after the session, and the registration-page countdown builds attendance in the days before."],[6199,"A live product demo runs **Private Q&A**, so prospects can ask about pricing or their own setup without the room seeing. A solutions engineer joins as moderator and works the **Active** list while the presenter demos."],[5145,"A live session often mentions today's date, a deadline this week or a bonus for people \"on the call right now\". In an evergreen webinar those lines sound wrong to a viewer on another day. Note where they fall so you can decide whether the recording still works."],[8302,"A live session recorded with **Auto Recording** on lands in **Library → Recordings**, already hosted on EasyWebinar. To run it as an automated webinar, clone the live webinar, choose **Evergreen** or **Simulive** for the copy, and pick the recording. See Clone a webinar and Run a live webinar."],[7577,"A live webinar catches everything that depends on the moment: a question you didn't expect, a comment you can answer by name, a poll result you can react to. What it misses is reach across timezones and repetition. Everyone has to turn up at one fixed time, and you have to present every session yourself."],[7633,"A live webinar connects to the rest of the platform in a few places:"],[5508,"A live webinar has a shared room: everyone is there at the same time, so the chat fills itself. An evergreen webinar doesn't. Each registrant picks their own session, attendees arrive at different times, and a genuinely shared chat isn't possible. Without anything in it, the chat panel looks abandoned."],[7666,"A live webinar is a session presented in real time at a set date and time. Attendees join at that moment, and chat, questions and polls happen as the presenter speaks."],[7492,"A live webinar is a session you present in real time at a set date and time. This guide explains what happens in a live webinar room, from the green room to recording, and when a recorded format suits you better."],[7548,"A live webinar is a session you present in real time, at a date and time you set, while your audience chats, asks questions, answers polls and sees your offers as they happen. The hard part is rarely the presenting. It's knowing what the room can do before you commit to live rather than a recording. EasyWebinar is a webinar platform that runs live, simulive and evergreen webinars in one product, and every plan includes live webinars that attendees join in the browser. This page walks through a live session from scheduling to the replay, and shows when a recorded format would serve you better."],[6703,"A live webinar is a session you present in real time. Attendees join at the scheduled moment, and chat and questions happen as they happen. Live is one of three webinar types; simulive and evergreen play a recording instead. The schedule you set here decides when registrants can attend, and the registration page is where they sign up for it."],[7564,"A live webinar is for sessions where your presence is the point. People come to hear you answer their question, react to their comment or make an offer while they're paying attention. Live is the format that gives them that."],[4875,"A live webinar is presented in real time at one fixed date and time. An automated webinar plays a recording instead, either at set times (simulive) or on a repeating schedule each registrant chooses from (evergreen)."],[6673,"A live webinar runs at one fixed time for everyone, in the timezone you set. Your registration page shows that time with its timezone. Choose the timezone you will broadcast in."],[6704,"A live webinar suits sessions where your presence is the point: a launch, a workshop or a class with real questions. Creating it fixes the date, time and timezone that people sign up for."],[3918,"A low open rate is usually a subject line or sender problem. A good open rate with a low click rate means people opened the email and the offer didn't land."],[1399,"A low open rate usually points to the subject line or the sender, so recheck your sending identity along with your wording. A good open rate with a low click rate means the email was opened and the offer didn't land, which isn't a setup problem."],[1781,"A mapping is three choices: **When** (the event) → **Then** (the action) → **Value** (the tag). Pick each one and click **Add mapping**."],[8594,"A marketing team already runs its nurture emails from its own system, so it maps the **Webinar Join Link** into a CRM field and sends one of the reminders from there, keeping EasyWebinar's reminders for the final hour."],[6544,"A marketing team clones last quarter's webinar for a new topic. The registration page layout, reminder emails and integration settings come across, and the team rewrites the content rather than rebuilding the structure."],[102,"A marketing team gives **Admin** to the person who owns the plan and settings. Colleagues who build registration pages and follow up on registrants work as **User**."],[3933,"A marketing team keeps HubSpot as its CRM and sends invitations from there. It pastes the EasyWebinar registration link into the campaign and maps webinar events to CRM tags so sales sees who registered."],[1832,"A marketing team maps **Missed** and **Didn't watch replay** to tags that start a replay sequence. People who still haven't watched get a second nudge from the email tool the team already uses."],[6723,"A marketing team presents a quarterly product briefing at one fixed time. It offers a second sitting with **Add Session** for prospects in other regions."],[9313,"A marketing team promotes the **Registration Page Link** in ads and social posts with **Shorten URL's** on. For an email to existing customers, it uses the **One-Click Registration Link**, so customers register without retyping details the team already holds."],[6905,"A marketing team records its strongest product presentation and runs it as a weekly simulive session. A product specialist joins the chat for each sitting and answers questions while the recording plays, so prospects get a consistent pitch and real answers."],[3267,"A marketing team running a monthly demo and a quarterly panel keeps one master webinar for each and clones it every time. The owner's name sits on the calendar, and People shows registrants from both in one list. The marketing teams overview covers the wider fit."],[6054,"A marketing team running a recurring educational webinar ends with a feedback poll on delivery, so each run tells the presenter whether the teaching is landing before the next session is planned."],[8448,"A marketing team running a webinar for pipeline turns on **Notify admin** and points it at a team address, so sales can see registrations as they arrive. In the connected CRM, the **Registered** event tags each new contact for the campaign."],[4511,"A marketing team running paid campaigns turns on **UTM Params** for every webinar, then filters People by **Source & UTM** each month to compare which channels produce attendees with high **Watch percentage**, not just sign-ups."],[7644,"A marketing team runs a live panel with guest speakers and streams it out with EasyCast for wider reach. The analytics then show who came live, who watched the replay and who missed it."],[8152,"A marketing team runs a live panel, then uses **Custom Build** to give the replay its own layout and a call to action aimed at people who weren't in the room. **Replay Watched** in Analytics shows how much of the audience came through the replay."],[4359,"A marketing team runs a live product webinar with an outside guest speaker. The guest has never used the platform, so the two-days-before test run is when they meet the device check and the green room for the first time."],[7454,"A marketing team runs a panel webinar with branded frame overlays and streams it to the company's LinkedIn page through EasyCast. Registered attendees watch in the webinar room, and the overlay carries the brand into the recording too."],[7279,"A marketing team runs a panel with two guest speakers from partner companies. Each guest gets the **Co-presenter Join Link**, joins fifteen minutes early, and checks their audio backstage. The host introduces the topic alone, then moves each guest onto the stage in turn."],[9660,"A marketing team runs a webinar for a product launch. The Overview gives them the **Registration Page Link** to promote, and the gap between **Registrants** and **Live Attended** tells them, after the session, whether reminders did their job."],[476,"A marketing team runs an evergreen product demo and relies on the relative reminder timing. Because each reminder counts back from the session, the same sequence serves every scheduled time."],[8875,"A marketing team runs paid ads to a webinar. It turns on **UTM Params**, tags each ad, and filters **People** by **Source & UTM** after the session to compare campaigns by who actually attended. The phone number is mapped into the CRM so sales has it."],[1429,"A marketing team sending regularly gets the most from **Domain Configuration**, because volume is where a verified domain pays back. If the company already runs SES, SendGrid or Mailgun, connect that account with **Add Service** and agree who looks after the credentials."],[9003,"A marketing team writes a blog post on the webinar's topic and embeds the registration form at the end, so readers who reach the bottom can sign up on the spot. The **Registration Page Link** goes into the newsletter and paid social."],[3133,"A master webinar to copy"],[6201,"A monthly onboarding webinar runs as a simulive session. Q&A stays private, and the team answers setup questions in real time while the recording plays, then checks the **Not Answered Rate** afterwards."],[3132,"A named owner per webinar"],[9689,"A new webinar shows zeros across the board until registrations start."],[1869,"A one-page overview of the EasyWebinar webinar platform: the three webinar types, the live room, selling, AI, follow-up, who it suits and its limits."],[5407,"A one-person business can keep a regular session running during busy weeks or holidays. The recording plays on time, and questions left in the contact box arrive by email."],[2657,"A paid audience expects to be heard. Set the room up on the **Engagement** and **CTAs** tabs before the session, not during it."],[9405,"A paid webinar asks people to pay before they are registered. In EasyWebinar you choose **Paid** when you create the webinar, set a price, and attendees pay through a Stripe checkout on their way to registering. The money goes straight to your own Stripe account, and every payment appears in the webinar's Overview and Analytics. Paid registration launched in **March 2026**."],[8141,"A paid webinar can sell a **Live + Replay bundle**, a second option that includes the replay recording with its own price. If you sell replay access, set the replay's **Availability** to match what the bundle promises. See Paid webinars."],[9470,"A paid webinar can still sell. Add offers on the **CTAs** tab, with an optional price and countdown timer, and offer sales are reported under **Paid Offer**, separately from registration revenue. Buyers of an offer receive the **Paid Offer — Transaction Success** email."],[2593,"A paid webinar turns your live online workshop or seminar into something people buy before they get the join link. To run one, you need four things: a price on the registration page, a way to take payment, a room built for teaching, and a clear rule about the replay. EasyWebinar takes payment through your own Stripe account at the moment of registration. It runs the session with chat, Q&A and polls, and lets you sell the replay as its own option."],[2602,"A paid workshop or seminar sells your time and attention live, to a group, for a set price. People pay at registration, so everyone in the room has already said yes to you."],[6202,"A panel discussion is tightly scripted, so Q&A is set to **Disable** and chat to **Hosts only**. Questions still reach the team privately, without interrupting the panel."],[980,"A photography course creator going evergreen"],[6413,"A poll asks a question. An offer sells something, with a headline, a button and an optional price."],[5984,"A poll has three parts:"],[5973,"A poll in EasyWebinar is a question with a set of answers that you put in front of your audience during a webinar. You build polls on the webinar's **CTAs** tab, next to your offers, and show them in the room when you choose. During a live session, each poll goes out when you launch it from the room's **Polls** panel. Create with AI can also write a set of polls for you, built around what you want the polls to achieve."],[5989,"A poll puts one question to the whole room, with a fixed set of answers. Everyone replies in the same format, so the results are easy to read at a glance. It also gives attendees something to do besides watch."],[911,"A practice with several coaches wants each presenter's session to follow the same shape. Each coach starts from the same template, generates a draft for their own topic, and regenerates until it sounds like them. A cloned webinar can then carry the settings from one session to the next."],[7246,"A presenter or moderator joining by link doesn't need an EasyWebinar account. Treat both links as private: anyone holding one can join your room in that role. The registration, attendee and replay links are the ones meant for your audience."],[2898,"A pricing consultant takes on four retainer clients a quarter. His webinar ends with an offer card that opens his application form, and **End of Session** points to the same form. His CRM stays where it is, and the integration tags each applicant by what they did."],[6053,"A product demo asks attendees which problem brought them, then shows the part of the product that solves it. After the session, the team filters **People** by webinar and looks at who answered polls and clicked the trial offer."],[7453,"A product manager presents a release walkthrough or product demo while a colleague moderates. The colleague waits backstage, handles the **Question** queue during the demo, and comes on stage for the last ten minutes to answer the top questions. **Share** carries the product demo, and the layout favours the screen."],[5012,"A product team links its \"watch the demo\" button to an evergreen registration page with just-in-time on a short interval. A visitor registers and is watching the recorded demo within minutes, in their own local time."],[8728,"A product team offers a recorded feature walkthrough with On Demand alongside just-in-time. Evaluators in a hurry watch straight away; others join a session a few minutes later."],[7090,"A product team puts a recorded demo behind its \"see it in action\" link. With just-in-time on, a visitor who registers can be watching within minutes, while the details are still fresh in their mind."],[8305,"A product team records a demo for an evergreen webinar and uploads it with **Local import**. The demo isn't published anywhere else, and hosting it on EasyWebinar keeps it private to the webinar."],[5204,"A product team records a well-received live demo, clones it as evergreen and offers just-in-time sessions to trial users. The team presents live only when the product changes."],[4851,"A product team records its best onboarding or demo session and runs it as evergreen, so a new trial user can watch within minutes of signing up. The team then keeps a live slot only for launches and release briefings."],[7282,"A product team rehearses a product launch demo in the green room: they test the screen share of the product, pick a layout that gives the demo most of the stage, and switch on a LinkedIn Organisation destination in EasyCast before going live."],[7839,"A product team running a live release walkthrough wants a copy for customers in other time zones. They check the header in the green room, go live, and point latecomers to the replay. On Demand covers letting people watch straight after registering."],[691,"A product team running an evergreen demo sets **Type** to Evergreen before generating, then chooses the video afterwards. Tracking code on the thank-you page tells their ad platform which clicks became sign-ups. The rest of the set-up is in creating an evergreen webinar."],[4665,"A product team running onboarding webinars cares most about **Total Attended** and **Replay only**, because many users catch up on the recording. A high **Not Answered Rate** tells them to go back through the questions and cover the gaps in their follow-up."],[7643,"A product team runs a live session for a release or a demo where customers ask hard questions. Co-hosts from support or engineering wait backstage until a question needs them."],[9004,"A product team runs a monthly product webinar and keeps the widget in the sidebar of its help centre or product blog, so customers looking for help can register for the next session without leaving."],[9172,"A product team runs a recurring demo webinar. It lets **Create with AI** draft the registration page from the demo script, edits the headline and bio, and checks the page on mobile before publishing."],[3270,"A product team runs a release webinar each month, then clones it as evergreen for people who missed it. The demand generation guide covers how those registrants become leads."],[8591,"A product team runs a weekly live demo. The three-reminder pattern goes out before every session without anyone touching it, and the team watches **Registered** to attended week by week to see whether a subject-line change made a difference."],[5584,"A product team runs an on-demand demo. A manual script places a short exchange about integrations at the point in the video where integrations come up, so the answer appears just as viewers are wondering."],[6906,"A product team serving several regions schedules the same recorded walkthrough at times that suit each one. Each session runs in the webinar's timezone, so the team sets times for each region accordingly."],[285,"A product team sets **Type** to **Evergreen** for an always-on product walkthrough, chooses poll goals for qualifying leads and segmenting for follow-up, then attaches their recorded demo as the video."],[3470,"A product team turns its standard demo into an evergreen webinar, so prospects can register any day of the week. Tags on the email and in-app links show which route brings people who watch the whole demo."],[7998,"A product team uploads its demo with **Local import** rather than publishing it on YouTube, so the video stays private to the webinar. The **EasyWebinar** badge confirms where it is hosted, and the storage meter shows the space it takes."],[5405,"A product team with a weekly feature walk-through can stop presenting the same demo each week. A product specialist joins the chat to answer setup questions while the recording runs the tour."],[639,"A prompt along these lines gives the pages plenty to work with:"],[1666,"A published workflow keeps sending on its own, so it's usually the cause. Check the Wallet's Recent activity log, which splits sends by channel and shows when each went out."],[2853,"A qualifying question at registration helps you prepare, but every extra field is personal data you have to look after. UK data protection law says personal information must be used for specified, explicit purposes. So ask one or two questions you'll actually use, and say why you're asking."],[2558,"A question queue you work through"],[3392,"A question such as \"How did you hear about us?\" is simply a custom field on the form. It catches sources that no link can tag, like a colleague forwarding the invitation or a mention at an event. It misses precision, because the answer depends on memory and on the options you offer."],[3529,"A quick first message"],[4539,"A rating of how engaged someone was, based on attendance, watch time and interaction."],[2687,"A real-estate investing trainer charges a small fee for a live seminar, then shows an offer for the full coaching programme. Ticket sales and in-room offer sales appear apart in **Revenue by Source**."],[3789,"A reason to register now"],[6390,"A recorded demo carries an offer for a trial, with the button pointing at the sign-up page. The team watches **Views to Clicks** across sessions to judge whether the demo builds enough interest."],[5730,"A recorded product demo ends with an offer for a trial or a call with sales. A countdown is optional here; many teams use it only during launch periods."],[2867,"A recorded session can still feel like a shared room. On a simulive webinar, everyone in a session watches together, and you can answer in the chat while the recording plays. On evergreen, you can turn on Chat Simulation (Beta) on the **Engagement** tab if you want the room to feel populated."],[9479,"A recorded training is sold on an evergreen schedule, so people pay and watch at a session time that suits them. The same pricing step applies, and every purchase lands in **Analytics → Revenue**."],[8289,"A recorded webinar has a **Webinar Video** panel on its **Content** tab that live webinars don't. It shows the video you chose and its length."],[4997,"A recorded webinar has no live room for you to enter, so you check it by joining a running session. The quickest way to get one is to register yourself."],[5390,"A recorded webinar has no live room for you to rehearse in, so you check it by joining a running session:"],[9632,"A recorded webinar has no live room to enter. On an evergreen webinar, wait for a session to be running: the **Sessions** panel's **Live** tab shows it, marked *Live now*, with **Enter room**."],[9691,"A recorded webinar has no live room to enter. When a session is running, it appears on the **Live** tab of the **Sessions** panel with **Enter room**."],[7777,"A recorder on your computer or phone captures whatever is on your screen and the audio you choose. It works for any session you can see, but it records only your view. It depends on you starting it, choosing the right audio and keeping the device awake. The file stays on that device until you move it."],[7789,"A recording captures your presenters and anything they share. In the UK, data protection legislation controls how organisations use personal information, so say at the start that the session is recorded. Tell guest presenters before they join the stage."],[5358,"A recording is fixed, so the fix is also fixed. Re-record the weak section, then swap the file with **Change Video** without touching the schedule. Read the figures for your webinar in webinar analytics."],[7051,"A recording of a webinar you have already run sits under **Recordings**. Choosing it turns that session into the content of the evergreen webinar."],[1925,"A recording plays at scheduled times, and everyone watching a session sees the same moment together. The chat still works as a shared room, and you can be in it answering questions. A simulive webinar suits a presentation you give over and over."],[5329,"A recording plays at the times you set, and everyone in a session watches the same moment. The chat stays a shared room, and you can be in it. This catches the group feeling and the chance to answer real questions. It misses the person who can't make any of your set times, unless you add more sessions or a daily or weekly schedule."],[7142,"A recording plays at times you set, like a scheduled broadcast. Everyone in a session sees the same moment, so the chat still works as a shared room, and you can answer questions live while the recording plays."],[1926,"A recording runs on a repeating schedule rather than a fixed date. An evergreen (automated) webinar can offer just-in-time sessions that start shortly after each person registers, in their local time. It can also offer instant viewing on demand and recurring sessions."],[7143,"A recording runs on a repeating schedule, and each registrant picks a time that suits them. Use it when the webinar is part of a funnel that should work whether or not you are available."],[8411,"A registrant without a confirmation has only what they saw on the thank-you page. If they closed it, they may have nothing to come back to. The first email they see may then arrive days later, from a name they don't connect with signing up."],[8542,"A reminder is an email in the **Webinar Reminders** group on the **Notifications** tab. It goes to people who registered, before the webinar starts, and its purpose is to turn a registration into an attendance."],[8099,"A replay gives everyone who registered a second chance at the session. It serves people who couldn't make the live time, people who left early and people who want to watch again. For a webinar that ends in an offer, it also extends the window to act on the offer."],[8751,"A replay is the recording of a past session, on the **Replay** tab. Instant replay, or On Demand, lets someone watch an evergreen webinar straight away without waiting for a session."],[8180,"A replay is the recording of a past session. Instant replay, or **On Demand**, is an evergreen option that lets someone watch straight away without waiting for a session."],[8081,"A replay page is where people watch the recording of a webinar session after it has happened. In EasyWebinar every webinar has a **Replay** tab that turns the page on, gives you its link, sets how long the replay stays available, decides where viewers go when the recording finishes, and chooses whether the page copies the live webinar or is designed separately. Most people who register don't make the live session, so the replay is often how the webinar reaches the rest of its audience."],[5886,"A replay page set to **Webinar Replica** shows the same video, offers, polls and chat replay as the session."],[1831,"A RevOps team maps **Clicked offer** and **Watched full** to separate tags, so lead routing in the CRM can treat them as different signals. The **Webinar Title** field records which webinar each contact came from."],[2953,"A room that doesn't feel empty"],[103,"A sales team running demos gives a login to each sales engineer who presents every week. A partner presenting once joins with the Co-presenter link instead."],[2326,"A sales webinar for a small business is a free online session that teaches one useful thing and ends with one clear offer. If you've never run one, the hard part isn't presenting. It's getting the format, the registration page, the offer and the follow-up to work together while you do everything alone. Run your first one live, show your offer inside the room with a countdown, then email attendees and no-shows separately afterwards. EasyWebinar holds all four steps in one webinar, so you build them once and reuse them."],[2370,"A sales webinar is usually free, but you can charge for a paid workshop instead. Attendees pay through a secure Stripe checkout, and the money goes straight to your Stripe account. EasyWebinar never holds your funds. The setup is under paid webinars."],[2426,"A sales webinar script needs a hook and welcome, the buyer's problem, what you teach and the offer. EasyWebinar's AI can draft one in named sections with timings and speaker notes, which you then edit."],[2357,"A sales webinar script needs a hook and welcome, the problem your buyer has, what you teach, and the offer. Plan those sections before you build slides, using the webinar outline template if you want a structure to fill in. Write in the words your buyers use. Nielsen Norman Group's guidance on plain language says simple, familiar words help experts as well as beginners."],[2334,"A sales webinar turns a list of curious people into buyers in one sitting. It teaches something useful first, so the offer at the end feels like the natural next step rather than a pitch."],[5889,"A sales-led demo uses **Hosts only**, so prospects from different companies can ask about pricing or their setup without the rest of the room seeing. Each conversation stays between that attendee and the team."],[4800,"A schedule set once keeps running until you stop it, so check it against the calendar your registrants live by."],[7703,"A screen recorder on your own device"],[2569,"A seminar that sells a programme"],[3525,"A sender people recognise"],[3784,"A sender people recognise"],[1249,"A sending domain isn't a page domain"],[8501,"A sensible schedule"],[3954,"A sensible starting plan is a first invitation two to three weeks before a live webinar and a second about a week before. After people register, your confirmation and reminder emails take over."],[2106,"A separate account per client that the agency manages"],[2105,"A separate account that the client owns"],[2542,"A separate checkout, then an invitation"],[5036,"A session that starts a few minutes after someone registers, instead of at a fixed future time. In EasyWebinar it's an option on evergreen webinars."],[1636,"A shop owner or tradesperson who runs one webinar a month has no one else to send the follow-up. A published workflow sends it after every session, which frees the owner to answer the questions that came in."],[5562,"A short script looks like this:"],[3559,"A short text nudge (Beta)"],[8691,"A simple rule: if you want people to watch the moment they register, use On Demand on an evergreen webinar. If you want people who missed a session to catch up, use the replay page."],[5315,"A simulated live webinar is for a session you'd otherwise present again and again. It gives you the polish and reliability of a recording with the atmosphere of a scheduled event. Attendees still arrive at one time and talk in one room."],[5301,"A simulated live webinar plays a recording you made in advance at times you set, so everyone in a session watches the same moment together. It fits anyone who gives the same presentation every week and is tired of delivering it from scratch each time. In EasyWebinar you choose **Simulive Webinar** when you create the webinar, pick the recording, and set dates or a daily or weekly schedule. You can then sit in the chat while the video plays, or let it run without you."],[5248,"A simulated live webinar plays your recording at set times so attendees watch together while you answer them in chat. This guide covers how it runs, when to choose it over evergreen, and how to set one up in EasyWebinar."],[6900,"A simulive session can have a replay page for people who missed it, set on the **Replay** tab. See Replay pages."],[4788,"A simulive webinar broadcasts the recording at times you set, like a scheduled television programme. Everyone in a session sees the same moment, so the chat works as a shared room, and you can answer questions live while the recording plays. It catches people who value the event feeling. It misses anyone who can't make your times, because attendees register for specific dates."],[5312,"A simulive webinar has five set-up steps rather than the four a live webinar has, because one extra step is where you pick the recording. Once the webinar exists, its **Content** tab carries a **Webinar Video** panel that live webinars don't have. The panel shows the recording you chose and its length, with **Preview** to play it back and **Change Video** to swap it."],[7578,"A simulive webinar plays a recording at scheduled times, so everyone in a session watches the same moment together. The chat still works as a shared room, and you can answer in it while the recording plays. It catches polish and reliability, but it misses anything that would change your talk on the spot."],[1973,"A simulive webinar plays a recording at set times, so everyone watches together. An evergreen webinar runs on a repeating schedule, with just-in-time, on-demand and recurring sessions that each registrant picks."],[6811,"A simulive webinar plays a recording at the dates and times you schedule. Everyone in a session reaches the same point of the video at the same moment, so the chat behaves like one shared room, and you or your moderators can answer questions while the recording runs. Creating one takes five steps in the manual builder: name and type, schedule, pricing, video selection and registration template."],[5866,"A simulive webinar plays a recording at times you set, and everyone in a session sees the same moment of the video. That makes a shared chat possible. Set chat up on the **Engagement** tab as you would for a live webinar, and you and your moderators answer in real time while the recording plays. Being in the chat is most of what simulive gives you over evergreen."],[5396,"A simulive webinar uses the same building blocks as a live one. Only the delivery differs."],[3204,"A single coordinator builds every webinar and hands it to the presenter. This keeps setups consistent, but that person becomes the bottleneck. A holiday or a sick day stops the whole calendar."],[2899,"A sleep coach sells a paid two-hour workshop through paid registration, then offers her group programme inside the room. The workshop recording becomes an evergreen version that plays on demand for new registrants."],[1190,"A small business without a CRM can start in EasyCRM instead of buying another tool. One webinar list, one broadcast with the replay and one follow-up workflow cover most of the work."],[475,"A solo coach describes a monthly masterclass, keeps the **Emails** chip on, and edits only the subject lines and sign-off. The sequence is ready in the same sitting as the pages."],[5014,"A solo consultant can't keep a calendar of live sessions. A just-in-time evergreen webinar means an enquiry from the website can be watching the consultant's explainer within minutes, with **Exclude specific time** keeping chosen hours clear."],[8308,"A solo consultant without a video platform account records a training, uploads it with **Local import**, and runs it as a simulive webinar. Nothing outside EasyWebinar is needed."],[4196,"A solo device check"],[9173,"A solo operator picks **Default Register Page**, changes the headline and background, and publishes. Every page has a working default, so the thank-you and replay pages can wait."],[7092,"A solo operator who can't present every day records a single webinar and runs it on a weekly schedule, with blackout dates over holidays and Chat Simulation keeping the room from looking empty."],[5583,"A solo operator without time to script uses AI Simulation, builds a cast with a mix of beginners, a sceptic and quieter observers, and reviews the cast before turning the simulation on."],[284,"A solo operator without time to write six emails keeps the default chips (script, pages and emails), leaves **Polls** and **Offers** off, and builds a single offer by hand on the **CTAs** tab."],[1717,"A starting set of tags"],[2269,"A step-by-step guide to sales webinars for small business owners and solo operators: choose live or automated, build the registration page, time the offer and follow up with attendees and no-shows."],[3785,"A subject line that names the outcome"],[3526,"A subject line that says what's inside"],[7307,"A team member has their own login to your EasyWebinar account. A presenter or moderator is running a specific webinar, and may be a guest with only a join link."],[77,"A team member is a person with their own login on your EasyWebinar account. Their role sets how much of the account they can change. **Admin** reaches every setting, billing included, while **User** gets basic access to features. Access is set by role alone, with no per-permission matrix."],[3208,"A team member is someone with a login on your account. A presenter or moderator is someone running one specific webinar, and they can join with a link without an account. Getting this difference right saves seats and keeps access tidy."],[7274,"A team member is someone with their own EasyWebinar login, created under **Settings → Team** as an **Admin** or **User**. A presenter or moderator is someone running a specific webinar. Add a person to your team when they work in EasyWebinar regularly; send a join link when they're a guest on one session. Sharing one login between hosts means only one of you can be in the live room as a host. See Team members."],[9624,"A team member is someone with their own login to your EasyWebinar account, added under **Settings → Team** as an **Admin** or a **User**. A presenter or moderator is someone running a specific webinar, added on that webinar's Overview tab. Add someone to the team when they work in EasyWebinar regularly; send a join link when they're a guest on one session."],[1192,"A team on HubSpot or ActiveCampaign keeps it and maps its webinar data across. It doesn't need EasyCRM at all, and nothing on this page changes how its webinars run."],[4143,"A team running a quarterly programme writes one outline per session, then builds the registration page, invitations and follow-ups from it. The outline is also the brief a subject expert presents from, so the session matches what the campaign promised. The webinar lead generation page covers what happens to the registrants afterwards."],[1639,"A team running monthly prospect webinars can keep its own CRM and pass tags across, or use EasyCRM for a single product line. The webinar lead generation guide covers capturing and scoring those leads."],[1834,"A team running one webinar per product line connects each webinar to its own list. When they launch a new live session from an existing one, cloning with **Integration settings** ticked keeps the mappings."],[4349,"A test run passes when the colleague who played the attendee could do everything a real attendee will. Two measures tell you most of it."],[4198,"A test send of each message"],[2900,"A three-coach practice runs its introductory session as a simulive webinar twice a week. The recording plays at set times, and one coach answers in the chat while it runs."],[2541,"A ticket paid at registration"],[6722,"A training provider runs the same class on set weekdays through a term. A **Weekly** schedule with an end date covers the term, and **Blackout Dates** cover the breaks."],[3932,"A two-person bookkeeping firm invites local business owners to a session on year-end records. The owner sends one invitation three weeks out and a second a week before, then reads the averages on the Broadcasts screen."],[1161,"A typical sequence runs like this:"],[5546,"A useful check: look down the attendee list as a newcomer would meet that room. If almost everyone is an enthusiastic expert on high engagement, add curious and sceptical attendees, and move some of the most active ones to Medium or Low."],[6867,"A video on YouTube stays discoverable there unless you've unlisted it, which is a reason to upload a recording you'd rather keep to registrants."],[5172,"A view of how often new registrants should be able to watch."],[2102,"A way to sell and a way to hand off leads"],[885,"A weak prompt produces generic output, so this list is worth five minutes before you start."],[3699,"A webinar built with **Create with AI** already has **Follow-up — Instant** and **Follow-up — 1d** in its sequence, and **Add email** adds more. Those drafts arrive with merge tags in place, so dates, times and join links fill themselves in. Keep the tags and edit the words around them, then use the templates above to tighten the copy and split the audience."],[8428,"A webinar built with **Create with AI** arrives with its emails already written, starting with a welcome email sent on registration. Those AI drafts come with merge tags already in place, so dates, times and join links fill themselves in. Read the wording before you publish, and edit it like any other email."],[279,"A webinar built with AI has the same pricing options as one built by hand. If you're charging for registration, read the generated pages and emails carefully so they describe the price and what's included correctly. See Paid webinars."],[6385,"A webinar can charge for registration and still sell offers inside. Registration payments are reported under **Paid Webinar** and offer sales under **Paid Offer**."],[9422,"A webinar can do both: charge for entry and sell a follow-on offer inside. EasyWebinar reports the two separately under **Revenue by Source**."],[3437,"A webinar can keep collecting registrants long after the live date. An evergreen webinar runs continuously instead of on a fixed date, which suits a demo or an offer you run all year. On Demand, also called instant replay, lets someone watch straight after registering. The same UTM capture and follow-ups apply, and the **Type** column in People shows whether a lead came through a live, simulive or evergreen webinar."],[1081,"A webinar CRM holds the people your webinars produce. This guide shows how EasyCRM imports registrants and attendees, keeps leads, clients and deals, and sends broadcasts and workflows, or how to keep the CRM you already use."],[1122,"A webinar CRM is where the people your webinars produce get worked: registrants and attendees become leads, the promising ones get a deal, and follow-up goes out on time. The question most small teams ask is whether they need a separate CRM for that. With EasyWebinar you don't have to decide in one direction. It's your CRM or ours: keep HubSpot, Salesforce Pardot (Account Engagement), ActiveCampaign, Mailchimp or Kit (formerly ConvertKit), or use EasyCRM, the CRM built into EasyWebinar, natively."],[1140,"A webinar CRM keeps the people a webinar produces in one place, with a next step attached. It turns \"we had a good webinar\" into deals you can count."],[380,"A webinar email sequence is the set of messages that confirms a registration, reminds people to turn up and follows up afterwards. Writing all of them by hand for every webinar is slow, so reminders get skipped and follow-ups go out late. EasyWebinar, the webinar platform, drafts six of those emails for you while its AI builds the webinar, with timing and merge tags already set. You then edit the wording and timing on the webinar's **Notifications** tab, add any emails you need, and check each one before it sends."],[321,"A webinar email sequence takes hours to write by hand. This guide covers the six emails EasyWebinar's AI drafts from your webinar's own script, how to edit their wording and timing, when to add more and what to check before anything sends."],[9276,"A webinar has several audiences at once. Some people still need to register, some are already registered, and some missed the session. Others are helping you run it. The Share dialog gives each group its own way in, from one screen."],[7744,"A webinar I ran several times has several recordings. Which is which?"],[837,"A webinar introduction script sample you can adapt:"],[3953,"A webinar invitation should name the reader's problem, say what they'll leave with, and give the date, time, time zone and length. It needs one registration link, repeated, plus your name, postal address and an unsubscribe link."],[3883,"A webinar invitation to your list is usually a marketing email, so these rules apply to it. This is a summary, not legal advice."],[2712,"A webinar is mostly a talk with questions. A workshop asks people to take part during the session, so chat, Q&A and polls do more of the work. Both are set up the same way in EasyWebinar."],[2614,"A webinar is usually a talk with questions at the end. A workshop asks the audience to do something during the session: answer, try, decide. A seminar sits between the two, with an expert leading a group through one subject in depth. The guide to seminar types and how to host one covers the formats in more detail."],[1162,"A webinar runs and generates registrants."],[2430,"A webinar sales funnel moves people from the registration page to the session, then to the offer and checkout. EasyWebinar's analytics count each step, from visitors and registrations to offer views, clicks and purchases."],[855,"A webinar script is the right length when it fits the running time with room to spare. The simplest way to check that is to measure it, section by section, before the day."],[930,"A webinar script template is a set order of sections, such as welcome, problem, teaching points, offer and close, with blanks to fill in and a share of the running time for each. It lets you write a webinar script by filling in sections instead of starting from an empty page."],[737,"A webinar script template you can fill in, section by section, plus how to generate a slide-by-slide webinar script with AI in EasyWebinar, set its length, edit it and regenerate it."],[817,"A webinar script turns a topic into something you can say out loud in a set time. It decides what comes first, how long each part runs and where the offer lands. Everything else in the webinar, from the slides to the registration page, is built from it."],[4187,"A webinar setup checklist for the days before and the hour before a live session: what to confirm, what to test in the green room, and what to set so nothing breaks once you go live."],[4252,"A webinar setup checklist is the list you work through before a live session so that the camera, the sound, the links and the timed pieces all work when people arrive. Most of what goes wrong on the day can be caught in a test run a day or two before, and the rest in the last half hour. This page gives you a fill-in checklist for both, says what each check catches, and shows where each one sits in EasyWebinar's live room, which lets you test privately before anything is broadcast."],[4261,"A webinar setup checklist turns that hour into a fixed order of checks. It covers testing and set-up only, not what you will say. For planning the session itself, the webinar content planning checklist on the EasyWebinar blog covers topics, structure and slides."],[4163,"A webinar that teaches and then sells needs eight sections: opening and housekeeping, the promise, three teaching points, a bridge, the offer, and questions with a close. Each one gets a time budget so the offer and questions keep their room."],[2484,"A webinar type is the way your content reaches people. EasyWebinar offers three types of webinar, and you choose one when you create it. The choice sets your schedule and how attendees take part. All three use the same pages, emails, polls, offers and analytics, so only the delivery differs."],[6326,"A webinar with no polls or offers yet shows **\"No CTAs found\"**."],[4453,"A webinar's own **Analytics** tab gives you counts: how many registered, how many attended, how many missed. **People** gives you the names behind those counts, for every webinar in your account at once."],[1707,"A webinar's registration page collects registrants and can take payment through your own Stripe account. Registrants get reminder emails from the Notifications tab, and SMS or WhatsApp messages, both currently in Beta. They join the room live, or a recording plays at a scheduled time (simulive) or on demand (evergreen). Attendance, offer clicks and revenue flow into the webinar's Analytics and the People view, and out to connected CRMs and email tools, or into EasyCRM."],[1913,"A webinar's registration page collects registrants and can take payment through your Stripe account. Registrants then get emails from the **Notifications** tab, with SMS (Beta) and WhatsApp (Beta) as extra channels. They join the room live, or a recording plays at a set time (simulive) or around the clock (evergreen). Attendance, offer clicks and revenue then flow into the webinar's **Analytics** and the **People** view, and out to your CRM or into EasyCRM."],[7992,"A webinar's type can't be changed, so the route from live to automated is cloning. Clone the live webinar, choose **Evergreen** for the copy, and pick the live session's recording on the new webinar's **Content** tab. The recording is already in your library. See Clone a webinar."],[7449,"A webinar's type can't be changed, so the way to automate a successful live session is to clone it as **Evergreen** and pick its recording. See Clone a webinar."],[2915,"A webinar's type is fixed once it exists, so moving from live to evergreen always means a clone. Automation, EasyCRM, checkout and EasyCast start at the Growth plan, and the standard trial is 7 days with a card required. Plan details are on the pricing page."],[3893,"A week later, check registrations against the list and send the second invitation."],[6681,"A weekly webinar runs until you stop it. Use **Blackout Dates** to skip individual days without ending the series, or set an end date to stop it."],[499,"A welcome email on registration, reminders a day, an hour and fifteen minutes before, a follow-up right after the webinar ends and another a day afterwards. All six arrive with merge tags in place."],[2769,"A wellness coach with a paid workshop"],[1372,"A whole-list send would overrun the allowance, so the targeted list is the better first broadcast. Even if every address were at Gmail, the largest day stays well under the bulk level, though the rules for all senders still apply. Two complaints on 600 emails would pass 0.3%, so this rough guide favours people who expect to hear from you. Each provider measures its own traffic, so treat the last row as a planning figure."],[1496,"A workflow in the CRM attached to the webinar"],[1179,"A workflow is an automated sequence that keeps running after you build it. You open it from **Marketing → Campaign**. A workflow stays in **Draft** until you publish it, so nothing reaches a contact while you're building. A good first workflow sends attendees one message and no-shows the replay. Our guide to webinar follow-up emails covers what to write in each."],[1618,"A workflow only sends once it's published. Before you publish, check which list it sends to, read every message once more, and confirm your sender is verified. Once published, it moves to the **Published** tab and starts running."],[3703,"A workflow stays in Draft until you publish it, so you can paste in these templates and revise them before anything reaches a contact."],[813,"A working template covers the whole run of the session. It starts with the welcome and housekeeping, moves through the problem and your teaching points, and ends with the offer and the close. Each section has a rough share of the running time. Each slide has notes on what you say, not just what the slide shows."],[9373,"Abandoned checkouts"],[1069,"About 23 for all five parts. The script and pages use 10 each, and emails, polls and offers use 1 each. Your balance shows at the top right of every step."],[728,"About Quality Score for Search campaigns, Google Ads Help: how Google judges the landing page behind a search ad."],[722,"About ten minutes end to end for the full build. The script is the slowest part, at roughly a minute and a half."],[306,"About ten minutes end to end. The script is the slowest part, at roughly a minute and a half."],[1691,"About this documentation"],[3420,"add a **Phone** field if sales will call;"],[8182,"Add a **Webinar Follow-up** on the **Notifications** tab, set its audience to no-shows, and include the replay link."],[8622,"Add a **Webinar Follow-up** with its audience set to no-shows and include the replay link."],[3113,"Add a **Webinar Follow-up** with the audience set to no-shows and include the replay link, or tag them with the **Missed** event in your email tool."],[2293,"Add a countdown"],[6253,"Add a deadline"],[8764,"Add a phone field, your own custom questions, a GDPR consent checkbox and UTM campaign capture to an EasyWebinar registration form, and see where that data shows up afterwards."],[6,"Add a team member"],[8203,"Add a video while you set up a webinar"],[8500,"Add another reminder"],[1260,"Add call and SMS (Beta) sending"],[3554,"Add each follow-up"],[1358,"Add each sending service to one SPF record, keeping a single record for the domain."],[8924,"Add it to your site"],[422,"Add more when the webinar has a specific job. A no-show follow-up with the replay link suits almost every session, because no-shows are usually the biggest group. A follow-up for people who clicked an offer and didn't buy suits any webinar that sells. Use **Add email** during the build, or **Create New** in the right group on the **Notifications** tab. Avoid splitting one message across several emails, though. In usability research on transactional email, companies that spread one transaction over too many messages caused usability problems."],[74,"Add someone to your team when they work in EasyWebinar regularly."],[353,"Add texts for the final reminder"],[2201,"Add the **C-Name** add-on from **Plan & Billing → Add-ons**. Then ask the client, or whoever runs their DNS, to create a CNAME record pointing the chosen subdomain at EasyWebinar. In DNS terms, a CNAME record marks one name as an alias for another, which is why the client's address can show your webinar pages."],[8837,"Add the **Phone** field if you'll want to text people or keep their number."],[2203,"Add the **White Label** add-on from the same **Add-ons** tab. It removes EasyWebinar branding from your pages and the room. Anything already switched on is marked **Active**, so check this tab first if a page still shows the wrong name."],[652,"Add the date, time and timezone, and a registration form that asks only what you need."],[5552,"Add the names for **Attendees on Event Page**, separated by commas."],[4070,"Add the poll plan underneath, using the same section numbers:"],[2249,"Add the White Label add-on under Plan & Billing, on the Add-ons tab. It removes EasyWebinar branding from your pages and the webinar room, so attendees see only the client's brand."],[2749,"Add urgency only when it's real"],[3421,"add your own custom fields, such as company or job title;"],[3331,"Add your pixels and analytics tag"],[1994,"Add-ons"],[2043,"Add-ons extend your plan without changing it. Switch one on with **Add to plan** on the **Add-ons** tab under **Settings → Manage Plan**."],[3119,"Add-ons: the **Paid Webinars**, **C-Name** and **White Label** add-ons mentioned on this page."],[5708,"Adjust its settings across the **Settings** and **Advanced** tabs."],[126,"Admin can change everything, including settings and billing. User gets basic access to the features."],[4640,"After a busy session, the **Not Answered Rate** on Q&A tells you how many questions you left behind. Those are worth answering in your follow-up email."],[8151,"After a live launch webinar, a creator sets the replay to **Expire after days**, keeps **Webinar Replica** so the enrolment offer stays on the page, and points **After the replay ends** at the course checkout. A follow-up to no-shows carries the replay link. See selling online courses with webinars."],[1954,"After a webinar, you need to know who came, who left early and who clicked. Webinar analytics shows the funnel from page visitors to registrations, live attendance and replay views. It also counts **Missed**: people who registered and never showed up, who are often the group most worth emailing."],[2633,"After each payment, copy the buyer onto your attendee list and send them the join link."],[159,"After generating: finish the webinar"],[3606,"After that, your deadline sets the schedule. Count back from the moment the offer or the replay closes, not forward from the session."],[679,"After the build you land on the Overview, with the landing pages listed beside the script and emails. The figures belong to a sample webinar."],[4352,"After the real session, the webinar analytics page shows how many registrants attended, which tells you whether the reminders did their job."],[3411,"After the session, export the attendance list and match it to the sheet by email."],[5885,"After the session, the webinar's **Analytics** tab has a **Chat** panel showing total messages, which you can filter by session."],[6718,"After the webinar exists, its **Overview** tab holds the schedule. The options menu on **Scheduling Options** changes the whole schedule, and the menu on a session row acts on one sitting. Click **Share** for the **Registration Page Link** you promote, or use the sharing links guide."],[5722,"After the webinar, open **Analytics**. **Offers Performance** shows views, clicks and purchases for each offer with a click rate. **Views to Clicks** and **Clicks to Purchases** show where the offer loses people, and **Revenue by Offer** shows what each offer earned. Comparing an offer with and without a countdown across sessions is the clearest way to see what the timer is doing for you."],[2562,"After the workshop: send buyers somewhere"],[1398,"After your first send, compare these cards with the industry averages shown beneath them. The figures here are sample data from a test account."],[675,"Afterwards, the same pages open in the normal builder. Click any block to select it: an orange toolbar appears above it, and the right-hand panel shows its settings across **Settings** and **Advanced** tabs. Use the orange **+** below a block to add a new one underneath."],[27,"Agencies"],[356,"Agencies"],[560,"Agencies"],[1271,"Agencies"],[3159,"Agencies"],[3346,"Agencies"],[3814,"Agencies"],[4428,"Agencies"],[4738,"Agencies"],[5090,"Agencies"],[5280,"Agencies"],[5480,"Agencies"],[5796,"Agencies"],[6270,"Agencies"],[6460,"Agencies"],[7913,"Agencies"],[8214,"Agencies"],[8371,"Agencies"],[8789,"Agencies"],[8936,"Agencies"],[9071,"Agencies"],[9242,"Agencies"],[9552,"Agencies"],[1959,"Agencies can add client workspaces. Each client gets a separate account you manage, and their webinars, registrants and settings stay apart from yours. Adding a workspace doesn't use a seat."],[2501,"Agencies run webinars for several clients, and small businesses often share the work between two or three people. Give each person their own login with team members and roles. To repeat a set-up, clone a webinar instead of building it again. Guides to client webinar funnels and team webinar programmes are planned."],[2147,"Agencies that sell webinars as a service hit the same wall: every client wants their own brand on the page, and every client wants to see results. White label webinar software solves that by giving each client a separate account, their own domain and pages without the vendor's name. In EasyWebinar, you set up a client workspace for each client, add the C-Name and White Label add-ons, and build a repeatable funnel inside it. Results come from each webinar's analytics and the People list export."],[4330,"Agree who speaks when, who answers questions and who ends the session."],[1991,"AI credits"],[959,"AI for webinars at a glance"],[1048,"AI for webinars features, from each platform's public documentation:"],[4160,"AI generation uses credits, shown at the top right of every step. The script and the pages cost the most, at 10 credits each."],[1950,"AI in EasyWebinar works before and during the webinar, while the platform and its results stay the point."],[2265,"AI landing page generation"],[949,"AI landing page generation: the pages written from your script."],[5789,"AI Moderation (Beta)"],[6112,"AI Moderation (Beta)"],[5353,"AI Moderation also needs the built-in chat. EasyWebinar's AI Moderator answers attendee questions from context you upload, including PDFs and other files, from the Pro plan onward. The AI webinar features page explains what it drafts and answers. Choosing **Third party** replaces the built-in chat, so AI Moderation and simulated chat stop applying."],[5876,"AI Moderation and simulated chat only work with **Easywebinar Chat**. If you want either of them, don't choose **Third party**."],[4921,"AI Moderator (Beta)"],[6974,"AI Moderator (Beta)"],[7909,"AI Moderator (Beta)"],[5468,"AI or manual: which to use"],[693,"AI webinar landing page features, from each platform's public documentation:"],[582,"AI webinar landing pages take on a job many small teams put off: writing the registration page, the thank-you page and the waiting page before a webinar. If you need a webinar landing page live this week and have no designer, you want two answers. Can the AI build it, and how much will you still change? In EasyWebinar, the AI writes all three pages from the script it drafts first, then hands them to the normal page builder, where you check the facts, edit what you like and publish."],[522,"AI webinar landing pages, explained: what the AI writes on your registration, thank-you and waiting pages from your script, how much you'll still change, and how to edit, check and publish them in the page builder."],[946,"AI webinar script generator: EasyWebinar's overview of writing webinar scripts with AI."],[287,"AI webinar-building features, from each platform's public documentation:"],[957,"AI Webinar: What AI Drafts, What It Answers During the Session, What You Still Review"],[1078,"AI-generated landing pages"],[3762,"Aim for about 40 characters, with the useful words first, such as the replay or the deadline. That's Nielsen Norman Group's 2014 guidance, because inboxes can cut longer lines off."],[1330,"Alignment is the idea that links all this to your goal. Your contacts see your From address, so these checks only help your name when they pass for that same domain."],[5168,"All 15 items come across when every box is ticked, so 15 ÷ 15 = 100% of this setup is copied. What's left is the work no copy can do: checking the recording and setting the evergreen schedule. If she later sees 400 registrants, 240 viewers and 36 offer clicks, that is 240 ÷ 400 = 60% attendance and 36 ÷ 240 = 15% of viewers clicking."],[621,"All five pages already carry your webinar's date, time and timezone, so you don't type them into the copy. The build doesn't set the schedule for you, though. Set it before you check the pages, or the dates you see won't be the real ones."],[613,"All four routes are documented. **Build Your Own** starts from a blank page, and the template step lets you filter by categories such as **Coach**, **Education** and **Lead Magnet**. The AI route writes three pages from your script. For your own site, the **Registration Widget** on the **Content** tab gives you embed code, covered in registration embeds."],[7152,"All three types use the same pages, emails, polls, offers and analytics. Only the delivery differs. Each webinar has these tabs:"],[1456,"Almost always, the sending identity isn't verified or no provider is connected. Open **Configurations**, check the notice on **Email Integration**, and finish whichever step is missing. If only a workflow is silent, check that it's published, because a draft sends nothing."],[3394,"An ad platform's pixel records who saw your registration page and who reached the thank-you page. The thank-you page only loads after someone registers, which makes it a reliable conversion signal. This catches which ads converted, and it lets you retarget people who looked and didn't sign up. It misses everything after registration, because the data sits in the ad platform rather than beside attendance."],[2100,"An address the client recognises"],[3294,"An Admin has full access, including settings and billing. A User gets basic access to organisation features, which suits people who build, present or moderate webinars without touching the account."],[6391,"An agency building a client's webinar uses **Create with AI** with **Balanced Approach** to draft offers, edits them into the client's voice, and maps **Clicked offer** to a tag in the client's CRM."],[5585,"An agency building evergreen funnels for several clients uses AI Simulation to set up each room quickly, then switches individual webinars to Manual Simulation once a client wants a fixed, approved script."],[690,"An agency building webinars for several clients can generate a first draft for each one and hand it to the client for review. Producers can each have their own login through team members, and next month's webinar starts from a clone."],[9006,"An agency builds a client's landing page in the client's site builder and pastes the widget in, so the page stays on the client's domain and in the client's design."],[4854,"An agency builds an evergreen webinar for a client's lead-generation funnel and adds blackout dates for the client's holidays. The recording can be swapped later without disturbing registrations."],[8307,"An agency builds automated webinars from videos its clients keep on Vimeo or Wistia. Importing the links leaves the files where the clients manage them, and the platform badge in the Library shows which videos depend on an outside host."],[9171,"An agency builds registration pages for several clients. It works from the **Agency** or **Marketing** templates, applies each client's fonts and background in **Settings**, and pastes the client's pixel into **Tracking Code** so the client's ad account sees the traffic."],[6545,"An agency keeps a webinar it has refined over several events and clones it as the starting point for each new one, unticking anything that should be set up fresh."],[7999,"An agency managing webinars that play videos from a client's YouTube channel checks the badges on the **Videos** tab before the client tidies up their channel, because removing a YouTube video breaks the webinar that plays it."],[1431,"An agency often sends for a client whose domain it doesn't control, so the DNS work may sit with the client's own team. Raise it early, because DNS changes can take a few hours to propagate. A separate client workspace keeps that client's webinars, registrants and settings apart from yours."],[5406,"An agency running a recurring webinar for a client can record it with the client's speaker once. The agency then schedules the sessions and staffs the chat itself, without booking the speaker again."],[473,"An agency running a webinar a month for each client builds every one with AI, then rewrites the six drafts in each client's voice. The account lead checks sender names and offers before anything is scheduled."],[4514,"An agency running webinars for a client filters by **Webinar**, exports the attendees with their **Lead score** and **Offer clicked** columns, and hands the file to the client's sales team, who don't use EasyWebinar."],[8877,"An agency running webinars for clients sets the consent wording to each client's own policy and exports the filtered registrant list for the client after each session."],[8449,"An agency running webinars for several clients sets each webinar's **Sender name** to the client's company and the **Reply-to email** to the client's own address. Because the settings are held per webinar, each client's registrants hear from the client, not the agency."],[5205,"An agency runs a live session for a client's launch, then hands over an evergreen copy with the same pages, polls and integration settings. The client's leads keep flowing into the same tool."],[9312,"An agency runs a webinar for a client whose founder is the speaker. The agency sends the founder the **Co-presenter Join Link** and its own team member the **Moderator Join Link for Live room**. Neither needs a login to the agency's account, and the founder waits backstage until the host brings them on."],[9659,"An agency runs webinars for clients, with the client's own speaker presenting. The account manager sends the speaker the **Co-presenter Join Link** from **Share**, so the speaker doesn't need an EasyWebinar login, and adds a colleague as moderator under **Team & Moderators**."],[3934,"An agency runs webinars for several clients and needs each invitation to come from the right sender. It verifies each client's sending identity before any broadcast goes out."],[3471,"An agency runs webinars for several clients from one account. Because integrations are set per webinar, each client's webinar can feed that client's own list, and a **Webinar** filter in People keeps their results apart."],[3269,"An agency runs webinars for several clients. Each client gets its own workspace and plan, so registrants and settings never mix, and staff work across them from one place."],[5891,"An agency that already handles conversations in an external chat tool runs a client's evergreen webinar with **Third party** chat, so messages land in the tool its team watches every day."],[101,"An agency webinar team gives its staff **User** logins on the agency account. Each client gets a client workspace, so that client's webinars, registrants and settings stay apart from everyone else's."],[410,"An AI drafts the emails from the content it has written for the webinar. It catches consistency, since the emails describe the same session as the pages. It misses anything the script got wrong, and it can't know details you never gave it, such as a price change."],[997,"An AI webinar starts the same way a hand-built one does: you know the topic and the offer, but the script, pages, emails and polls are still blank. AI for webinars in EasyWebinar fills that gap in two places. Before the session it drafts the whole webinar from one prompt, and during the session it can answer attendee questions from Pro onward. This page shows what each part does and what you still need to check yourself."],[3966,"An announcement tells people a webinar exists, often on social media or your site. An invitation is a personal email to your list with a registration link, asking each person to save a seat."],[2814,"An application suits a programme with limited places or an entry bar. It filters out poor fits before they reach your diary. It also adds a step, so expect fewer people to finish than would click a booking link."],[5684,"An automated webinar has no presenter to announce the deadline at the right moment. Since each session plays without the coach, its deadline has to keep time without the coach as well. The timer therefore belongs on the offer, which every session's audience sees."],[4769,"An automated webinar is a webinar whose content is a recording rather than a live presenter. The platform plays that recording at scheduled times, and registrants join a session the way they would join a live one. In EasyWebinar, two of the three webinar types work this way: **Simulive** and **Evergreen**. A **Live** webinar needs no recording, because you supply the content by presenting. Automated webinars on EasyWebinar gives the product overview."],[4774,"An automated webinar lets a session you deliver often keep running without you in the room. People who find your registration page can watch soon, at a time that suits them, instead of waiting for your next live slot."],[4759,"An automated webinar plays a recording at the times your registrants choose, so one good session can run all week without you presenting it again. EasyWebinar builds that from three parts: the recording, the webinar type, and the schedule that turns the recording into sessions. This page explains how those parts fit together, so you can pick a set-up before you build."],[8805,"An EasyWebinar registration form starts with two required fields, **Full name** and **Email**. Select the form block in the page builder and you can add a **Phone** field, add your own custom fields, turn on a **GDPR Compliance** consent checkbox, and turn on **UTM Params** so each registration records the campaign that brought it in. What you collect then shows up in the **People** view and can be mapped into a connected CRM."],[7144,"An evergreen schedule can offer just-in-time sessions, On Demand viewing and a recurring daily or weekly schedule. All three can run at once, and the registrant picks."],[8681,"An evergreen webinar can offer three ways to watch. On Demand is the one with no wait at all: the registrant doesn't pick a session time, they start the recording when they're ready."],[4789,"An evergreen webinar can run **Daily** or **Weekly** at the times you choose, with **Add Time** for more than one slot a day. These sessions run in the webinar's timezone. This catches people who like to plan ahead. It misses people who arrive keen right now and face a long wait."],[8582,"An evergreen webinar generates a long run of sessions from one schedule, and can add just-in-time sessions that start shortly after someone registers. Reminders are timed relative to the webinar rather than to a clock, which is why they work for recurring and evergreen schedules without being set session by session."],[5224,"An evergreen webinar is a recorded webinar that runs continuously instead of on a fixed date. A visitor picks a session that suits them, and the recording plays for them at that time."],[7062,"An evergreen webinar is one of EasyWebinar's three types. Live webinars are presented in real time; simulive webinars broadcast a recording at times you set, with everyone watching together. Evergreen also plays a recording, but the schedule repeats and each registrant chooses their own time."],[5111,"An evergreen webinar keeps a session you have already delivered working for people who weren't there on the day. If your best live webinar only reached the people who turned up, EasyWebinar, a webinar platform for live and automated webinars, lets you clone it as an evergreen webinar. You keep the pages, polls, offers and emails you choose, play the recording of the live session, and let each registrant pick a time."],[5185,"An evergreen webinar offers three ways to watch, and you can use any combination of them."],[6999,"An evergreen webinar plays a recording on a schedule that keeps running, instead of on one fixed date. A visitor to the registration page picks a session that suits them, and the recording plays for them at that time. You set up the schedule once, with any combination of just-in-time sessions, On Demand viewing and recurring daily or weekly times, and EasyWebinar generates the sessions from it."],[5120,"An evergreen webinar runs continuously instead of on a fixed date. Someone arriving at your registration page picks a session that suits them, and the recording plays for them at that time. EasyWebinar fixes a webinar's type when you create it, so a live webinar never becomes evergreen in place. You make a copy instead: the clone is set to Evergreen, and it plays the recording of your live session."],[3023,"An evergreen webinar runs continuously, so every new visitor can watch soon after they find you. Its schedule has three options, and you can use any combination:"],[5330,"An evergreen webinar runs on a repeating schedule rather than a fixed date. Someone landing on your page picks a session that suits them, and the recording plays for them then. This catches nearly everyone, whatever their timezone, and it runs whether or not you're around. It misses the single shared room, because attendees arrive at different times, so a genuinely shared live chat isn't possible."],[2860,"An evergreen webinar runs on a repeating schedule rather than a fixed date. You can offer three ways to watch, in any combination."],[7579,"An evergreen webinar runs on a repeating schedule, and each registrant picks a session that suits them. It catches people who would never make a fixed time, and it works whether or not you're available. It misses the shared room, because each viewer meets the content on their own schedule."],[6388,"An evergreen webinar teaches one core idea and closes with an enrolment offer pointing at the course checkout on the creator's course platform. A countdown marks a bonus window, and a **Webinar Replica** replay keeps the offer working for people who watch later."],[5727,"An evergreen webinar teaches the core idea of a course and ends with an enrolment offer. A countdown on the offer marks the window for an enrolment bonus. Because the webinar is automated, the timer carries the deadline for every session without the creator being there."],[3230,"An exported list is personal data, so agree who on the team may export and why. UK data protection law expects personal data to be used for specified, explicit purposes and kept for no longer than is necessary."],[1811,"An integration links one webinar to a CRM or email tool you already use. It has two halves. **Event mappings** apply a tag when someone does something, such as attending or missing the session. **Custom fields** copy webinar details, such as the title and each person's join link, into fields on the contact."],[2689,"An investor-education firm runs a paid seminar with a disclaimer block on the registration page and a required consent box. Replays use **Specific date** so older material closes on schedule."],[3844,"An invitation moves people from your list onto your registration page. After they register, other emails take over: the registration confirmation goes out immediately, and reminders go out before the session starts. The invitation only has to earn the click."],[6288,"An offer in EasyWebinar is a card with a headline and a button that you show to attendees during a webinar. The button sends people wherever the next step is: a checkout, a booking page or a sales page. You build offers on the webinar's **CTAs** tab, with an optional price and an optional countdown, then push them to the room when you make your pitch. Afterwards, Analytics shows how many people saw, clicked and bought each offer, and what it earned."],[2828,"An offer in EasyWebinar is a card with a headline and a button, shown during the webinar. You build it on the **CTAs** tab, and the offers page covers every setting."],[6307,"An offer is not the same as charging for the webinar itself. Paid registration sells access to the session before it starts; an offer sells something separate, during or after it. EasyWebinar reports the two separately."],[6308,"An offer turns your pitch into something attendees can act on. It puts one button on screen at the moment you ask, pointing at the exact next step. Nobody has to remember a web address or hunt for it later."],[4162,"An offer's button opens the URL you set, such as your own checkout, booking or sales page."],[1428,"An office admin or owner usually does this alone, with a first send already in the diary. Validating a single business address is the quick way to get going. Move to a domain once sending becomes regular, and ask whoever manages your website to help with the DNS."],[5890,"An onboarding session runs as a simulive webinar. The recording does the teaching, and the customer success team sits in the chat answering setup questions live while it plays."],[4048,"An outline turns a topic into a running order. It tells you which three ideas you are teaching, how long each one gets, and where the offer and polls sit. The slides and script then follow a plan instead of creating one."],[688,"An owner who runs the webinar 