kshop in EasyWebinar"],[8045,"Set up a replay page"],[6763,"Set up a simulive webinar in EasyWebinar that plays a pre-recorded video at the dates and times you schedule, with a shared chat you can answer in while it plays."],[5462,"Set up AI Simulation"],[976,"Set up an AI webinar"],[5634,"Set up an offer countdown"],[4219,"Set up and test in EasyWebinar"],[4978,"Set up any other options you want alongside it: **On Demand**, **Recurring Schedule**, **Availability** and **Schedule Preferences**."],[5776,"Set up chat"],[2110,"Set up client webinar funnels in EasyWebinar"],[5465,"Set up Manual Simulation"],[6104,"Set up Q&A"],[7316,"Set up Q&A: set up the question queue a moderator will work through."],[1607,"Set up sending and verify who you send as under **Configurations**."],[3800,"Set up sending first"],[6333,"Set up the **Button**: size, icon, text and **Button URL**."],[2292,"Set up the offer card"],[2921,"Set up the payment plan on your own checkout page, then paste that page's link into the offer button. EasyWebinar's offer opens the checkout you already use, so the payment terms stay where you manage them."],[8773,"Set up the registration form"],[5637,"Set up the registration-page countdown"],[2299,"Set up your first sales webinar in EasyWebinar"],[3151,"Set up your team in EasyWebinar"],[9362,"Set your price"],[4729,"Set your pricing"],[4728,"Set your schedule"],[680,"Set your schedule, then click **Preview** to open each real page in a new tab. **Save** keeps a draft and **Publish** makes the page public. Each page has its own **Publish** button, and your registration link doesn't work until the registration page is published."],[4912,"Settings"],[8644,"Settings"],[1422,"Several parts of the product rely on this setup or sit right beside it:"],[7196,"Share links"],[8928,"Share links"],[9063,"Share links"],[3238,"Share one login, or ask each person to set up their own tool."],[7609,"Share your offer link at the right moment."],[8055,"Sharing"],[9317,"Sharing link features, from each platform's public documentation:"],[9213,"Sharing links"],[7489,"Sharing links: the co-presenter and moderator links that bring people backstage."],[9208,"Sharing links: the link that only works once this page is published."],[9035,"Sharing links: the Registration Page Link and One-Click Registration Link that sit beside the widget."],[9692,"Sharing links: who gets each link behind **Share**, and which to keep private."],[7511,"Sharing your screen, zooming in and drawing"],[3965,"Short enough that the useful words show. Nielsen Norman Group advises displaying no more than about 40 characters and putting the most important words first."],[9232,"Shorten the links"],[4987,"Shorter intervals mean less waiting. The interval sets how often a new just-in-time session comes round, so it also sets the longest a new registrant waits."],[2310,"Should a first sales webinar be live or automated?"],[8671,"Should every evergreen webinar have On Demand on?"],[5108,"Should I clone as Simulive or Evergreen?"],[6475,"Should I clone before testing changes on a running webinar?"],[7216,"Should I set up EasyCast in the green room?"],[8527,"Should I test a text or WhatsApp reminder first?"],[8945,"Should I use the widget or the registration page link?"],[1282,"Should I verify a domain or a single address?"],[4035,"Should my webinar offer have a countdown?"],[3569,"Should no-shows get a different email from attendees?"],[6125,"Should questions be public or private?"],[3571,"Should the replay link go in the first follow-up?"],[3821,"Should you send a second invitation to people who didn't register?"],[3876,"Should you send a second invitation to people who didn't register? Yes, once, with a new angle rather than the same email again. Lead with one question the session answers, or with what a real attendee will be able to do."],[6256,"Show an offer during the webinar"],[2124,"Show-up rate"],[6645,"Sign in to EasyWebinar. **Any team member can create a webinar**; you don't need to be the account owner."],[1775,"Sign in to that service and allow access."],[544,"Sign-ups your ad platform can see"],[8415,"Signing up is the moment a buyer cares most. An email from a name they know, with a reply that reaches a real person, can turn that moment into a chat. Knowing who signed up also lets the broker prepare."],[5577,"Simulated attendees can show in the attendee list too, with **Show attendee list on event page**. The webinar-wide **Show Attendees** setting on the **Settings** tab controls whether attendees see the list at all."],[3032,"Simulated attendees make the room look populated. They shouldn't claim results or endorse your course."],[5587,"Simulated chat features, from each platform's public documentation:"],[5572,"Simulated chat is there to stop an automated room looking empty. It isn't a place for social proof that didn't happen."],[974,"Simulated chat on recorded webinars (Beta)"],[5263,"Simulated live or evergreen: which to choose"],[5409,"Simulated live webinar features, from each platform's public documentation:"],[5247,"Simulated Live Webinar: Run a Recording at Set Times, With Chat and Offers Beside It"],[5260,"Simulated live: a recording at set times"],[1876,"Simulive"],[2279,"Simulive"],[2451,"Simulive"],[7133,"Simulive"],[6536,"Simulive and evergreen copies play a video from the Library. Recordings of webinars you've already run appear under **Recordings** automatically when auto recording was on, ready to pick on the copy's **Content** tab. See The Library."],[5951,"Simulive and evergreen webinars"],[7907,"Simulive and evergreen webinars"],[8208,"Simulive and evergreen webinars"],[2627,"Simulive and evergreen webinars both offer **Free** or **Paid** on their pricing step, so a recorded paid seminar is possible from the start. If you'd rather prove the workshop live first, turn a past live session into an evergreen one by cloning it. The simulive setup explains how a recording plays at set times."],[9446,"Simulive and evergreen webinars have the same **Free** or **Paid** choice. On those types, the pricing step comes after the schedule and before **Continue to Video Selection**, where you pick the recording the sessions will play."],[8231,"Simulive and evergreen webinars play a recording, so before you can run one you need a video in your library. EasyWebinar gives you two ways to get it there. **Local import** uploads the file and EasyWebinar hosts it for you, so you don't need a separate video host. **Import from URL** plays a video that already lives on **YouTube**, **Vimeo**, **Wistia** or at a direct **MP4** link. The route you pick decides whether the video uses your storage and whether you need to tell EasyWebinar how long it runs."],[7963,"Simulive and evergreen webinars play a video from your library:"],[6884,"Simulive is for a presentation you give again and again, or a session at an hour you'd rather not be awake for. You record the talk once. EasyWebinar then broadcasts it at the times you choose, like a scheduled programme."],[5311,"Simulive is one of three webinar types in EasyWebinar, beside live and evergreen. Live means you present in real time. Simulive and evergreen both play a recording, and they differ in who decides when it plays."],[2348,"Simulive plays a recording at set times, with attendees registering for specific dates. Everyone in a session sees the same moment together, so the chat still works as a shared room. It suits a second run when you want to be in the chat but not on camera. The setup is covered in create a simulive webinar."],[6910,"Simulive set-up, from each platform's public documentation:"],[5475,"Simulive webinars"],[5449,"Simulive webinars at EasyWebinar: the product overview."],[7504,"Simulive: a recording at set times"],[5783,"Simulive: answer while the recording plays"],[4714,"Simulive: set times, one shared room"],[4667,"Single-webinar reporting, from each platform's public help docs:"],[498,"Six is a sound start: a confirmation, reminders a day, an hour and fifteen minutes before, and two follow-ups. Add a no-show follow-up with the replay link, and an offer-click follow-up if the webinar sells."],[421,"Six is a sound starting point: one confirmation, three reminders and two follow-ups. The three-reminder pattern of a day, an hour and fifteen minutes before is EasyWebinar's own suggested default, and the last one does most of the work."],[2197,"Size it on live attendees, since that is the number that fails loudly if you get it wrong. Selecting a plan starts a subscription, so settle with your client first who pays for it and whether you re-bill it. Plan costs are on the EasyWebinar pricing page."],[4813,"Skip holidays by hand, and move slots when clocks change."],[5994,"Skip polls and you present without knowing who is listening or what they need. Attention drifts through the long sections, and nobody is asked to do anything until the offer. After the session, attendance is the only record of who took part."],[169,"Small businesses and solo operators"],[558,"Small businesses and solo operators"],[1103,"Small businesses and solo operators"],[1268,"Small businesses and solo operators"],[1514,"Small businesses and solo operators"],[3347,"Small businesses and solo operators"],[3812,"Small businesses and solo operators"],[4928,"Small businesses and solo operators"],[5281,"Small businesses and solo operators"],[5478,"Small businesses and solo operators"],[6983,"Small businesses and solo operators"],[8215,"Small businesses and solo operators"],[9073,"Small businesses and solo operators"],[782,"Small coaching practices"],[4321,"Small text is the first thing to go. Nielsen Norman Group's guidance on legibility says that tiny text dooms legibility, and that text needs high contrast between characters and background. On a slide, that means fewer words in larger type, dark text on a plain light background or the reverse, and no fine print in the corners."],[466,"SMS (Beta) and WhatsApp (Beta) work alongside email. They suit the last reminder before a session, when an email may sit unread. SMS (Beta) needs the **Phone** field on your registration form, which you can add with custom registration fields. Send yourself a test before you use either with an audience."],[2653,"SMS (Beta) works only for people whose phone number you have. Set your sender name in **Email Settings** to a name buyers will recognise, as the confirmation email settings explain."],[8904,"SMS and WhatsApp (both Beta) go to the phone number from the registration form. Add the **Phone** field first."],[8868,"SMS and WhatsApp notifications, both in Beta, are set on the **Notifications** tab and go to the phone number from your registration form. Without the **Phone** field on the form, there's nobody to message. Reminder emails cover the email side."],[8362,"SMS and WhatsApp welcome messages (Beta)"],[3630,"So what should a thank-you email after a webinar say? Say thanks once, name the single idea people came for, give the replay and offer one next step. Leave out the full summary, because they were there. Only keep the reply line if the reply-to address is one somebody actually reads."],[2502,"Solo operators sometimes sell a seat rather than a product at the end. The set-up for that is in paid webinars and paid registration. A separate guide to paid seminars and workshops is planned."],[3049,"Some course creators charge for the webinar itself: a paid masterclass, a workshop, or a paid preview of the course. Choose **Paid** when you create the webinar, set the price in US dollars, and attendees pay through Stripe as they register. Turn on the **Live + Replay bundle** to sell the recording too."],[6021,"Some practical habits:"],[7776,"Some setups ask the host to press record once the session begins. It gives control over the start and end, but it fails quietly when nobody presses the button. The first minutes are often the ones lost, because that's when the host is busiest."],[409,"Some tools ship a standard set of reminder and follow-up emails. It catches timing, because the send times come built in. It misses your content: the wording is generic until you rewrite it around this webinar's topic and offer."],[7169,"Someone registers on your registration page, paying first if the webinar is paid."],[4955,"Someone who arrives at your page at 2:07 can be watching by 2:15, instead of waiting until tomorrow. The recording is the same one your other evergreen sessions play; only the start time changes."],[3434,"Source & UTM is the filter that answers which campaign brought each registrant, once UTM Params is on."],[1327,"SPF lets a domain owner name the hosts allowed to use its domain, and a receiving system can check that permission. Think of it as a published guest list. A domain can't have several competing SPF records, so a new sending service joins your existing record rather than getting a second one."],[1243,"SPF: who may send for your domain"],[1592,"Split it into attendees and no-shows in a spreadsheet."],[3413,"Split the sheet into attendees and no-shows, and send each group its own follow-up from your email tool."],[7600,"Split your attendees into those who came live, those who watched only the replay, and those who did both. This shows how much of your audience the live moment reached. Its weak spot is timing: replay numbers keep growing for days, so a count taken the next morning undercounts."],[2754,"Spot the warm leads"],[5265,"Staff the chat while the recording plays"],[7607,"Start a recording, then let attendees in."],[2865,"Start from a coaching template so the page looks finished on day one, then edit the headline into the problem you teach."],[1687,"Start here"],[1383,"Start here for every send. The notice above the list says what must be verified, and the Sending health label sits beside the default service."],[7801,"Start recording, and keep the device awake and plugged in for the whole session."],[4812,"Start the recording at each slot yourself, or present it live again."],[2169,"Start with the account the funnel lives in. A client workspace keeps that client's webinars, registrants and settings apart from yours and from your other clients. The client receives an email confirmation to set their password and join, so use an address they actually read."],[1667,"Start with webinar follow-up: one message for attendees and the replay for no-shows. It runs after every webinar and takes no effort once built. Watch it for a full cycle before adding a nurture sequence."],[7702,"Started by hand in the room"],[534,"Started from a ready-made template"],[334,"Starting from a fixed template set"],[4080,"State the one result attendees will leave with, then name your three teaching points. This is the agenda in spoken form. If you cannot write the promise as one sentence starting \"By the end you'll be able to\", the topic is still too wide."],[5842,"Stay on **Chat**, the first of the four sections (**Chat**, **Q&A**, **AI Moderation** and **Chat Simulation**)."],[8558,"Stay on the **Emails** channel and find the **Webinar Reminders** group."],[1936,"Step 1 is where the webinar type is decided, so pick Live, Simulive or Evergreen with care, because changing it later means cloning the webinar."],[6435,"Step 1: Open the clone dialog"],[6588,"Step 1: Open the create dialog"],[6766,"Step 1: Open the create dialog"],[9526,"Step 1: Read the four stats"],[6950,"Step 1: Start a new webinar"],[9527,"Step 2: Check or change the schedule"],[6436,"Step 2: Choose the type of the copy"],[6951,"Step 2: Name it and choose Evergreen"],[6767,"Step 2: Name it and choose Simulive"],[6589,"Step 2: Name your webinar and choose its type"],[6952,"Step 3: Build the schedule"],[9529,"Step 3: Manage individual sessions"],[6437,"Step 3: Name the copy"],[6768,"Step 3: Set the schedule"],[6590,"Step 3: Set your schedule"],[6596,"Step 4: Choose free or paid"],[6769,"Step 4: Choose free or paid"],[6958,"Step 4: Choose free or paid"],[6438,"Step 4: Choose what to copy"],[9530,"Step 4: Set free or paid registration"],[9531,"Step 5: Add presenters and moderators"],[6598,"Step 5: Choose a registration template"],[6770,"Step 5: Choose the recording"],[6959,"Step 5: Choose the video"],[6439,"Step 5: Create the copy"],[6960,"Step 6: Choose a template"],[6440,"Step 6: Finish a copy that changed type"],[6773,"Step 6: Pick a registration template"],[9533,"Step 6: Share the webinar"],[9534,"Step 7: Enter the room"],[2509,"Step three happens here. Create with AI drafts the webinar from a short description, while Create Manually lets you build each part yourself."],[656,"Steps 1 to 4 come from your prompt and script: the AI writes the headline, learning points and bio from them. Step 5 comes from the webinar itself, because the pages carry its schedule and the default form asks for name and email. The AI writes steps 6 and 7 in the same pass as the registration page. Step 8 stays with you: use the device toggle and **Preview**, then **Publish** each page."],[445,"Steps 1, 2, 4 and 5 come done when the AI builds the webinar: six drafts, timed, with merge tags in place. You still split the follow-ups by audience, add the emails your goal needs, and do the final read in step 7. If a format already works, you can also clone a webinar and edit its emails instead."],[3243,"Steps 3 and 5 are where most mistakes happen, because they are repeated for every webinar."],[2639,"Steps 3, 4 and 6 are where most hand-run workshops fail. A copied email is wrong, a payment arrives at night, or a forwarded link gets through."],[2186,"Steps 4 to 7 are where time goes and where errors slip in. A setting missed in step 4 shows up as a broken reminder in launch week, and an import done late in step 7 means the client's sales team calls cold leads."],[2066,"Still stuck?"],[7908,"Storage and your plan"],[8210,"Storage and your plan"],[2961,"Stream to your existing audience"],[1734,"Streaming"],[7513,"Streaming out and ending"],[9503,"Stripe. Connect your Stripe account on the pricing step or from **Integrations → Payment**."],[3118,"Style the live room: backgrounds, overlays and layout so the room matches your course brand."],[8049,"Style the page in the builder"],[1713,"Supported integrations"],[3544,"Swap in your own next step"],[6876,"Swapping the video leaves your schedule, pages and registrations as they were. That makes it safe to replace a presentation with an improved cut while sessions are already booked."],[2832,"Switch **Free** on under **Offer Price** if the next step is a free call or an application."],[6352,"Switch off the toggle on the offer's row in the **CTAs** list, or"],[6350,"Switch on **Countdown timer** to put a countdown on the offer card. It's a single toggle, set on each offer separately. Use it when the offer really does close or change at a set time, and say in the sub headline what ends."],[5689,"Switch on **Countdown timer**."],[8174,"Switch on **Enable replay page** and send the **Replay Page Link**. It's also in the **Share** dialog as **Attendee Replay link**."],[8112,"Switch on **Enable replay page**. The page then reads *Replay page is live, anyone with the link can watch*."],[4975,"Switch on **Just-in-time Registration**."],[8706,"Switch on **On Demand**."],[7028,"Switch on as many of the three viewing options as you like. Each one you enable shows on the registration page as a choice."],[2392,"Switch the offer on at the right moment during the session."],[4641,"Switch to **People** to see who registered and who attended. For a full list across all your webinars, with columns such as watch percentage, offer clicked and lead score, plus filters and export, use People in the sidebar."],[4642,"Switch to **Revenue**."],[2368,"Switching on **Countdown timer** puts a countdown on the offer card, which makes the offer feel time-limited. Only use one if the deadline is real. The countdown timers page covers how it behaves."],[2837,"Switching on **Countdown timer** puts a countdown on the offer card. Use it when something genuinely closes, such as applications for this cohort or a bonus call slot. The countdown timers page explains how it behaves on live and recorded sessions. A countdown on an offer that never closes teaches your audience to ignore the next one."],[2054,"Symptom"],[3409,"Tag each link, and keep a copy of the full address beside its channel."],[3323,"Tag every promotion link with UTM parameters"],[4502,"Tag the links in your ads, emails and posts as you normally would."],[8847,"Tag the links you share as you normally would, in ads, emails and posts."],[3326,"Tag your promotion links before you launch"],[7872,"Take a screenshot or record your screen (Chromebook Help): the screen recorder steps, audio choices and where recordings are saved."],[5682,"Take a typical case. A business coach runs an evergreen webinar that plays twice a day and ends on an offer for a twelve-week coaching programme. Anyone who enrols before their session ends gets a bonus strategy call. The coach is rarely watching, and every session has a new audience."],[2807,"Take a typical case. A career coach helps mid-career teachers move into new work, and her six-month group programme has filled through word of mouth for two years. This term, eight places are still open three weeks before the start date, and referrals have gone quiet."],[8252,"Take a typical case. A cooking instructor films a 45-minute sourdough class in her home kitchen. She wants it to run as an evergreen webinar for the 2,000 people on her newsletter, ending with a link to her paid six-week course. The class is part of what she sells, so she doesn't want it sitting free on a public channel."],[7769,"Take a typical case. A course creator sells a self-paced bookkeeping course for freelancers and runs a free live workshop on the first Monday of each month. About a third of registrants attend live, and the rest ask for the replay the next morning. She presents alone, so she watches the chat, the clock and her slides at once."],[5132,"Take a typical case. A course creator who teaches beginner woodworking runs a free live masterclass on building a first workbench. It ends with an offer for the paid course on her course platform. The session sells well, but she can only present once a month."],[6157,"Take a typical case. A customer success team at a software company runs a live release briefing for its customers every quarter. Around 300 account admins attend, and many arrive with questions about their own setup. One product manager presents, while two success managers support the session."],[8971,"Take a typical case. A dental practice group with six clinics runs a free monthly live webinar on clear aligners for prospective patients. Its marketing coordinator works alone, and the group's aligner treatment page already draws steady visitors. The goal is for those readers to sign up without leaving that page."],[603,"Take a typical case. A dog trainer who works alone runs a free 45-minute live session each month on loose-lead walking. Each session ends on an offer for her paid four-week group programme. She promotes it to her email list and in local community groups, and nothing can go out until the registration page is ready."],[1321,"Take a typical case. A family-run garden centre with two branches has 1,800 leads from its spring planting webinars. The office admin is asked to send the first broadcast on Monday, for an offer closing on Friday. She opens the CRM, writes the email, and learns that nothing can go out yet."],[5836,"Take a typical case. A financial planning firm runs a monthly live webinar for people within a few years of retirement. Around 120 people attend, and many want to ask about their own pension or savings. One planner presents, and a colleague supports the session."],[6530,"Take a typical case. A fitness coach who works alone runs a free live kick-off for her 30-day kettlebell challenge each month. About 120 people register, and each kick-off ends with an offer to join her paid coaching group."],[8829,"Take a typical case. A five-person cybersecurity consultancy runs a quarterly live briefing on ransomware readiness for IT managers at mid-sized firms. It wants each registrant's role and company size, so its two consultants follow up with the right people. It also needs consent before any marketing email goes out."],[3197,"Take a typical case. A five-person marketing team at a construction project software company runs four webinars a month. They are a product demo, an onboarding session for new customers, a partner briefing and a topical panel. Each has a different owner, and two outside guests present every month."],[401,"Take a typical case. A four-person agency runs a monthly lead-generation webinar for each of five home-improvement clients: a roofer, a window fitter, a kitchen studio and two landscapers. Every session needs a confirmation, three reminders and at least one follow-up, each in that client's voice."],[4268,"Take a typical case. A four-person customer success team at an accounting software company runs a live onboarding webinar for new customers every month. One manager hosts, and a product specialist joins as co-host to answer setup questions in the chat."],[7571,"Take a typical case. A leadership coach who works with first-time managers runs a free 60-minute live workshop each month with a business partner. They take questions from the audience, then invite people to apply for their group coaching programme."],[824,"Take a typical case. A leadership coach who works with newly promoted managers wants to run a free 40-minute live session on handling a first difficult conversation. She has the topic, three points she always teaches and a six-week group programme to offer at the end. What she doesn't have is a script, and the date is two weeks away."],[8413,"Take a typical case. A mortgage broker with a two-person office runs a free monthly webinar for first-time buyers. Around 60 people register from local ads and estate agent referrals. The broker's aim is a follow-up call with anyone ready to apply."],[5520,"Take a typical case. A novelist is launching a monthly online book club for readers of literary fiction. She records a 30-minute introduction to the club and runs it as an evergreen webinar, linked from her newsletter. She expects around 40 viewers a day, spread across several sessions."],[5322,"Take a typical case. A nutrition coach who works alone runs a free 40-minute talk on weekly meal planning every Thursday evening. Most of her new clients come from it. She has given it about thirty times. The slides haven't changed in months, and neither have the questions people ask in chat."],[1558,"Take a typical case. A one-person bike repair workshop runs a free monthly webinar on home bike maintenance, then offers a yearly servicing plan. About 300 people register each month, and fewer than half turn up. The owner wants every one of them to hear back the next morning."],[2341,"Take a typical case. A one-person home organising business sells a paid decluttering package, and every new client has asked the same questions on a discovery call. The owner wants to answer those questions once, in a free 40-minute session, and invite the room to book."],[8106,"Take a typical case. A personal-finance educator runs a free live class on clearing credit card debt, on the first Thursday of each month at 19:30. About 250 people register, many of them parents and shift workers. The class ends with an offer to join an eight-week budgeting programme."],[9283,"Take a typical case. A podcast network with six shows hosts a monthly live webinar where a guest from one show gives a talk. The network's producer runs the session and a show host interviews the guest. Promotion goes out on every show's feed and newsletter, and the guest has never used the network's webinar account."],[1012,"Take a typical case. A pottery teacher sells a self-paced glazing course on her course platform and wants an evergreen webinar to bring in buyers. She has the course and a price, but no script, no registration page and no follow-up emails."],[4782,"Take a typical case. A project-management software company's product team runs a 40-minute onboarding walkthrough for new trial users every Thursday at 15:00. A product manager presents it, and the content barely changes from week to week."],[6711,"Take a typical case. A real-estate training company with three instructors runs a live exam review for about 80 trainee agents. It meets on Tuesday and Thursday evenings for the eight weeks before each licensing exam, and it pauses on public holidays."],[9647,"Take a typical case. A regional wildlife charity has a fundraising team of three and around 900 monthly donors. Each month it runs a live 40-minute briefing for donors, with a guest speaker from its field team. The goal is to keep monthly givers engaged, so fewer cancel their gift."],[2609,"Take a typical case. A registered nutritionist runs a free monthly talk for new parents and now wants to sell a 90-minute live meal-planning workshop. She wants buyers to ask questions as she goes, and she wants to sell the recording to people who can't make the time."],[89,"Take a typical case. A six-person digital marketing agency runs monthly lead-generation webinars for four accounting firms. Two producers build the registration pages, an account manager hosts, and a junior marketer moderates the chat. Each firm expects its own leads and settings to stay private."],[5996,"Take a typical case. A small marketing agency runs a monthly live workshop on local search for owners of small businesses. About 150 people register, and the agency wants to know which of them are worth a call afterwards. The two partners present, and neither has time to watch the chat closely."],[4968,"Take a typical case. A small skincare brand sells online with a team of four, and most new visitors arrive from social ads. It has made a 20-minute recorded webinar that walks through a morning and evening routine. The goal is to turn ad clicks into first orders."],[6891,"Take a typical case. A small Spanish language school with five teachers offers a free taster lesson for adult beginners before each term. Around 40 people join each sitting, held on Saturday mornings and Wednesday evenings for the six weeks before enrolment closes."],[2483,"Take a typical case. A small travel company sells guided walking holidays and wants more bookings from people who are still deciding. A live talk once a month works, but the team can't present every evening for every time zone. What they need is a set-up that matches that job, not a list of features."],[6315,"Take a typical case. A software-training company with four instructors runs a monthly live webinar on passing a cloud architect exam. About 250 IT professionals attend. The session ends by selling a certification bundle of three practice courses and an exam voucher."],[207,"Take a typical case. A solo marketing consultant who helps local accountancy firms win clients plans her first webinar. It's a 40-minute live session on getting referrals through LinkedIn, two weeks away. She has about 400 contacts and wants to end with an invitation to her three-month advisory programme."],[2163,"Take a typical case. A three-person agency runs webinar funnels for three clients: a physiotherapy clinic, an online language tutor and a home-insulation installer. Each client wants a monthly webinar under their own name that fills their calendar. They also want a short results note at month end."],[3386,"Take a typical case. A three-person demand generation team at a freight software company runs a live webinar each quarter for warehouse operations managers. It promotes each session through a paid LinkedIn ad, a partner's newsletter and its own email list. Registrations look healthy, but nobody can say which channel sent the people who stayed to the end."],[1147,"Take a typical case. A two-person marketing team at an office furniture supplier runs a monthly webinar for facilities managers. Each session brings a few hundred registrants. Their sales lead wants to know who attended, who asked for a quote and who should get a call."],[4055,"Take a typical case. A two-person product marketing team at a project-management software company is planning its first quarterly webinar for operations managers who are evaluating tools. They have a strong topic, a 45-minute slot and a free trial to offer at the end. They also have three weeks to build everything."],[7441,"Take a typical case. A university admissions office runs a live open evening each term for prospective postgraduate students. Two admissions officers present, a current student joins for the last twenty minutes, and around 200 applicants from several countries register."],[3600,"Take a typical case. A watercolour teacher sells a six-week online course, and this morning she ran a free live class on mixing skin tones. She closed with an offer, and enrolment stays open until Friday. It's now lunchtime, and she still has to write her follow-ups."],[9110,"Take a typical case. A wedding photography educator runs a free live workshop on posing couples each month, with one assistant helping her. Each workshop feeds a small paid mentoring group, and her goal is about 150 signups from Instagram and her email list."],[8552,"Take a typical case. A yoga teacher-training school runs a free evergreen info session for people thinking about its 200-hour course. The session plays several times a week, and people register from the school's Instagram. Most sign up on their phones, often days before the session they pick."],[7236,"Take a typical case. An employment law firm runs a quarterly live panel for HR directors on new workplace rules. Two partners host, and two guest speakers join from outside the firm. Around 180 people attend, and the session is recorded for clients who miss it."],[8699,"Take a typical case. An HR software vendor sells leave and payroll tools to companies with 50 to 200 staff. Its marketing team of three records a 15-minute explainer on a change to holiday pay rules and links it from a monthly newsletter. The aim is to get HR managers to book a product demo afterwards."],[1910,"Take a typical case. An independent bookkeeper who works with restaurant owners runs a free live talk each month, and new clients book after it. Between talks, people still land on her site and find nothing to watch, so she asks what kind of platform could run the talk live and then keep it running."],[4462,"Take a typical case. An independent insurance agency with a six-person sales team runs a fortnightly live webinar on choosing life cover for young families. About 180 people register through social ads and a partner newsletter. Each session ends with an offer to book a quote call."],[7985,"Take a typical case. The operations team at a franchise with 40 bakery outlets runs a live training session each month for new store managers. It covers opening checks, food safety and the till system. Managers who join later need the same training, usually in their first week."],[2998,"Take a typical example. A course creator opens enrolment with a live webinar in launch week, presenting to an email list and answering questions in the chat. The launch closes, but new subscribers keep arriving. The creator wants that same pitch to enrol them without presenting it every week."],[3988,"Teach first, offer at the end"],[2747,"Teach first, then invite the next step"],[2425,"Teach one useful thing first, then make one offer that follows from it. Show the offer card when the teaching reaches the problem your product solves. Use a countdown only when the deadline is real."],[2917,"Teach the problem your best clients had before they found you, then invite people to book a call or apply. Follow up attendees, no-shows and offer clickers separately, and run the session again on a schedule once it works."],[2875,"Teach the session, then paste your booking link into the chat."],[2556,"Teach the session: chat, Q&A and polls"],[2636,"Teach, keeping one person on chat and questions if you can."],[9234,"Team & Moderators"],[1993,"Team and account"],[106,"Team and client access, from each platform's public documentation:"],[1,"Team members and roles"],[7195,"Team members and roles"],[1866,"Team members and roles: give the colleagues who manage your integrations their own login."],[3307,"Team members and roles: the full steps for adding colleagues and changing their roles."],[2086,"Team members and roles: who joins as a co-host or moderator, and how they get in."],[9532,"Team members are a different thing"],[78,"Team members are one of three ways to bring people in:"],[82,"Team members let colleagues work in your account under their own names. Each person signs in separately, so nobody passes a password around. The role you pick decides whether they can reach billing and settings."],[10,"Team members, presenters and moderators"],[9349,"Team members: logins for colleagues, as opposed to join links for guests."],[9697,"Team members: when to give someone a login instead of a join link."],[3190,"Team webinar management is the set of habits and settings that lets several people run webinars every month without stepping on each other. It covers who has access, who owns each webinar, and how a proven setup gets reused."],[1739,"Teams running several webinars"],[1105,"Teams that already have a CRM"],[8970,"Teams that already run websites and blogs want registration to sit inside the content that brings in traffic. It matters most to whoever owns the website, at the point a webinar is announced. That's when a form that drops into an existing page saves building a new one."],[6529,"Teams that run webinars as a programme reuse what works. Copying a proven webinar, or moving it from live to automated, without a rebuild is what makes that practical."],[1883,"Teams, agencies and security"],[839,"Tell people how the session works before you start teaching."],[7709,"Tell people they are being recorded"],[4915,"Test a just-in-time session"],[4215,"Test AI answers before anyone sees them"],[4212,"Test as an attendee, not only as the host"],[8503,"Text and WhatsApp reminders (Beta)"],[8779,"Text reminders (Beta)"],[3749,"Thank people once, name the one idea they came for, share the replay link and offer a single next step. Keep it short, because attendees don't need a summary of a session they just watched."],[853,"Thank people, repeat the next step once, and tell them what happens after the session."],[9231,"Thank You Page Link"],[3533,"Thank-you email after a webinar"],[4454,"That changes what you can do after a session. Instead of knowing that a large share of registrants missed the webinar, you can see who they are, narrow the list to one webinar, and export it for a replay email. Instead of knowing that some people clicked your offer, you can find the ones who watched most of the session, clicked, and still haven't bought."],[2890,"That is the case for running the session on repeat. Four live runs take four evenings of your time, but an evergreen version can reach the same number of registrants while you coach. Swap in your own rates after your first live run, because they will differ."],[1911,"That is the job EasyWebinar is built for. A host builds a webinar with a registration page, emails, polls and offers. It runs live or from a recording, and the host follows up through analytics, integrations or the built-in EasyCRM. The short version of its approach is to run a webinar live, find the version that converts, then turn that session into an automated webinar that runs every day."],[3388,"That measure misses the point of a webinar. A registration that never turns up gives sales nothing to follow up. A smaller channel, meanwhile, can send people who watch most of the session and click the offer. The useful question is which campaign produced attendees and interest, not just form fills."],[392,"That tab sorts every email into five groups: **Registration Confirmation**, **Webinar Reminders**, **Webinar Follow-ups**, **Attendee Action Follow-ups** and **Paid Offer — Transaction Success**. Each entry shows its trigger, its audience and its subject line, so you can scan the whole sequence on one screen."],[7559,"That's the plain meaning of the term, and it answers the common question of whether a webinar is live. Some are and some aren't. A live webinar has a person presenting in real time. A simulive or evergreen webinar plays a recording, even when it looks like a broadcast."],[7083,"The **AI Moderator** answers attendee questions from documents you upload and keeps the chat civil. It is in Beta. It's aimed at large webinars and automated ones running without you."],[5007,"The **AI Moderator**, in Beta, answers attendee questions from documents you upload and keeps the chat civil. On an automated webinar running at 3am, it can answer a question that would otherwise wait for someone on your team."],[4506,"The **Analytics** tab on a single webinar shows the funnel and offer numbers; People shows the individuals across all webinars. Use Analytics to find where a webinar is leaking, then People to find who is in that gap. See Webinar analytics."],[2870,"The **Analytics** tab shows how many people visited, registered, attended live, watched the replay and bought. Its offers panel shows each offer's views, clicks and purchases, with a click rate. The webinar analytics guide explains each figure."],[9001,"The **Clone Webinar** dialog lists **Embed widgets** under **Webinar assets**, alongside registration pages, the webinar schedule and replay settings. Leave it ticked to copy the widget into the new webinar."],[5121,"The **Clone Webinar** dialog opens from the webinar's card. Its **Cloning from** section shows the original's type, date and registration count. **Clone into** shows the type the copy will be, and **Choose webinar type** offers Live, Simulive or Evergreen."],[4383,"The **Continue to Green Room** button stays greyed out until the room can see both a camera and a microphone. Allow both in your browser, pick them on the device check, and try again."],[6300,"The **CTAs** tab holds two kinds of call to action. A poll asks your audience something. An offer asks them to do something: buy, book, enrol, download."],[1798,"The **Custom fields** tab sends webinar details into your CRM's own fields: \"Choose which of your CRM's custom fields should receive each piece of webinar data.\""],[7277,"The **Design** panel sets the background, a frame overlay and the stage layout. Layout matters most when several presenters are on stage or someone is sharing a screen."],[4312,"The **End of Session** and **Expired Session** boxes on the **General** tab are empty by default. Left empty, attendees sit on a finished webinar with nowhere to go. Paste your offer, booking page or thank-you page into **End of Session**, do the same for **Expired Session**, and click **Save Changes**."],[4390,"The **End of Session** box is empty by default, so attendees stay on a finished webinar. Paste your next-step page into it on the **General** tab before the session and click **Save Changes**."],[5877,"The **EW Contact Box** is a built-in question and contact form for attendees, and it works even when chat is off. **No Chat** plus a pinned contact box suits a presentation you don't want interrupted but still want questions from."],[7958,"The **Files** tab is also where the AI Moderator's documents live. The AI Moderator is in **Beta**: it answers attendee questions from documents you give it, reading **PDF, DOCX or PPTX** files up to **25MB** each, and it retrains every time you add a source."],[2214,"The **Lead score** column on **People** shows how engaged each person was, for example Hot, and it is calculated from engagement. Behaviour-based scoring starts at the Growth plan. Sort by it, and send the hottest names to the client's sales team first."],[4603,"The **Overview** view is built around a funnel. People arrive at the registration page, some register, some of those attend live or watch the replay, and some never show up. Below the funnel sit the numbers for what happened during the session: chat, questions, and how your offers performed."],[4604,"The **People** view lists who registered and who attended. The **Revenue** view breaks down what the webinar earned, separating paid registrations from offers sold in the room."],[3720,"The **People** view shows the individuals behind those numbers. Filter by **Behavior → no-show** for one webinar to see who still needs the replay. Filter by high watch time plus offer clicked to find people who were interested and didn't buy, then check their **Replay watch percentage** before you write to them again."],[3390,"The **People** view shows this in practice. It lists every registrant and attendee across all your webinars, and its **Source & UTM** filter shows the campaign behind each one. How other webinar platforms record registrant source is covered in How EasyWebinar compares for generating leads with webinars, below."],[1825,"The **Phone Number** field only has something to send if your registration form collected it. Select the form block in the page builder and add the **Phone** field; custom registration fields covers the other fields you can add."],[4509,"The **Phone** column fills only if your form collects it. Add the **Phone** field, or your own fields, on the registration form block. See Custom fields."],[6902,"The **Plan** tab, under **Settings** → **Manage Plan**, counts people watching simulive and evergreen sessions on its **Automated attendees** meter, separately from **Live attendees**."],[6856,"The **Recordings** filter is where earlier webinars show up. If you ran a session live with recording on, its recording can become the content of this simulive webinar."],[8445,"The **Registered** metric in a webinar's Analytics counts unique sign-ups, which is the number of people your confirmation should have reached. The **People** view lists each registrant with a **Registered on** column. See Webinar analytics and People."],[2652,"The **Registration Confirmation** email goes out the moment someone registers and carries their join link, so leave it on. Add webinar reminder emails on the **Notifications** tab, each with its own timing before the start. A common pattern is three: a day before, an hour before and fifteen minutes before."],[8388,"The **Registration Confirmation** email is the first message a new registrant gets from your webinar. EasyWebinar sends it immediately after someone registers, and it carries their attendee join link, which makes it the email that actually gets people into the session. It sits on the webinar's **Notifications** tab, and the **Email Settings** button on the same screen decides whose name your emails show, where replies go, and whether an admin address receives a copy of each registration."],[9163,"The **Registration Page Link** in the Share dialog is the published page's address, and the **Registration Page** button on the webinar header opens the live page."],[8950,"The **Registration Widget** lets people register for your webinar without leaving your own website. On the webinar's **Content** tab you copy its **Embed Code** and paste it into your page wherever the form should appear: a landing page, a blog post, a sidebar. The same snippet is in the **Share** dialog, and **View & Customize** lets you change how the widget looks before you copy it."],[9162,"The **Registration Widget** on the Content tab gives you embed code to put the form on your own page instead of sending people to the EasyWebinar page."],[8722,"The **Replay** tab controls a separate page for people who missed a session, with availability options of **Always Available**, **Expire after days** or **Specific date**, and an **Experience** of **Webinar Replica** or **Custom Build**. Those settings belong to the replay page, not to On Demand."],[2667,"The **Replay** tab controls the page itself. **Always Available** keeps it up with no expiry, which suits training that keeps its value. Once the page is on, it reads that anyone with the link can watch, so treat the link as public and share it with care."],[8092,"The **Replay** tab holds everything about the page people use to catch up on a past session. It has five parts:"],[6378,"The **Revenue** view splits earnings under **Revenue by Source**, separating paid registrations from offers sold in the room. Offer sales show under **Paid Offer**."],[9608,"The **Sessions** panel lists every sitting of the webinar under its own tab:"],[7275,"The **Share** dialog on the webinar holds seven links, including the presenter and moderator ones above. **Shorten URL's** gives you shorter versions. See Sharing links."],[1829,"The **Social media** category on the Integrations tab is separate from CRMs. To broadcast a webinar to YouTube or LinkedIn while it runs, use EasyCast, under **Integrations** in the sidebar."],[9304,"The **Team & Moderators** panel on the Overview is the other way to bring people into a webinar: **Add presenter** adds someone who appears on camera and presents, and **Invite** sends an invitation to join the webinar team. A join link suits a guest on one session; adding someone to your account team suits people who work in EasyWebinar regularly."],[8439,"The **Thank you Page** is one of the five pages on the **Content** tab and appears straight after registering. On a paid webinar, it shows the attendee's **Webinar join link** with a **Join Room** button, and the session **Date** with **Add to Calendar** buttons for Google, Outlook and Apple. The page and the email cover the same moment from two sides: one in the browser, one in the inbox."],[5385,"The **Video Selection** step lists your library. The **All**, **Videos** and **Recordings** filters switch between uploaded videos and recordings of past webinars, and search finds a video by name."],[6875,"The **Webinar Video** panel on the **Content** tab shows the selected recording and its length. **Preview** plays it back, and **Change Video** swaps it for another."],[1548,"The **Workflows** screen opens from **Marketing → Campaign**. Across the top are four status tabs, **All**, **Published**, **Draft** and **Archived**, each with a count, so you can see at a glance how many sequences are live. The **Create Workflow** button starts a new one. The screen is shown in the set-up steps below."],[3616,"The 2008 study's guidance is to start with what matters most to the reader. It warns that email opening with marketing risks being deleted before people reach what they need. A person who registered came for the session, so the replay link sits above the offer in these templates."],[454,"The agency's job shifts from writing 35 emails to reviewing them. It writes just the five no-show follow-ups itself, and the full builds cost far more credits than the emails do."],[1040,"The AI badge marks a generated webinar, but its settings and tabs match a webinar you built by hand."],[899,"The AI badge marks a webinar that started from a generated script, and every part of it stays editable on its usual tab."],[391,"The AI build runs as a series of steps: script, pages, emails, polls and offers. On the emails step, the six drafts are listed with when each one sends, and **Add email** sits below them. After the build, the same emails appear on the webinar's **Notifications** tab, grouped by kind."],[594,"The AI covers three of the five pages every webinar has: the registration page, the thank-you page and the waiting area. The other two, the webinar page and the replay page, keep the working default that every page has. All five open in the same drag-and-drop builder, so you can style the last two whenever you're ready."],[238,"The AI drafts a full sequence:"],[1041,"The AI drafts land in the same places as your own work. Pages open in the normal builder, emails on the Notifications tab, and polls and offers under CTAs. That means you can follow up on attendees with your usual follow-up emails and read results in webinar analytics."],[412,"The AI drafts this sequence for every webinar it builds:"],[6232,"The AI Moderator is in Beta. In **Suggest Answer** mode it drafts answers for you to approve; in **Answer directly** mode it answers without approval."],[6195,"The AI Moderator, in Beta, has a **Q&A Moderation** screen. With **Suggest Answer** it drafts an answer for you to approve before attendees see it; with **Answer directly** it answers without host approval. It doesn't replace a human moderator on a live webinar."],[7995,"The AI Moderator's knowledge base documents live on the **Files** tab. Because the moderator retrains with each new source, give it a moment after an upload before you test it."],[9154,"The AI then writes your registration page, thank-you page and waiting area, using the script as source material: headline, what you'll learn, instructor bio and the rest. The pages open in an editor with **Edit**, **Preview**, **SEO** and **Scripts** tabs, and a desktop and mobile view. Treat the output as a strong first draft; afterwards, pages open in the normal builder."],[1042,"The AI works with all three webinar types. You can create a live webinar, a simulive webinar or an evergreen webinar this way. The webinars overview explains the differences."],[705,"The AI writes the registration page, thank-you page and waiting area. The webinar page and replay page keep their defaults until you edit them."],[614,"The AI writes three pages and leaves two on their defaults. Here's what each page is for and what to look at once the build is done."],[1019,"The AI writes your registration page, thank-you page and waiting area, using the script as its source. The pages open in the normal editor, so you can change any line. The AI landing page guide explains the editor tabs."],[237,"The AI writes your registration page, thank-you page and waiting area, using the script as source material: headline, what attendees will learn, an instructor bio and the rest. The pages open in an editor with **Edit**, **Preview**, **SEO** and **Scripts** tabs and a desktop and mobile view. Treat them as a strong first draft. Click **Continue**."],[674,"The AI writes your three pages and opens them in the editor. Switch between desktop and mobile, read every line and change what's wrong. Then click **Continue**."],[1222,"The allowance resets on your signup date, not the first of the month. The activity log marks rows outside the current cycle as **Not counted**."],[4602,"The Analytics tab is the per-webinar report. It answers two questions: how many people got through each stage, from landing on the registration page to watching, and where the others stopped."],[9452,"The attendee lands on a Stripe checkout page showing your business name and the webinar as the item. They can pay by card, or by Apple Pay or Link where available. Their email is carried over from the registration form, so they don't type it twice."],[5472,"The attendee list"],[3925,"The average open rate lands on the 21% benchmark, and the average click rate beats 2.6%. But the second send pulled both averages down, which is why you note each send yourself. The two sends also use 1,600 emails, more than the 1,000 included each month, so plan them around your Wallet reset date."],[4824,"The blackout dates cut the week's set sessions by 40%, so each remaining session gets busier. Turning on On Demand spreads that load and takes the waiting out for a large share of people. Turning on just-in-time would do the same for those who still want a session."],[5181,"The bottom of the dialog confirms the new name and its type. Click **Clone Webinar** to create the copy."],[3997,"The bridge"],[4082,"The bridge is the two or three minutes between teaching and selling. Sum up what attendees can now do alone, then name what is still hard. Your offer should close exactly that gap. Without a bridge, the pitch feels like a change of subject."],[1822,"The briefing itself doesn't need to change. What changes is how much of each person's session reaches the CRM, and how fast. An attendee who stayed to the end is a different lead from one who left after ten minutes, and the CRM should know that."],[9649,"The briefing itself rarely goes wrong. The risk sits in the handovers between people and tools before it starts. Fewer places to look means fewer chances to miss a detail."],[3961,"The Broadcasts screen sets your average open rate beside a 21% industry benchmark, and your average click rate beside 2.6%. Those figures are averages across all your broadcasts, so note each send's totals yourself."],[707,"The build doesn't set your schedule, and for simulive or evergreen webinars it doesn't choose the video."],[460,"The build drops you on the finished webinar's Overview. It carries an **AI** badge in My Webinars and behaves exactly like a webinar built by hand. You still need to set the schedule, and for simulive or evergreen, choose a video."],[461,"The build ends on the finished webinar, where the drafted email sequence is already attached and waiting on the Notifications tab for your review."],[9052,"The builder controls"],[7624,"The builder has four steps and takes about two minutes. Any team member can create a webinar, and you only need a connected Stripe account if you plan to charge."],[6658,"The builder opens on **Step 1 of 4: Build**."],[7386,"The button stays greyed out until the room can see a camera and a microphone. If it can't open your camera, you'll see **\"Not able to access selected media device\"**. Allow camera access in your browser and close any other app that's using the camera. Camera not detected walks through the fixes."],[7250,"The button stays greyed out until the room can see a camera and a microphone. If you see **\"Not able to access selected media device\"**, see Camera not detected."],[4337,"The button stays greyed out until the room can see both a camera and a microphone. You need both to enter, even if you turn them off afterwards. If your camera isn't found, the camera not detected page walks through the fixes."],[2618,"The buyer pays and registers in one step, and only buyers receive the join link. This catches the biggest risk, which is paid buyers who never get in. It misses anyone who wants to try before they buy, so it suits a known audience or a second, deeper session."],[6317,"The buying decision hasn't changed, but the moment it happens matters more. Attendees decide while the instructor is still talking, so the next step needs to sit on screen right then. It also helps to count each step between seeing the bundle and paying for it."],[3244,"The calendar and the owner's name stay with you. Everything else changes: each person gets their own login under **Team**, guests get a join link, **Clone Webinar** copies the setup, **People** holds every registrant in one list, and **Archive** clears old webinars from view."],[3665,"The CAN-SPAM Act requires a commercial email to carry a \"clear and conspicuous notice\" that people can decline further messages. Once someone asks, you have 10 business days to stop sending them commercial email. Put a visible unsubscribe line at the foot of every follow-up that pitches something."],[3884,"The CAN-SPAM Act requires commercial email to carry a clear notice that people can decline further email, and \"a valid physical postal address of the sender\". It also bars a subject heading likely to \"mislead a recipient\". The template above leaves room for both lines."],[5471,"The Chat setting"],[7779,"The check takes seconds, and the green room is the place to do it, because nothing there is broadcast."],[8854,"The checkbox is a form option. What it asks for, and how you use the data afterwards, is yours to decide."],[1354,"The choice usually comes down to what you already run. If your company has an SES, SendGrid or Mailgun account that someone manages, connect it. If you have no provider account, start from the default row and leave provider work for later. Whichever you select, the verification notice still applies, so choosing a service never replaces verifying your identity."],[7570,"The choice usually falls to the person who presents: a coach, a consultant, a marketer or a trainer. It matters most before a launch, a first run of a new talk, or any session sold on access to the presenter. It also matters when you're deciding whether to rely on a recording at all."],[8108,"The class itself doesn't need to change. The people who missed it need the same session, with the same offer, and a clear point where the chance ends. The replay should finish where the live class finished: on the next step."],[2175,"The client signs up and invites you in. Their data is cleanly theirs, and when the relationship ends they keep everything. What it misses is control: you depend on each client to manage billing, and you log in and out of several accounts with no single place to see them all."],[4137,"The conclusion is that a small overrun in each teaching point removes the whole question section. Give each teaching point a hard end time in the outline, and treat the questions as fixed time rather than whatever is left over."],[8406,"The confirmation does two jobs at once. It tells the registrant that signing up worked, and it hands them the join link they need later. EasyWebinar's guidance calls it the one email you shouldn't turn off. Every other step of getting someone into the room depends on that link."],[8404,"The confirmation is the only one of the five tied to the moment of sign-up. Reminders count down to the session, follow-ups react to what happened in it, and the transaction email answers a purchase. The confirmation answers a registration."],[8438,"The confirmation starts the email sequence and **Webinar Reminders** carry it to the session. Reminders share the same **Email Settings**, so the sender a registrant learns from the confirmation is the one they see again before the webinar. See Reminder emails."],[8581,"The confirmation starts the sequence and carries the join link; reminders bring the registrant back to it. Both are on the same tab and share the same sender settings."],[611,"The copy describes your actual session, because it's drawn from the script for the talk. That catches the gap between what the webinar covers and what the page promises. What it can miss are facts the AI may get wrong or can't know, such as the price, what's included and anything you changed after the script was written."],[5182,"The copy needs a video, and the recording of the original live session is in your library. On the video step, filter the picker with **Recordings**, click the session, and continue."],[5123,"The copy's name is pre-filled with the original's name and \"(Copy)\". Change it to something you will recognise in your list, such as the topic plus \"evergreen\"."],[3222,"The copy's name is pre-filled with the original's name and \"(Copy)\". Rename it before you click **Clone Webinar**, using a pattern the whole team follows, such as format and month. Four \"(Copy)\" webinars in one list is how the wrong one gets edited."],[5366,"The cost is that you're still present for every session, only to press play. If you miss one, the session simply doesn't happen."],[3675,"The cost is time on the day you can least spare it, and the lists start to age the moment you export them. Someone who watches the replay this evening still gets tomorrow's \"you missed it\" email."],[9054,"The countdown block"],[5719,"The countdown is part of an offer, so everything about the offer still applies: its price, its button and where that button goes. Point the button at the checkout for exactly the offer the timer describes."],[4617,"The counts are built to avoid double counting. **Visitors** counts unique IPs on the landing page. **Total Attended** counts each person once and files them under **Live only**, **Replay only** or **Both**. How other platforms report on a single webinar is covered in How webinar analytics compares, below."],[5466,"The CSV format"],[1135,"The dashboard is the first screen when you open EasyCRM. Four headline numbers run across the top: total leads, active clients, open deals and revenue. Each carries a comparison, such as new leads this week or deals converted this month, so you see direction as well as totals. Charts below show the revenue trend, deal outcomes and your pipeline."],[1136,"The dashboard is where you check whether last month's webinars turned into deals. The figures shown are sample data."],[1615,"The dashboard is where you land in EasyCRM, and Marketing in its sidebar leads to your workflows. The figures shown are sample data."],[6379,"The date range at the top of Analytics applies to everything on the page, so widen it if an offer looks emptier than expected."],[4204,"The day before"],[4205,"The day itself"],[3534,"The day-after email for attendees"],[3538,"The day-after email for no-shows"],[4206,"The days before: confirm and set"],[8717,"The deciding question is whether the webinar is valued for its content or for the occasion."],[8716,"The default *Watch instant replay* works, but the word \"replay\" can suggest a recording of something people missed. If your page sells the webinar as fresh content, wording that says what happens next, such as starting the training now, can read more naturally. Whatever you choose, keep it short enough to fit a button."],[6700,"The default form collects **Full name** and **Email**, both required. Use **Add Custom Field** to collect more on the form."],[7077,"The demo is the same every week, so the presenter is not what makes each session work. Once it is recorded, sessions can repeat by themselves. The wait can then shrink to minutes for anyone who registers between Tuesdays."],[4336,"The device check comes first, and the button to the green room stays greyed out until a camera and microphone are found."],[2077,"The device check shows your camera preview and microphone, and **Continue to Green Room** becomes clickable. Nothing in the green room is broadcast; the header reads **Not Live** until you click **Go Live**."],[2285,"The direct pitch"],[420,"The drafts also leave out the **Attendee Action Follow-ups** and **Paid Offer — Transaction Success** groups. Those depend on what each attendee does, so you add them yourself after the build."],[423,"The drafts are a strong first version, not a finished one. Read each email in order, as a registrant would receive it, and check these points."],[966,"The EasyWebinar approach to AI for webinars"],[531,"The EasyWebinar approach to AI webinar landing pages"],[4712,"The EasyWebinar approach to automated webinars"],[5460,"The EasyWebinar approach to chat simulation"],[6448,"The EasyWebinar approach to cloning"],[2740,"The EasyWebinar approach to coaching webinars"],[8353,"The EasyWebinar approach to confirmation emails"],[5633,"The EasyWebinar approach to countdown timers"],[147,"The EasyWebinar approach to Create with AI"],[1728,"The EasyWebinar approach to CRM mapping"],[8772,"The EasyWebinar approach to custom fields"],[5062,"The EasyWebinar approach to evergreen webinars"],[6969,"The EasyWebinar approach to evergreen webinars"],[4908,"The EasyWebinar approach to just-in-time"],[5775,"The EasyWebinar approach to live chat"],[6606,"The EasyWebinar approach to live webinar scheduling"],[7501,"The EasyWebinar approach to live webinars"],[6248,"The EasyWebinar approach to offers"],[8640,"The EasyWebinar approach to On Demand"],[9360,"The EasyWebinar approach to paid webinars"],[2538,"The EasyWebinar approach to paid workshops and seminars"],[5939,"The EasyWebinar approach to polls"],[6103,"The EasyWebinar approach to Q&A"],[8920,"The EasyWebinar approach to registration embeds"],[8496,"The EasyWebinar approach to reminder emails"],[8044,"The EasyWebinar approach to replay pages"],[2946,"The EasyWebinar approach to selling courses"],[9222,"The EasyWebinar approach to sharing links"],[5257,"The EasyWebinar approach to simulated live webinars"],[6782,"The EasyWebinar approach to simulive webinars"],[18,"The EasyWebinar approach to team members"],[7186,"The EasyWebinar approach to the green room"],[7902,"The EasyWebinar approach to the Library"],[7338,"The EasyWebinar approach to the live room"],[9543,"The EasyWebinar approach to the Overview tab"],[4410,"The EasyWebinar approach to the People view"],[9048,"The EasyWebinar approach to the registration page builder"],[8200,"The EasyWebinar approach to video hosting"],[4557,"The EasyWebinar approach to webinar analytics"],[1091,"The EasyWebinar approach to webinar CRM"],[1241,"The EasyWebinar approach to webinar CRM email sending"],[330,"The EasyWebinar approach to webinar email sequences"],[3522,"The EasyWebinar approach to webinar follow-up emails"],[3781,"The EasyWebinar approach to webinar invitation emails"],[3320,"The EasyWebinar approach to webinar lead generation"],[3128,"The EasyWebinar approach to webinar management"],[1491,"The EasyWebinar approach to webinar marketing automation"],[3985,"The EasyWebinar approach to webinar planning"],[7699,"The EasyWebinar approach to webinar recording"],[746,"The EasyWebinar approach to webinar script writing"],[4194,"The EasyWebinar approach to webinar setup checklists"],[2097,"The EasyWebinar approach to white label webinar funnels"],[2276,"The EasyWebinar approach to your first sales webinar"],[719,"The editor has a desktop and mobile view, and the builder has a device toggle. Check the mobile layout before you publish, because most registrations tend to come from phones."],[8345,"The email EasyWebinar sends the moment someone registers, carrying their join link, and the Email Settings that control the sender name, reply-to address and admin copies."],[970,"The emails"],[3682,"The emails live on each webinar's **Notifications** tab, and the replay settings live on its **Replay** tab. Set the replay first, because your emails depend on it."],[8441,"The emails step of **Create with AI** drafts a full email sequence, beginning with a welcome email on registration and followed by reminders and follow-ups. After the build, the emails sit on the **Notifications** tab like any others. See Create a webinar with AI."],[3041,"The enrolment offer is a card with a headline and a button, shown in the room when you're ready."],[1824,"The events table under Map events to tags lists all eleven triggers, including **Left early** and **Stayed on webinar at least**. How other platforms tag behaviour and send webinar data to a CRM is covered in How CRM mapping compares, below."],[1716,"The events you can trigger on"],[8701,"The explainer has no live element that needs everyone together. Its value is the content, so the timing matters only to the reader. When a manager has fifteen spare minutes now, that is the moment to watch."],[4804,"The first is **show-up rate**: attendees divided by registrants. It shows whether your session times suit people. Its weak spot is that on-demand viewers start at once, so a high on-demand share can make the scheduled sessions look stronger or weaker than they are. Compare scheduled and on-demand viewers separately."],[4350,"The first is whether every message arrived. Count the confirmation and reminders your colleague received against the number you set up. If you set three reminders and two arrived, look at the timing of the missing one before the day. This only tells you about one inbox, so it can't show whether messages land in other people's spam folders."],[663,"The first rate is the page's job and the second belongs to the reminders. If her 15% looked low against her own past sessions, she'd edit the headline, the form or the promise. If attendance looked low instead, she'd change her reminder emails and leave the page alone."],[3591,"The first two rows and the day-after rows follow the same timing as the follow-ups an AI-built webinar drafts. The rest are sensible starting points, so move them to fit your own deadline. If you have to send today, go straight to Write them in this order, further down."],[262,"The flow ends on the new webinar's **Overview**. From here it works exactly like a webinar built by hand."],[2850,"The follow-up email templates give wording for attendees, no-shows and offer clickers. If your emails include a sales message, the US CAN-SPAM rules require a clear notice of how to opt out and a valid postal address."],[3456,"The follow-up lists come from the same numbers. Taking 180 attendees from 450 registrants leaves 270 no-shows, the largest single group for the replay email. The 21 people who clicked the offer are the first calls for sales."],[9055,"The form block"],[9161,"The form block is where you collect more than name and email."],[8815,"The form block's settings add four things on top of that:"],[8869,"The form is one block on the registration page. Everything else on the page, from the template to the countdown and page-wide settings, is covered in Registration page builder."],[9139,"The form starts with **Full name** and **Email**, both required. Selecting the form block lets you add a **Phone** field and your own custom fields, turn on **GDPR Compliance** and **UTM Params**, and restyle the submit button. Each of these is covered under Custom fields, consent and UTM capture below."],[2649,"The form starts with **Full name** and **Email**. On the **Content** tab you can add a **Phone** field, your own custom registration fields, and **GDPR Compliance**, which adds a consent checkbox you word yourself and can make mandatory."],[9654,"The four stats are a summary of what the **Analytics** tab shows in detail. A brand-new webinar shows zeros on both until registrations start."],[1046,"The full funnel is covered in selling online courses with webinars, and the AI webinar builder overview shows the AI on the main site."],[2641,"The full pricing walkthrough lives on paid webinars and paid registration. This section covers only the choices a workshop host has to get right."],[3681,"The full steps are in the next section."],[4981,"The full walkthrough, including pricing, video selection and templates, is in Create an evergreen webinar."],[2211,"The funnel doesn't end when the webinar does. What happens in the next day decides whether the client sees leads or just a list."],[2398,"The funnel shows which step lost the most people, so you know what to fix first. These figures are sample data for a demo brand."],[277,"The generated sequence lives on the **Notifications** tab with every other email, where you can change subjects, content and timing. See Confirmation emails and Reminder emails."],[7177,"The green room and backstage"],[7508,"The green room and backstage"],[7684,"The green room and backstage"],[7875,"The green room and backstage: where you check the recording state before you broadcast."],[950,"The green room and backstage: where you get ready before going live."],[4338,"The green room header reads **Not Live**, and nothing here reaches your audience. Check your camera framing, test your screen share with **Share**, and get your co-hosts in place before you press anything."],[7219,"The green room is the private state of the EasyWebinar live room before a broadcast starts. You and your team can test cameras, microphones and screen sharing and arrange the stage, while the header reads **Not Live** and nothing reaches attendees. Backstage is the waiting area inside the room: co-presenters and moderators arrive there, off stage, and appear to the audience only when you move them on."],[7388,"The green room is the room as it will look, with nothing broadcast. Check your framing, test **Share**, and get your co-hosts in place."],[7562,"The green room is where a live webinar starts. Everything you check here stays private, so test your share and framing before you press Go Live."],[7229,"The green room separates getting ready from being seen. Live presenting has a few things that can go wrong in the first minute, such as a muted microphone or the wrong window shared. The green room lets you find them while nobody is watching."],[9285,"The guest doesn't need an account; they need a way into the room for one evening. Listeners don't need a different page; they need a link short enough for show notes. Newsletter subscribers the network already knows shouldn't have to fill in a form again."],[7409,"The header now shows **LIVE** with a timer of how long you've been broadcasting, the recording badge changes to **Recording**, and **Go Live** becomes **End Session**. If you're streaming with EasyCast, a platform icon appears for each destination. Click it to open the stream on that platform and see what viewers there are seeing."],[7593,"The header shows **Auto Recording Enabled** when recording is on, and the badge changes to **Recording** once you're live. To change it, open **Settings** and then **Recording Settings**."],[4344,"The header shows **Auto Recording Enabled** when recording is on. To change it, or to check your devices and moderation, click **Settings**."],[6344,"The headline and sub headline do the selling, so give each a job:"],[4090,"The housekeeping slide sits in section 1 and answers the questions people would otherwise type into chat. Keep it to four lines:"],[1807,"The integration shows under **Connected apps** as **Ready to use**."],[1164,"The interesting ones get a **Deal** and move through your pipeline."],[3901,"The invitation needs only the registration link from this dialog; keep the presenter and moderator links out of anything you send your list."],[1323,"The job itself hasn't changed. Mail has to leave under a name that belongs to the business, and someone has to read the replies. That work only needs doing once if it lives where the broadcasts and workflows live. Every later send can then rely on it, so a busy admin doesn't repeat it."],[6532,"The kick-off is the same each month apart from the date. She also wants a version that runs without her, for people who find her between challenges. Both needs call for copying the setup rather than redoing it."],[3611,"The last row needs care. A fixed date can't be true for everyone when people watch an evergreen webinar on different days. Set the replay to **Expire after days**, then write \"in three days\" in your email rather than a date."],[3535,"The last-call email for attendees"],[3539,"The last-call email for no-shows"],[3542,"The left-early email"],[6893,"The lesson itself barely changes from one sitting to the next. What differs is the questions people ask afterwards about levels and class times. So the teaching can be recorded once, and the teacher's time can move into the chat, while learners still start together."],[6788,"The Library"],[7883,"The Library"],[8207,"The Library"],[6451,"The Library and recordings"],[7973,"The Library is the account-wide store for everything you upload or record. Simulive and evergreen webinars get their content from it, finished live sessions end up in it, and your storage allowance is spent there."],[7978,"The Library is the shared store behind your webinars. It is where simulive and evergreen webinars get their content, and where finished live sessions end up. A video added once can be used by any webinar that needs it."],[7826,"The Library page explains the other tabs and how storage is counted."],[7876,"The Library: every tab, and how storage is counted."],[6580,"The Library: where the recording of a live original waits to be picked."],[8185,"The Library: where the recordings your replay pages play are kept."],[3117,"The Library's recordings: where your live session's recording lands before you clone it as Evergreen."],[9275,"The links fall into three kinds. **Audience links** (registration, one-click registration, attendee join, attendee replay) are meant for the people watching. **Team links** (co-presenter and moderator) let someone into the room in a role, so they're private. The **Thank You Page Link** is where registrants arrive on their own, so you rarely need it."],[1384,"The list is headed **Select Email service**, and it invites you to connect an SMTP provider to send emails from EasyCRM. **EasyCRM Mail** is already on the list, marked **Default**. To use your own provider, click **Add Service**, then **Configure** on the one you use, and enter its credentials. The section on built-in mail or your own SMTP provider, above, explains how to choose."],[22,"The live room"],[7905,"The live room"],[9235,"The live room"],[7581,"The live room has a fixed order: get in, get ready, go live, run the session and end it. Each part below covers one stage and the setting that matters for it."],[7433,"The live room is where a live webinar actually happens. Everything you set up beforehand leads to this hour. The room gives you a private place to prepare, a clear start, the tools to engage and sell, and a definite end."],[7427,"The live room is where you present a live webinar. You arrive in a green room where nothing is broadcast, then go live when you're set. The same room holds backstage, chat, polls, offers, questions and the recording controls."],[1939,"The live room is where you present. You arrive in a green room where nothing is broadcast, so you can check your camera framing and test your screen share. Co-hosts and moderators wait backstage, and your audience doesn't see or hear them until you bring them on. The live webinar guide covers the room from start to finish."],[2071,"The live room needs a working **camera and microphone** before it lets you into the green room. The button stays greyed out until the room can see both. You can turn them off once you are in, but you can't enter without them."],[7623,"The live room reached 35% of registrants, and the replay added another 80 people the live moment missed. The 180 people in **Missed** are the clearest next step: send them the replay. The follow-up email guide covers what to send. Harvard Business Review's research on online leads found that most companies are not responding nearly fast enough, so send it while the session is fresh."],[7991,"The live room's header shows **Auto Recording Enabled** when recording is on, and the badge changes to **Recording** once you're live. Recording can be changed under **Settings → Recording Settings**, and recorded sessions land in **Library → Recordings**. See Run a live webinar."],[5170,"The live webinar on **My Webinars**, with the session already run."],[826,"The logic of the job has not changed: a script needs an order, a running time and words for each slide. What can change is who writes the first draft. If the draft arrives already split into timed sections, the coach's job becomes editing rather than writing from nothing."],[7573,"The logic of the situation is simple. A live session has two audiences. The team needs a private space to get ready, and the attendees should see only the finished room. Questions and offers need their own places, and the recording should start without anyone remembering it."],[1149,"The logic of the situation is simple. The webinar platform already knows who registered and who attended. Moving that data by hand adds a step every time a session ends, and every step is a chance to lose someone."],[2072,"The message appears when the room can't open the camera or microphone you selected."],[343,"The missed group"],[5545,"The mix of attendees decides whether the chat reads as real. A room full of enthusiastic experts posting constantly looks staged. A few beginners asking basic questions, one sceptic, a couple of quiet observers on low engagement and some mid-level regulars looks like an audience."],[8686,"The names overlap, so the two are easy to mix up."],[76,"The new member appears in the Team list with their role and joined date, and the seat count on the **Settings** overview goes up by one."],[1385,"The notice on the screen sets out the requirement. You must either complete **Domain Configuration** for a dedicated domain, or validate a single business email address under **Email addresses**. Either route counts, and nothing sends until one of them is done. The **Domain Configuration** link in the notice and the **Domains** button both sit on this screen."],[1627,"The number sent tells you the workflow is running, but not whether anyone read it. Open and click rates tell you about engagement, but a low open rate is usually a subject line or sender problem rather than a bad offer. A good open rate with a low click rate means the email was opened and the offer didn't land."],[7137,"The objects you work with"],[3998,"The offer"],[2286,"The offer after the session"],[2283,"The offer at the end"],[5675,"The offer countdown lives inside the **offer** itself, so it travels wherever that offer is shown. The registration-page countdown is a **block** on the page, so you style and position it like any other part of the page."],[6319,"The offer editor screenshot under Create an offer shows **Countdown timer**, **Offer Price** and the **Button** settings side by side. How other platforms handle offer countdowns and offer reporting is covered in How offers compare, below."],[4074,"The offer is mentioned lightly during the teaching, then made properly at the end. Attendees are not surprised by it, and the close can be stronger. The risk is that early mentions feel like interruptions if they are not tied to the point you just made."],[4089,"The offer itself belongs after the bridge and before the questions. Placing it there means the questions can deal with objections while the offer is still on screen. On a simulive or evergreen webinar the same order holds, because polls and offers appear at the points you set rather than when you remember them."],[5703,"The offer stays ready but does not appear until you switch it on."],[4077,"The offer step in **Create with AI** asks you to choose one of three positionings: **Soft Pitch**, which is education-focused with the offer at the end; **Balanced Approach**, which gives value plus a clear offer throughout and a strong close; and **Direct Pitch**, which is offer-focused with repeated calls to action and strong urgency."],[2284,"The offer woven through"],[3541,"The offer-click email"],[5699,"The offer's **Title** is internal. Attendees never see it, so name it in a way that helps your team, for example \"Course enrolment, launch week\"."],[6752,"The one you set for the webinar. A live session runs at one fixed time for everyone, and the registration page shows that time with its timezone."],[4167,"The outline is your private plan of sections, timings and asks. The agenda is the public version you promise attendees. The script is the word-for-word talk written from the outline, slide by slide."],[4158,"The outline template lives on this page. EasyWebinar works from the description you write, so the planning decisions stay with you."],[9646,"The Overview matters most to teams running more than one webinar a month. Each webinar needs a quick status check and the right links for the right people. That need grows when guest speakers change from one session to the next."],[20,"The Overview tab"],[6610,"The Overview tab"],[9481,"The Overview tab has a **Convert to paid** button for free webinars. If you convert an existing free webinar to paid, its registration page keeps the free form, so people can still register without paying. Create a new webinar and choose **Paid** on the pricing step instead."],[9515,"The Overview tab has a **Convert to paid** button, but the registration page of a converted webinar keeps the free form. Create a new webinar and choose **Paid** on the pricing step instead."],[9638,"The Overview tab is the first of nine tabs on every webinar, and the one each webinar opens on. It summarises the webinar rather than configuring its content: numbers at the top, then schedule, sessions, pricing and team."],[9640,"The Overview tab is the home screen for one webinar. It answers everyday questions without opening any of the other eight tabs. Is anyone registering, when does it run, what does it cost, who is presenting, and which link do you send?"],[1938,"The Overview tab shows how one webinar is performing and holds its share links, so it is the screen you check before and after each session."],[6581,"The Overview tab: checking the copy's schedule and its empty registrant count."],[7314,"The Overview tab: where **Enter Room** opens the green room for a session."],[9037,"The Overview tab: where **Registrants** confirms your test registration."],[9212,"The Overview tab: where the **Registration Page** button opens the live page."],[9345,"The Overview tab: where the **Share** button sits, next to **Registration Page**."],[5049,"The Overview tab: where the Live tab shows a just-in-time session on air."],[4698,"The Overview tab's live stats: four of these numbers at a glance, updating while the webinar runs."],[9112,"The page and the webinar describe the same event. So it makes sense for the page to read the schedule from the webinar, not from a copy someone keeps in step. When the date lives in one place, the page and the session can't disagree."],[8781,"The page builder"],[620,"The page builder's own advice is that if you only have time for two pages, make them the registration page and the replay page. The AI covers the first, so the replay page is the obvious one to style yourself."],[9195,"The page needs to be published. Open it in the builder and click **Publish**. Saving alone isn't enough."],[1348,"The page setting needs the **C-Name** add-on plus a CNAME record, and it changes the public links people click. Treat them as two separate jobs, even if you use the same company domain for both."],[612,"The page stays on your own website, in your own design, with the webinar's registration form placed inside it. That keeps the brand consistent. What it misses is the copy around the form, which is still yours to write."],[969,"The pages"],[633,"The pages are only as specific as the prompt behind them. A weak prompt produces generic output, and the generic wording flows through the script into every page. Include these five things:"],[706,"The pages are written from the script, so anything wrong in the script carries into the pages. Fix the script before you continue."],[3455,"The paid ad brought the most registrants but the lowest show-up rate. The partner newsletter brought half as many, yet its attendees clicked the offer at twice the paid ad's rate. The team's own list showed up best but clicked least, which suggests the offer suits new prospects more than existing contacts."],[6508,"The panel at the bottom of the dialog repeats the new webinar's name and type before you commit. Read it once: it's the last chance to catch a wrong type, which can't be changed afterwards."],[7238,"The panel itself doesn't need to change. What matters is where the speakers wait and when the audience starts watching. If the checks happen out of view, the session can open on the first question instead of a sound test."],[1689,"The parts of EasyWebinar"],[1731,"The People view"],[5949,"The People view"],[6263,"The People view"],[8777,"The People view"],[8930,"The People view"],[4402,"The People view in EasyWebinar lists every registrant and attendee across all your webinars, with watch percentage, offer clicks and lead score, and lets you filter by behaviour or campaign and export the result."],[2851,"The People view lists everyone across your webinars. **Watch percentage** and **Offer clicked** are the two columns worth sorting by. Someone who watched most of the session and clicked your offer is ready for a personal note from you."],[1955,"The People view lists everyone who registered or attended across all your webinars. You can filter by behaviour and source, then export the list."],[4461,"The People view matters to whoever owns the follow-up after a webinar, whether that's sales, marketing or a solo host. It matters most in the first day or two after a session, when per-person engagement data decides who gets a call first."],[2846,"The people who registered aren't one group. Attendees heard the pitch, no-shows didn't, and offer clickers were close to acting. EasyWebinar's **Notifications** tab handles each group separately."],[8831,"The person signing up is the person answering the questions. So it makes sense to ask everything in one form, at the moment they register. Then the answers, the consent and the campaign all stay on the same record."],[209,"The pieces don't really need separate starting points. The script, the page, the emails, the polls and the offer all come from the same idea. If that idea is written down once, each piece can be drafted from it, and her time goes into editing instead."],[2827,"The pitch works best when it feels like the obvious end of the lesson. Teach the method, show one person's situation it applies to, then explain what working together looks like. Put the offer on screen when you start talking about the programme, not before."],[7771,"The plain logic is that a recording matters only if it exists when the session ends. A setting that is on until you turn it off is safer than a button you have to remember under pressure. The copy is also more useful when it lands where the replay and the next webinar are built."],[2167,"The plan chooser for a new workspace offers Launch, Growth, Pro or Scale, billed monthly or yearly. How the separate-account set-up stacks up against other tools is covered in How EasyWebinar compares for white label webinar software, below."],[7775,"The platform captures the session from its own side, without a button press. It catches the most common failure, which is forgetting to start. It also keeps the copy next to the webinar, so reusing it needs no upload. What it can miss is anything the platform doesn't include in its recording, so you still need to know what that is."],[6028,"The poll stays on the webinar, ready to switch on, and nothing is deleted."],[4119,"The poll step asks what the polls are for rather than what to ask. Pick the goals that match polls A, B and C in your outline, such as increasing engagement, understanding pain points or gauging interest in your offer. Click **Generate polls** and you get five, each with its question, its options and a short explanation of where it fits in the run of show. Keep the three that match your outline and edit them on the Polls side of the CTAs tab."],[971,"The polls and offers"],[6000,"The polls step lists seven goals, shown in the screenshot under Let AI write your polls, and returns five polls for 1 credit. Each poll comes with a short note on where it fits in the run of show. How other platforms handle polls is covered in How polls compare, below."],[6020,"The polls that get answered ask about the attendee, not about what they remember. A question about their situation, their plans or their biggest problem takes a second to answer and needs no right answer. A knowledge test feels like an exam, and people skip it."],[1980,"The post on why teams choose EasyWebinar sets out the case in more depth."],[9160,"The price block is added only when **Paid** is chosen at creation. A free webinar converted to paid afterwards keeps the free form, so choose **Paid** at creation if you want to charge."],[3786,"The problem before the product"],[2616,"The product side is the same in each case. A paid workshop in EasyWebinar is a webinar with **Paid** chosen on the pricing step, plus the engagement settings that suit teaching."],[3995,"The promise"],[9293,"The public link to your registration page, and the one you promote in ads, emails and posts. It only works once the registration page has been published in the page builder; saving alone isn't enough."],[6161,"The Q&A screenshot under Set up Q&A shows the three options side by side: **Public Q&A**, **Private Q&A** and **Disable**. After the briefing, the **Questions & Answers** panel in **Analytics** shows how many questions were asked and how many were answered. How other platforms handle attendee questions is covered in How Q&A compares, below."],[5998,"The questions the agency needs answered are the same every month. Only the audience changes. So the questions can be written once, with fixed answers, and kept with the webinar instead of being improvised on the day."],[3919,"The rates on the Broadcasts screen are averages across all your broadcasts, not the figures for one send. Write down the totals after each invitation so you can see how that send moved the average. Registrations also come from other places, such as social posts or an embedded form on your site, so not every registrant came from the email."],[8973,"The reader has already been convinced on the treatment page. So the form belongs there, next to the content that did the convincing, rather than one click away on another site."],[5142,"The recording is the whole evergreen webinar, so check it before you clone. Open the Library and go to the **Recordings** tab. Each recording is named after its webinar, with its duration and the date it was recorded."],[3000,"The recording of the live session already holds the pitch that worked. What needs to change is who presses play: each new registrant, at a time that suits them, rather than the creator."],[5310,"The recording plays at times you choose, like a programme scheduled on television. Everyone in a given session sees the same point of the video at the same moment. That's what lets the chat still work as a shared room, with you in it answering questions while the video plays. It's the usual choice for a presentation you give over and over, or a session that runs at a time you don't want to be awake for."],[2488,"The recording runs on a repeating schedule, with each registrant picking their own time. Evergreen suits a demo, an onboarding session or an offer you run all year. It is not the best fit for a one-off event where the live questions are the point. The automated webinar overview explains how the schedules work together."],[8814,"The registration form is a block on the registration page, like the countdown or a heading. Its settings decide what a person is asked when they sign up. Out of the box it asks for a name and an email address, both required."],[8927,"The registration page"],[9366,"The registration page"],[2361,"The registration page builder opens straight after you create the webinar, with your date, time and a live countdown already in place. Pick a template, then check the page in the **Device toggle**'s mobile view, because many people will register from a phone. The page isn't public until you click **Publish**."],[9109,"The registration page decides how much of your promotion becomes an audience. It matters most to whoever pays for the traffic, in the weeks before a session. That's when a page that matches your brand and shows which campaign drove each signup earns its keep."],[9652,"The registration page is built on the **Content** tab and opened from the **Registration Page** button here. The **Registrants** stat counts people who complete its form."],[8997,"The registration page is still where your webinar's full pitch lives: the design, the countdown, the copy. Build and publish it in the page builder even if most registrations come through the widget, because the link and the page are what you'll use in email and on social."],[3016,"The registration page is the link you promote, so it carries most of your signups. It opens in a drag-and-drop builder with your date, time and timezone already in place."],[9090,"The registration page is the page people see before they sign up, and the link you promote. EasyWebinar builds it in a drag-and-drop page builder that already carries your webinar's date, time and timezone, with a countdown and a name-and-email form in place. You choose a template, edit blocks on a canvas, check the mobile layout, and click **Publish** to make the page public. The same builder edits the webinar's other four pages."],[9100,"The registration page matters most because it carries your signups. It's built from **blocks**, such as the date and countdown block and the form block, plus whatever else the template includes. You select a block on the canvas to change it, add new blocks underneath, and set page-wide options such as fonts, background and tracking from the top bar."],[9335,"The registration page needs to be published. Open it in the page builder and click **Publish**."],[9449,"The registration page shows the price above the form."],[9102,"The registration page turns interest into a registrant. It says what the webinar is and when it runs, then asks for the details you need. After that, it hands each person on to the thank-you page."],[8554,"The reminders themselves don't change from one session to the next. What changes is the start time each one counts back from. A reminder set against the session, rather than the calendar, fits every session the schedule creates."],[2958,"The replay"],[3537,"The replay email for no-shows"],[8140,"The replay link also appears in the webinar's **Share** dialog as **Attendee Replay link**, alongside the registration and attendee join links meant for your audience. See Sharing links."],[3060,"The replay link is public while the replay page is on, so treat it as something anyone can share."],[8650,"The replay page"],[6365,"The replay page has two looks, set on the **Replay** tab:"],[8098,"The replay page is also the fifth of the five webinar pages on the **Content** tab, after the registration page, thank-you page, waiting area and webinar page. The Replay tab decides how the page behaves; the Content tab is where you can style it in the page builder."],[7085,"The replay page on the **Replay** tab is for people who missed a session that has already happened. It is separate from On Demand. See Replay pages."],[4543,"The result count is shown below the table."],[198,"The result is a normal EasyWebinar webinar. It has the same nine tabs and the same settings as one built by hand. The only visible difference is the **AI** badge on its card in **My Webinars**."],[6713,"The review is the same course with the same instructors every evening. What changes is only the calendar. So one webinar with a repeating schedule fits the course far better than sixteen separate ones, as long as the gaps can be marked."],[7477,"The room needs to see a working camera and microphone before it lets you in. See Camera not detected."],[4970,"The routine itself is not the problem. What matters is the time between the click and the first minute of the video. Ad traffic arrives all day and from more than one country, so any single fixed time is wrong for most visitors."],[4123,"The row toggle turns a CTA on or off without deleting it, which helps when you want the offer ready but not live until section 7. In the offer editor, switch on **Countdown timer** if your outline gives the offer a deadline, and set **Button URL** to your own checkout, booking or sales page. The offers and countdown timers pages cover both settings in detail. Testing your room and rehearsing belong to your preparation checklist, and the green room page covers the private space for that run-through."],[2083,"The same fixes apply on their computer. Co-hosts and moderators who get in land backstage, not on stage, so your audience doesn't see or hear them until you bring them on. A guest co-host or moderator joins with the Co-presenter Join Link or the Moderator Join Link from the Share dialog."],[345,"The same job in EasyWebinar"],[546,"The same job in EasyWebinar"],[768,"The same job in EasyWebinar"],[1252,"The same job in EasyWebinar"],[1502,"The same job in EasyWebinar"],[2109,"The same job in EasyWebinar"],[2298,"The same job in EasyWebinar"],[2548,"The same job in EasyWebinar"],[2763,"The same job in EasyWebinar"],[3149,"The same job in EasyWebinar"],[3328,"The same job in EasyWebinar"],[3551,"The same job in EasyWebinar"],[3798,"The same job in EasyWebinar"],[4218,"The same job in EasyWebinar"],[4723,"The same job in EasyWebinar"],[5075,"The same job in EasyWebinar"],[5268,"The same job in EasyWebinar"],[7519,"The same job in EasyWebinar"],[7714,"The same job in EasyWebinar"],[3666,"The same section requires a valid physical postal address in commercial email. It also prohibits a subject line you know is likely to mislead a reader about the message. So \"Replay closes Friday\" is only safe to send if the replay really closes on Friday. Check each email for both, and add them if they aren't there."],[1416,"The same split applies to text messages. A webinar's own SMS (Beta) is built on the same **Notifications** tab, and those messages go from your EasyWebinar number. That builder needs a phone number for each person, which comes from the Phone field on your registration form."],[3617,"The same study found that spreading one transaction across too many messages caused problems: people lost track and overlooked what mattered. Follow-ups aren't transactions, but the lesson carries over. That's the reason for stopping at about three emails per group, each with one next step."],[2859,"The schedule decides how long an interested prospect waits between registering and hearing your pitch, so set it before anything else."],[7021,"The schedule step is where an evergreen webinar differs from every other type. It offers three ways for people to watch, plus controls for when the schedule runs."],[7038,"The schedule step shows a message saying attendees will see times in their local time based on the selected timezone. On an evergreen webinar that applies to just-in-time sessions only. Daily and weekly sessions run at the times you set, in the webinar's timezone."],[6843,"The schedule step shows a message saying attendees will see times in their local time. For simulive sessions that isn't how they run: they play at the times you set, in the webinar's timezone. Only just-in-time sessions on evergreen webinars follow each attendee's local time."],[6836,"The schedule step works the same way it does for a live webinar."],[8417,"The screenshot under Choose who your emails come from shows the **Sender name**, **Reply-to email** and **Notify admin** settings in one dialog. How other platforms handle the sender, replies and admin copies is covered in How confirmation emails compare, below."],[211,"The screenshot under Describe your webinar shows the prompt box, **Type**, the five output chips and the credit balance on one screen. How other platforms use AI to build a webinar is covered in How Create with AI compares, below."],[4466,"The screenshot under Filter the list shows the six filter categories, from **Webinar** to **Device & Location**, with **Apply Filter** marked. How other platforms handle people lists, filters and exports is covered in How the People view compares, below."],[8556,"The screenshot under Find your reminders shows the **Webinar Reminders** group with reminders timed 15 minutes, 1 hour and 24 hours before the webinar. How other platforms set reminder timing and channels is covered in How reminder emails compare, below."],[9651,"The screenshot under Open the Overview tab numbers seven areas, from the nine tabs to **Team & Moderators**. How other platforms handle guest links, registration links and session history is covered in How the Overview tab compares, below."],[9287,"The screenshot under Open the Share dialog shows the co-presenter, moderator and one-click registration links in a single list. How other platforms handle presenter links, one-click registration and short links is covered in How sharing links compare, below."],[7240,"The screenshot under Read the room shows the **Not Live** status, the **Backstage** panel and **Go Live** in one view. How other platforms handle a pre-show room for speakers is covered in How the green room compares, below."],[8703,"The screenshot under Set it up shows the **Instant replay text** field, which reads *Watch instant replay* until you change it. How other platforms offer viewing straight after registration is covered in How On Demand compares, below."],[4972,"The screenshot under Set it up shows the **Just-in-time Registration** switch at the top of the schedule step, above **On Demand** and **Recurring Schedule**. How other platforms schedule sessions that start soon after registration is covered in How just-in-time compares, below."],[8110,"The screenshot under Set up a replay page shows **Availability** beside **Redirects** and **Experience** on one tab. How other platforms handle replay deadlines, offers and page design is covered in How replay pages compare, below."],[5840,"The screenshot under Set up chat shows both **Who can see messages** options, **Everyone** and **Hosts only**, beside the chat mode. How other platforms handle who sees attendee messages is covered in How live chat compares, below."],[5524,"The screenshot under Set up Manual Simulation shows the CSV upload beside **Preview Simulated Chat** and **Attendees on Event Page**. How other platforms handle simulated chat is covered in How chat simulation compares, below."],[816,"The screenshots in the set-up steps below show the prompt screen and the finished script."],[968,"The script"],[269,"The script and pages are the expensive steps, which is another reason to get the prompt right before you generate. Each step shows your balance before you commit, and you can switch off any output chip to skip that part."],[893,"The script comes back organised into named sections, such as hook and welcome and problem framing. Each section has its own timing and speaker notes. Read it all before you move on. It's much easier to fix the script now than to fix the pages and emails that get built from it."],[3014,"The script downloads as a PDF so you can present from it. A full build uses about 23 credits."],[235,"The script is organised into named sections, such as the hook and welcome and the problem framing, each with its own timing and speaker notes. Read it before you continue. Fixing the script now is much easier than fixing the pages and emails built from it. Click **Continue to landing pages**."],[900,"The script is the source for the rest of an AI build, so it's worth getting right first."],[877,"The script should come to about 5,200 words, and the teaching points take 45% of it (2,340 ÷ 5,200). If her draft runs to 6,500 words, it is 25% too long (1,300 ÷ 5,200), which at her pace is 10 extra minutes. That is time taken straight from the offer and the questions."],[4116,"The script step asks for the number of slides and the duration, then writes the script slide by slide. It comes back in named sections, such as the hook and welcome and the problem framing, each with its own timing and speaker notes."],[4159,"The script step needs a number of slides and a duration before it writes, and changing either changes the result when you regenerate."],[815,"The script step shows four controls. **Webinar topic** holds your prompt, and you can still edit it. **Slides and duration** let you change either number and regenerate. **Regenerate** writes a fresh script, and **Download PDF** takes the script away so you can present from it. Below them sits the script itself, organised into sections with a timing and speaker notes for each."],[937,"The script step uses 10 credits. A full build of all five parts uses around 23. Your credit balance shows at the top right of every step."],[3791,"The second invitation"],[4805,"The second is **time to first watch**: how long a registrant waits between signing up and the session starting. Shorter waits are the reason just-in-time exists, and HBR's research on online leads found that most companies are not responding nearly fast enough to people who show interest. Its weak spot is that you work it out from your schedule, not from a single number."],[4351,"The second is whether every step worked from the attendee's side. Your colleague should have entered the room, seen your slides, answered a poll and been sent to your redirect at the end. Ask them to read one line from your smallest slide off their phone. If they can't, your slides fail the phone check."],[1092,"The sections of the CRM"],[2354,"The session centres on the offer, with repeated calls to act and strong urgency. It suits a warm audience that already knows you. It can feel heavy to people meeting you for the first time."],[4464,"The session doesn't need to change. What changes is the order of the calls. Someone who watched most of the webinar and clicked the offer should hear from an agent first. The ad budget should follow the channel that brought them."],[3602,"The session has already sorted her list. Some people came, some didn't, and some clicked the offer. She doesn't need one better email, just a short message for each of those groups, timed around Friday."],[4075,"The session is built around the offer, with several calls to action and a clear deadline. It suits a warm audience that already knows the product. It misses people who came to learn, who may feel sold to before they have been taught anything."],[4806,"The Sessions panel lists every session your schedule generated, which is where you check the spacing and gaps between sessions; dates shown are sample data."],[1305,"The setup rests on two separate decisions, and it helps to keep them apart. The **email service** is the system that carries your mail out of the CRM. The **sending identity** is the domain or address your mail comes from, verified so the CRM will send as you. Choosing a service doesn't verify your identity, and verifying your identity doesn't choose a service."],[9224,"The seven links"],[9214,"The seven links in an EasyWebinar webinar's Share dialog, from the registration page link you promote to the one-click registration, attendee, co-presenter, moderator and replay links, and who each one is for."],[8998,"The Share dialog groups every way to share a webinar: **Share Links**, **Registration Widget** and **Share on social media**."],[95,"The Share dialog holds a Co-presenter Join Link for a presenter and a **Moderator Join Link for Live room** for someone who handles chat and questions. Treat both as private. Anyone holding one can join your room in that role."],[9270,"The Share dialog is the single place to copy any link a webinar produces. Click **Share** on the webinar's header and the dialog opens with three groups:"],[9306,"The Share dialog's **Registration Widget** holds the same embed snippet as the **Content** tab. Use it to put the form on your own site instead of sharing a link."],[3188,"The short answer to \"how do teams manage regular webinars?\" is this. Give every person their own login, name one owner per webinar, and start each new webinar as a copy of the last good one."],[1001,"The short answer to \"what can AI do for my webinar\" is this: it writes the first draft of everything, and you still own the facts, the schedule and the offer."],[2258,"The Short Life of Online Sales Leads (Harvard Business Review, 2011) describes why slow responses to online leads cost sales."],[2438,"The Short Life of Online Sales Leads (Harvard Business Review, 2011): why the speed of your response to a new lead matters."],[4890,"The Short Life of Online Sales Leads (Harvard Business Review): research on how slowly companies respond to interested people."],[7682,"The Short Life of Online Sales Leads, Harvard Business Review (2011): research on how quickly companies respond to interested leads."],[3765,"The Short Life of Online Sales Leads, Harvard Business Review, March 2011: the case for answering online enquiries quickly. One co-author ran a sales software company, and the full article is behind a paywall."],[3508,"The Short Life of Online Sales Leads, Harvard Business Review, March 2011: the case for replying to online leads quickly."],[6883,"The short version: simulive is a broadcast of a recording on a timetable. It sits between live, where you present every time, and evergreen, where the schedule runs continuously and each person picks their own time. For the evergreen setup, see Create an evergreen webinar."],[4340,"The side rail holds **Chat**, **People**, **Offers**, **Polls**, **Design**, **Question** and **Theme**. Open **Polls** and **Offers** now, so you know where they are when you need them. The green room and backstage page describes each control."],[7563,"The side rail on the right holds **Chat**, **People**, **Offers**, **Polls**, **Design**, **Question** and **Theme**. **Layout** sets how presenters are arranged on stage. The green room and backstage page explains each control in full."],[337,"The six emails the AI drafts"],[7949,"The small badge in the corner of each card shows where the video is hosted:"],[9594,"The stat cards across the top tell you, at a glance, whether people are signing up and turning up."],[1617,"The status tabs show at a glance which follow-up sequences are live, which are still drafts and which you have retired."],[1017,"The step-by-step Create with AI guide covers every screen. Here is what each part gives you and what to look at."],[7632,"The step-by-step guide to creating a live webinar covers every field, including weekly schedules and blackout dates. On the day, the guide to running a live webinar takes you from the green room to **End Session**."],[5384,"The step-by-step simulive guide walks through each screen if you want more detail. Once you skip the template, you can design the page later in the registration page builder."],[5169,"The steps below take a live webinar you have already run and create an evergreen copy of it. They take a few minutes once the recording is ready."],[3863,"The subject line carries most of the open. Nielsen Norman Group advises showing no more than about 40 characters and putting the useful words first, so nothing important is cut off (2014). For more subject lines to test, read our guide to webinar email subject lines that increase registrations."],[3225,"The tabs across the top are **All**, **Live**, **Simulive**, **Evergreen** and **Archived**, and each shows a live count. **Search** finds a webinar by name, which is why the naming pattern matters. **Sort By** defaults to **Newest First**, and a toggle switches between grid and list views."],[2190,"The Team and Clients menu holds both your colleagues' logins and the separate workspaces you run for clients, so you reach both from one place."],[3206,"The team keeps one well-built webinar for each format and starts every new one as a copy. This stops settings drifting between sessions. It misses nothing on its own, but it only works if the master is kept up to date."],[3256,"The Team screen is where you check that every colleague has their own login and the right role before the month starts."],[93,"The Team screen pictured under Open the Team screen lists each member with a **Role** column reading **Owner**, **Admin** or **User**. How other platforms handle team logins, guest presenters and client accounts is covered in How team members compares, below."],[4071,"The template is yours to keep and reuse. EasyWebinar does not ship a blank outline; it works from the description you write, so a filled-in outline is the best input you can give it."],[4078,"The template names eight sections. This is what each one has to achieve, and what usually goes wrong in it."],[3650,"The templates above use a course because it's the clearest case, and our webinars for course creators page covers that audience in full. Everything in square brackets changes with the webinar. Keep the structure and change these parts."],[4211,"The test run: what to try and in what order"],[8361,"The thank-you page"],[645,"The thank-you page only loads after someone registers, which makes it a reliable conversion signal. Tracking code is set per page: the registration page tells your ad platform who was interested, and the thank-you page tells it who converted. Its weak spot is that it counts sign-ups, not attendance. A low **Registered** to **Attended** number is a reminder problem rather than a page one."],[9514,"The thank-you page shows their personal join link, a **Join Room** button, the session date and **Add to Calendar** buttons for Google, Outlook and Apple. They also get your confirmation email."],[1874,"The three webinar types"],[7131,"The three webinar types"],[6652,"The three webinar types use the same pages, emails, polls, offers and analytics. Only the delivery differs."],[7130,"The three webinar types, what every webinar is made of, and how registration, the room, follow-up and analytics fit together."],[5383,"The timezone you set on this step decides when every simulive session plays for attendees around the world."],[3691,"The To column is the one to check today. On this sample webinar the instant follow-up goes to Everyone, so change it before pasting in an attendee-only thank-you."],[6493,"The top of the dialog sets out what you're copying from and what you're creating."],[5838,"The topic doesn't have to change for people to speak up. What changes is who can read the message. When a question goes only to the firm, an attendee can ask it in plain words. The colleague can reply while the planner keeps presenting."],[9147,"The tracking, SEO, social and CSS areas also appear on the webinar's **Settings** tab, where they cover every page at once. Set them there to cover the whole webinar, or here for one page."],[9467,"The transaction list shows each buyer's name, email, session, registration date, amount and status. You can search it and filter by status."],[8130,"The two are easy to confuse, and they answer different needs."],[6375,"The two ratios point at different fixes:"],[7228,"The two together are what EasyWebinar's glossary calls the green room: a presenter backstage before the broadcast starts."],[2350,"The type can't be changed after you create the webinar. If you pick the wrong one, clone the webinar and choose the right type on the copy. Your original stays untouched, and its registrants stay with it."],[4773,"The type can't be changed once the webinar exists. If you pick the wrong card, clone the webinar and choose the right type on the copy."],[4772,"The type cards on this first step decide whether your webinar plays a recording, so pick Simulive or Evergreen here for an automated webinar."],[6719,"The type is fixed once the webinar exists. To reuse a live webinar as simulive or evergreen, clone it and choose the new type on the copy."],[1927,"The type is fixed once you create the webinar. To change it, you clone the webinar and pick a new type. This is also how you turn a session that worked live into an evergreen webinar from a past live recording."],[9656,"The type of a webinar can't be changed after it's created. To run the same webinar as a different type, clone it from its card on **My Webinars**. The copy starts with no registrations."],[3033,"The type of a webinar is fixed, so the path from a live session to an evergreen funnel is cloning:"],[2259,"The UK's data protection legislation (GOV.UK) lists the principles for using and keeping personal data, including client registrant lists."],[3767,"The UK's data protection legislation, GOV.UK: the data protection principles that apply to anyone using registrants' details."],[3509,"The UK's data protection legislation, GOV.UK: the principles that apply to the personal details you collect at registration."],[2931,"The UK's data protection legislation, GOV.UK: the principles that apply to the questions you ask on a registration or application form."],[2725,"The UK's data protection legislation, GOV.UK: the principles that apply when you collect buyers' names, emails and phone numbers."],[3306,"The UK's data protection legislation, GOV.UK: the principles that apply when your team exports and shares registrant lists."],[3721,"The useful measures for this job are the ones that show someone taking the next step: watching the replay, clicking the offer in the room, and buying. They have weak spots, so read them with care."],[4783,"The usual answer is to keep presenting it live, or to email a recording link to people who missed it. Presenting live ties up a senior person every week, and the trial user who signs up on Friday may wait almost a week. A bare video link usually loses the chat, the polls and the offer that made the session work."],[2808,"The usual fix is to book more free discovery calls, often from a social post with a calendar link. That can work, but it tends to fill the diary with people who are curious rather than ready. Each of those calls costs an hour she would rather spend with paying clients, and the follow-up notes take longer still."],[402,"The usual fix is to copy last month's emails and change the names, dates and topic. That tends to work until a reminder still mentions the previous topic, or a join link points at last month's room. Writing fresh copy for every client avoids that, but it can take most of a day each month."],[825,"The usual route is to open a blank document, or to borrow a script template and rewrite every line of it. That often takes a full day, and the timing is a guess until the first rehearsal. The registration page then gets written separately, so its promises can drift away from what the session actually covers."],[4056,"The usual route is to start with slides. Someone opens last quarter's deck, adds new material and only later works out where the trial offer goes. It tends to work, but the timing is often guessed and the polls are written the night before. The registration page and emails can also drift from what the session teaches."],[1013,"The usual route is to write each piece separately, perhaps with a general AI chatbot in another browser tab. That tends to mean copying text between tools, and the pages, emails and offer can end up describing slightly different things."],[604,"The usual route is to write the page herself, late in the evening after training clients. She might copy last month's page and change the date, which can leave an old promise or a stale price in place. Starting from an empty template often takes longer than planned, so promotion tends to slip by a few days."],[7572,"The usual setup can be fiddly. Many presenters join a meeting link early and hope the audience doesn't arrive while they're still testing audio. The partner may appear on camera mid-sentence. Questions can get lost in a busy chat, and the recording may be an afterthought."],[2610,"The usual way to do this joins several tools together. A payment page takes the money, a form or spreadsheet tracks who paid, and someone emails a meeting link by hand. That tends to work for ten people and break at forty. A late payer misses the email, or a forwarded link lets in someone who never paid."],[4269,"The usual way to prepare is a private video call the day before, with the presenters talking through their slides. That can catch a bad microphone. It probably won't catch what attendees see, or whether the reminders arrive. Nor will it show where people land when the session ends."],[7442,"The usual workaround is a general video meeting tool, set up by whoever is free that week. It may work, but the student often joins straight into the broadcast and tests their microphone in front of everyone. Questions can get lost in a busy chat, and the recording may end up in one person's account."],[1559,"The usual workaround is a spreadsheet export and a batch of emails written late at night. It tends to work in the first month and fade by the third. The no-shows rarely get their replay link, and a lead who asked a good question can wait a week for a reply."],[3387,"The usual workaround is a spreadsheet. Someone exports the registrant list, exports attendance after the session and matches the two by email address. That can take an afternoon, and without a campaign tag on each registration it may still not show the source. Budget then tends to follow whichever channel produced the most sign-ups."],[1148,"The usual workaround is an export after every session. Someone downloads the attendee list, cleans it in a spreadsheet and uploads it to a separate CRM. That step can take an afternoon, and it tends to slip in a busy month. When it slips, the warmest leads may wait days for a reply."],[3198,"The usual workaround is one shared login and a spreadsheet of who does what. That tends to work until two people edit the same webinar, or a guest needs a password just to present for 40 minutes. Each new webinar is then rebuilt by hand, and settings that worked last month are often forgotten."],[2164,"The usual workaround is one shared webinar account with each client's logo pasted onto its pages. That tends to work for the first client. By the third, the registrant lists are mixed together and every link carries the same vendor address. The monthly numbers come from a spreadsheet someone rebuilds by hand."],[7986,"The usual workaround is to download the recording from a meeting tool, upload it to a shared drive and email the link around. That can work, but copies end up in several places and nobody is quite sure which one is current. A link held in one person's account may stop working when they leave."],[5133,"The usual workaround is to download the recording, create a new automated webinar and rebuild everything around it. That can mean recreating the registration page, re-entering polls and offers, rewriting the reminder emails and reconnecting her email tool. Each step takes time, and a missed detail can quietly break the funnel."],[1322,"The usual workaround is to export the list and send from the shared office mailbox or a separate email tool. That may get one message out, but it tends to cost more with each send. Results sit apart from the lead records, replies scatter, and the next send starts from a fresh export."],[3852,"The usual workaround is to export the webinar link into a separate email tool, build the list there, and send from it. That can work well. It can also mean two logins, a list that drifts out of date, and registrants who never reach the place where you track them."],[5323,"The usual workaround is to keep presenting it live, or to send registrants a link to a video they can watch whenever they like. Presenting live may cost her every Thursday evening for the rest of the year. A plain video link tends to lose the sense of a group arriving together. The questions she answers well in the room may then go unasked."],[7770,"The usual workaround is to start a separate screen recorder on the laptop just before going live. It tends to work until the morning it doesn't. The recorder was never started, or the microphone wasn't selected. Even when it works, the file sits on one laptop until someone uploads it."],[2342,"The usual workaround is to string tools together. That often means a video-call link, a payment page, a spreadsheet of who signed up and a few emails sent by hand. Each piece works alone, but nobody tells you who watched, who clicked and who still needs the recording."],[8253,"The usual workaround is to upload the class to a public video site as an unlisted video, then paste the link into a webinar tool. That can work, but unlisted links may be shared onwards, and the class now depends on a second account. If the video is removed there, the webinar may stop playing."],[2199,"The video below shows the team roles screen and the custom domain settings you'll use in the next step."],[4841,"The video you click on this step is what every automated session plays, so check you have picked the final cut before you continue."],[7042,"The video you pick here is the recording every session plays, whichever way a registrant chooses to watch."],[3415,"The weak point is step 4. Matching by email can break when someone registers with one address and joins with another, and the sheet is out of date by the next morning. Speed matters too: research published in 2011 found most companies weren't responding to online leads nearly fast enough. A sheet that takes an afternoon to build slows that first reply further."],[4332,"The weak point is that the call isn't the webinar, so the replay page and the redirect go untested too. A microphone that works in one tool may still need permission in another, and nothing in the call tests the attendee view."],[3895,"The weak points are steps 2 and 7. The list goes out of date, and registrations live somewhere other than the opens and clicks."],[2880,"The weak points are the last three steps. The booking link scrolls away in the chat, the spreadsheet only shows who was there, and every run needs you live."],[2676,"The webinar analytics funnel shows **Live Attended**, **Replay Watched** and **Total Attended**, which counts each person once. The People view lists who registered and who attended."],[4127,"The webinar analytics Overview shows **Offers Performance**, with offers, views, clicks and purchases and a click rate, alongside **Views to Clicks** and **Clicks to Purchases**. Its **Questions & Answers** panel shows how many questions were asked and how many you answered. Use both after each session, then adjust the minutes in your outline before the next one."],[7162,"The webinar header also has **Registration Page**, **Share** and **Enter Room**."],[6699,"The webinar is created and opens in the **registration page builder**, with your schedule applied and a live countdown to the session."],[6664,"The webinar name is empty. The builder won't move on until you enter one."],[6716,"The webinar opens straight into the registration page builder with your schedule applied. Edit the page, add form fields and publish it there. The registration embeds put the same form on your own site."],[3986,"The webinar outline template"],[9521,"The webinar Overview tab"],[6758,"The webinar Overview tab: change the schedule for the whole webinar, or act on a single session, after it exists."],[9516,"The webinar Overview tab: see registrants and revenue, and edit the price with the pencil."],[7488,"The webinar Overview tab: where **Enter Room** sits, and where past sessions are listed."],[2051,"The webinar Overview tab: where one webinar's free or paid setting and live stats sit."],[3972,"The webinar Overview tab: where the **Share** dialog and its links live."],[1417,"The webinar SMS builder (Beta) opens from a webinar's Notifications tab, so it isn't where you connect SMS Integration (Beta) for the CRM."],[6196,"The webinar's **Analytics** Overview includes a **Questions & Answers** panel showing how many questions were asked and how many you answered. The **Not Answered Rate** is a useful check after a busy session: it tells you how many questions you left behind."],[8871,"The webinar's **Analytics** tab counts visitors and registrations. The People view's **Source & UTM** filter shows which campaign produced attendees, not just registrations."],[7601,"The webinar's **Analytics** tab shows **Registered**, **Live Attended**, **Replay Watched** and **Missed**. **Total Attended** counts unique people, broken into **Live only**, **Replay only** and **Both**. Further down, **Offers Performance** shows views, clicks and purchases for each offer. The webinar analytics page explains each panel, and People lists everyone individually."],[8583,"The webinar's **Analytics** tab shows the funnel from **Visitors** to **Registered**, **Live Attended**, **Total Attended** and **Missed**. Two gaps tell you different things:"],[6364,"The webinar's **Settings** tab has an **End of Session** URL, which is empty by default. Point it at your offer or booking page so attendees aren't left sitting on a finished webinar. **Expired Session** does the same for anyone arriving at a link that has expired."],[1494,"The webinar's own follow-up emails"],[91,"The webinars themselves don't need to change. What changes is who signs in, and where each client's work lives. Staff need their own names on their own logins, and each firm needs a space of its own."],[4202,"The week before"],[4307,"The week-before and two-days-before parts of the checklist happen outside the room. These are the settings you can't change once the session has started without disturbing it."],[6715,"The weekly controls are easy to spot. With **Weekly** selected, the schedule step shows **Days of week**, **Session Time** with **Add Time**, and **Blackout Dates**. How this scheduling stacks up against other live webinar tools is covered in How live webinars compare, below."],[2171,"The White Label add-on removes EasyWebinar branding from the pages and the room. Together with C-Name, this is the usual agency combination: the client's webinars on the client's domain, with no EasyWebinar branding anywhere."],[4073,"The whole first part is teaching, and the offer comes once, near the close. It builds trust well and suits a cold audience meeting you for the first time. What it misses is attention: if the teaching overruns, the offer arrives after people have left."],[8964,"The widget is for keeping people on your site. If a visitor is already reading your page, the shortest path to a registration is a form right there, not a link to somewhere new."],[2621,"The workshop is free, and you sell a paid next step during it with an offer button. This catches the biggest audience. It misses the commitment a paid ticket brings, and it puts the sale at the end rather than the start."],[259,"Then describe what you're selling and where the button should send people, and pick an offer category: online course, coaching programme, membership, digital product or service package. Click **Generate Offers**."],[6360,"Then describe what you're selling and where the button should send people. Pick a category (online course, coaching programme, membership, digital product or service package) and click **Generate Offers**. You get complete offers, each with a title, headline, sub-headline, button text and link. Tick the offers you want to keep, then click **Save and continue**. The offers step costs 1 credit."],[3202,"There are four common ways a team splits the work of running webinars every month. Each one catches some problems and misses others."],[1563,"There are four common ways to follow up after a webinar. Each catches some people and misses others, which is why the choice between them matters."],[608,"There are four common ways to get a webinar landing page live. Each one catches some problems and misses others, so it helps to know which route you're on before you judge the result."],[829,"There are four common ways to get from a topic to a script. Each one catches some problems early and leaves others for the rehearsal."],[406,"There are four common ways to produce a webinar email sequence. Each catches some problems and misses others, which is why the AI draft works the way it does."],[2617,"There are four common ways to take money for a live session. Each one catches some problems and misses others, so it helps to see them side by side before you build."],[4273,"There are four common ways to test a webinar before going live. Each catches some problems and misses others, which is why the checklist uses more than one."],[2812,"There are three common next steps at the end of a coaching webinar. Pick one per session, because two buttons split attention. Each one suits a different kind of programme."],[7774,"There are three common ways a webinar gets recorded. Each one catches some failures and misses others, which is why the default matters."],[4072,"There are three common ways to arrange a webinar that ends in an offer. They share the same sections and differ in how early, and how often, the offer appears. Choosing one before you fill in the