"Yes. Turn off the other output chips before you click **Generate**. Script, Landing pages and Emails are selected by default."],[8753,"Yes. Turn on **Just-in-time Registration**, **Recurring Schedule** or both alongside it, and the registration page offers each as a choice."],[9506,"Yes. Turn on **Live + Replay bundle** on the pricing step and set a second price."],[8475,"Yes. Turn on **Notify admin** in **Email Settings** and give it an address. It sends a copy of registrations there."],[5042,"Yes. Turn on **On Demand** as well; **Schedule Preferences** then reads **Display Just-in-time & Instant-replay**."],[9340,"Yes. Turn on **Shorten URL's** in the Share dialog."],[6417,"Yes. Turn on the **Countdown timer** toggle when you edit the offer."],[6078,"Yes. Turn the poll's toggle off in the CTAs list, or set **Published** to off in the editor."],[6571,"Yes. Untick **Email notifications** under **What would you like to copy?**"],[5233,"Yes. Upload the trimmed file with Local import and EasyWebinar hosts it, or import it from YouTube, Vimeo or Wistia. Then select it on the video step."],[8899,"Yes. Use **Add Custom Field** on the form block for anything you need to ask."],[503,"Yes. Use **Add email** on the emails step of the build, or **Create New** in any group on the **Notifications** tab. Add a no-show follow-up and an offer-click follow-up for most webinars."],[6422,"Yes. Use **Attendee Action Follow-ups**, tag them with the **Clicked offer** event in your CRM, or filter **People** by offer clicks and export the list."],[6936,"Yes. Use **Change Video** in the **Webinar Video** panel on the **Content** tab. Schedule, pages and registrations stay as they are."],[4537,"Yes. Use **Columns**."],[5041,"Yes. Use **Exclude specific time** to keep those hours clear."],[310,"Yes. Use **Regenerate** on the script step. Change the number of slides or the duration first if you want a different result."],[1071,"Yes. Use **Regenerate** on the script step. If you change the slides or duration first, the new script follows those settings."],[9201,"Yes. Use **Tracking Code** in the builder's **Settings** for this page, or the **Tracking Code** sub-tab on the webinar's **Settings** tab."],[125,"Yes. Use the Co-presenter or Moderator join link from the Share dialog."],[2716,"Yes. Use the pencil beside **Registration Pricing** on the webinar's Overview tab to edit the title and price."],[9509,"Yes. Use the pencil on **Registration Pricing** in the Overview tab."],[3759,"Yes. When **Create with AI** builds a webinar, the email sequence includes an instant follow-up and one a day later. Every email stays editable, so you can split the audience and change the wording."],[497,"Yes. When EasyWebinar's AI builds a webinar, it drafts six emails from the webinar's script: a welcome email, three reminders and two follow-ups. You then edit them on the **Notifications** tab before anything sends."],[1458,"Yes. When you connect Amazon SES, SendGrid or Mailgun, EasyCRM sends through your account with that company rather than replacing it. So set up the account first, then bring its credentials to the **Configure** step on the **Email provider** tab."],[1974,"Yes. You can keep HubSpot, Salesforce Pardot (Account Engagement), ActiveCampaign, Mailchimp or Kit (formerly ConvertKit), or use EasyCRM natively. EasyCRM is optional for running webinars."],[7478,"Yes. You need a camera to enter, but you can switch **Camera** and **Mic** off in the green room and still go live."],[6146,"You also choose who can see the queue:"],[3301,"You archive it instead. Open the webinar's card menu and choose Archive, and it moves to the Archived tab, out of your main list, with its records kept."],[832,"You build the deck, then write notes under each slide. This catches visual flow, since every slide has a reason to be there. It misses the spoken links between slides, so the talk can feel like a list of points. It also tends to overrun, because slides get added without anyone counting the minutes."],[7588,"You can add a countdown timer to an offer card, and the offers page explains the editor. Payments run through your own Stripe account, and checkout starts on the Growth plan."],[2722,"You can add disclaimers and consent, keep chat private and set the replay to close. EasyWebinar holds a SOC 2 Type II report and follows GDPR-aligned data practices. Check your own sector's rules before you start."],[2678,"You can also check the **Revenue** total on the webinar Overview tab, next to **Registration Pricing**."],[7384,"You can also click **⋮** on the webinar's card in **My Webinars** and choose **Go Live**, which takes you into the same room."],[8820,"You can also restyle the form's submit button from the same place."],[3070,"You can also send each person's join link and replay link into your email tool's custom fields, so reminders can come from your own system."],[3706,"You can also send follow-ups from your own email tool. Map webinar events to tags so it knows what each person did, and create the tags in that tool first."],[6025,"You can build every poll before the webinar and keep some hidden:"],[3047,"You can build offers before the webinar and keep them hidden until the moment comes. On a live webinar you push the offer from the **Offers** panel in the room."],[6351,"You can build offers well before the webinar and keep them out of sight:"],[5700,"You can build the offer, countdown included, before the webinar and hold it back:"],[2345,"You can check one part of this on the offer editor shown further down this page. The **Countdown timer** is a single switch on the offer card, beside the price and the button settings. How that compares with other webinar platforms is covered in How EasyWebinar compares for sales webinars for small business, below."],[5136,"You can check the choice for yourself: the clone dialog lists eight items in three groups, with a **Select everything** option. How that choice sits against other platforms is covered in How EasyWebinar compares for evergreen webinars, below."],[3201,"You can check the clone choices on the page: registration pages, the schedule, replay settings, embed widgets, polls, offers, email notifications and integration settings. How those choices stand against other platforms is covered in How EasyWebinar compares for webinar management for teams, below."],[405,"You can check the defaults yourself: the at-a-glance table above lists all six emails and their timing. How that compares with other platforms is covered in How EasyWebinar compares for webinar email sequences, below."],[2811,"You can check the in-room offer, the countdown on it and the move from live to repeat in the comparison grid. That is covered in How EasyWebinar compares for coaching webinars, below."],[1016,"You can check this on the setup screen: five output chips, **Script**, **Landing pages**, **Emails**, **Polls** and **Offers**, sit under one prompt box. How that compares with other webinar platforms is covered in How EasyWebinar compares for AI for webinars, below."],[814,"You can fill in a template by hand, and the template further down this page gives you one to copy. Or you can have the AI write the first draft. In EasyWebinar, the AI asks for the number of slides and the duration, then writes the script slide by slide in named sections such as hook and welcome and problem framing."],[3377,"You can keep the tools you already run. Keep HubSpot, Salesforce Pardot (Account Engagement), ActiveCampaign, Mailchimp or Kit (formerly ConvertKit), or use EasyCRM natively: your CRM or ours, both are supported. Connecting a CRM and mapping your webinar data covers the set-up field by field."],[2920,"You can put a checkout link on the offer button, so people buy on your own checkout page. For a high-commitment programme, many coaches invite a call or an application first, because buyers usually want to talk before they commit."],[2374,"You can run a first sales webinar with general tools. It takes more steps, and the gaps between tools are where people get lost."],[4960,"You can run all three together, and the registration page offers each as a choice."],[2873,"You can run this whole recipe with a meeting tool, a spreadsheet and your email account. It works, and many coaches start this way."],[4272,"You can see the first part of this on the device check screen, shown in Set up and test in EasyWebinar, below. The button to enter stays greyed out until the room can see both a camera and a microphone. How that compares with other tools is covered in How EasyWebinar compares for webinar setup checklists, below."],[2613,"You can see the split for yourself on any paid webinar. The **Revenue by Source** view separates paid registrations from offers sold in the room. The way this compares with other platforms is covered in How EasyWebinar compares for paid webinars and workshops, below."],[3711,"You can send each person's replay link across as well, using the **Webinar Replay Link** field on the custom fields tab. Your email tool then fills it into the template."],[9120,"You can skip this step: until you select a template, the button reads **Skip & Create Webinar**, and you can choose a template later."],[6698,"You can skip this step. The button reads **Skip & Create Webinar** until you select a template, and you can choose one later."],[7410,"You can switch your camera and microphone off with **Camera** and **Mic** and stay live, which suits a session built on slides or a video rather than your face."],[506,"You can turn off any of the five output chips before generating, so you choose which parts the AI builds. The emails are built from the script, so keeping the script on gives the drafts their source material."],[6675,"You can't schedule a session in the past. Earlier dates are greyed out in the picker."],[7790,"You can't watch every recording end to end, so check two things that tell you most of what you need."],[4874,"You choose a recording and a schedule. EasyWebinar then plays the recording at each session time, and registrants join as they would a live webinar. On an evergreen webinar, each registrant picks from the just-in-time, on-demand or set times you switch on."],[1923,"You choose a type when you create a webinar, and the choice decides how the session is delivered. All three use the same pages, emails, polls, offers and analytics; only the delivery differs."],[7140,"You choose a type when you create a webinar. **The type can't be changed afterwards**, so it is worth deciding first."],[4771,"You choose the type on the first step of the builder, before anything else. The choice shapes how content is delivered, what your schedule looks like and what attendees can do in the room."],[3003,"You choose the type when you create the webinar, and it can't be changed afterwards."],[2623,"You choose the webinar type once, when you create it, and **the type can't be changed later**. Make this choice before you think about pricing."],[315,"You choose: **Soft Pitch**, **Balanced Approach** or **Direct Pitch**."],[609,"You control every word and block, and nothing appears that you didn't write. What it misses is speed. The thank-you and waiting pages need writing too, and a busy team often leaves them on their defaults."],[831,"You copy a set of section headings and fill in the blanks. This catches structure problems: you won't forget the housekeeping, the transition or the close. It misses voice, because template wording sounds like a template until you rewrite it. It also misses timing unless the template gives each section a share of the minutes."],[5140,"You copy the live webinar and play its recording. This keeps the exact session that converted and the setup around it. It can miss things that only made sense live, such as \"see you next Thursday\", references to today's date, or drawings you made on your slides."],[5138,"You create a new automated webinar and set up the pages, polls, offers and emails again. This lets you fix anything you disliked about the live version. It misses consistency: small differences creep in, and integration settings are easy to forget."],[2176,"You create an account for each client from inside your own, and manage them all from one place. This catches both earlier problems, because data stays apart and you keep control of set-up. The open question it leaves is billing, since each account carries its own plan, so agree who pays before you create one."],[2187,"You create each client workspace from your own account, so step 1 happens in one place. The C-Name add-on and a CNAME record cover step 2. For steps 3 and 4, an **Agency** registration template gives you a starting point, and **Clone Webinar** copies a finished funnel. Analytics and the People export cover steps 5 and 6, and a connected CRM handles step 7 as registrants and attendees arrive."],[833,"You describe the webinar and an AI writes the first draft. This catches the blank-page problem and gives you structure and timing at once. It misses your voice and your facts, so a generic prompt produces a generic script. You still have to read every line and check what it says about your offer."],[9623,"You don't have to add everyone here. The **Share** dialog has a **Co-presenter Join Link** and a **Moderator Join Link for Live room**, and a presenter or moderator can join with a link without having an EasyWebinar account at all."],[8996,"You don't have to choose one. You can use the widget on your own site and the link everywhere else."],[8279,"You don't have to visit the Library first. Simulive and evergreen webinars have a **Video Selection** step, after pricing, where you choose the recording the sessions will play."],[123,"You don't invite; you create their account with **Add user**, including their password, then pass the details to them."],[1549,"You don't need EasyCRM to run webinars. If your follow-up already runs in HubSpot, Salesforce Pardot (Account Engagement), ActiveCampaign, Mailchimp or Kit, you can connect that CRM and map your webinar data instead. Connected contacts can be tagged by what they did, such as attended, missed or clicked your offer, and your own CRM's automation takes it from there."],[1828,"You don't need EasyCRM to run webinars. It's for managing the people your webinars produce. Registrants reach it by connecting EasyCRM as an integration on a webinar, as on this page, or with **Import from EasyWebinar** on its Leads screen."],[3620,"You don't need to write these in the order they send. This order gets the most important messages finished first."],[1340,"You don't need your own domain to send broadcasts, because a single business email address also counts as a sending identity. The choice depends on how much you send and how much setup you can take on now."],[2661,"You enter through a green room and backstage where nothing is broadcast. A co-host or moderator lands backstage, not on stage. They can watch chat and Q&A until you bring them on. The live webinar walkthrough covers going live and ending the session."],[1564,"You export the list and write the emails yourself. It catches whoever you remember, on the day you get to it. It misses anyone who needed a second message, and it stops entirely in a busy week."],[1020,"You get a welcome email, three reminders and two follow-ups, with merge tags already in place for dates, times and join links. They sit alongside your webinar reminder emails settings, where you can add more."],[260,"You get complete offers with a title, headline, sub-headline, button text and link. Select the ones you want and click **Save and continue**."],[455,"You get the sequence by building the webinar with AI, then finish it on the **Notifications** tab. The AI webinar builder overview covers the whole build."],[1303,"You have written your first broadcast, and it can't go out yet because sending isn't set up. That setup is what this page covers."],[1567,"You import registrants into EasyCRM, save them as lists and build a workflow for each list. This catches people across the weeks after a webinar, not just the next day. Like any automation, it sends exactly what you built, to everyone on the list, so it needs checking before it goes live."],[2174,"You keep a single account and change the logo and colours on each client's pages. It is quick to start and needs only one plan. It misses data separation: every client's registrants sit in one list, and anyone on your team can see all of them. It also leaves every link on your vendor's address unless you add a domain."],[2355,"You keep the session purely educational and make the offer in the follow-up emails. It suits a very cautious audience. The cost is time: a 2011 Harvard Business Review article found that most companies weren't responding to online leads nearly fast enough."],[5377,"You need a recording. EasyWebinar can host it for you, or play one from YouTube, Vimeo or Wistia. You can also add it during set-up without leaving the flow, so a missing file doesn't stop you."],[4826,"You need a recording. EasyWebinar can host it, or play one from YouTube, Vimeo or Wistia, and you can add it during setup without leaving the flow. Any team member can create a webinar, and the team members guide explains roles. To charge for registration, connect a Stripe account first."],[7819,"You need a scheduled live webinar, which Create and schedule a live webinar walks through. You also need a working camera and microphone to enter the room, even if you turn them off afterwards."],[7582,"You need a working camera and microphone to enter, and the **Continue to Green Room** button stays greyed out until the room can see both. You can turn them off afterwards. If the room can't find your camera, the camera troubleshooting guide covers the usual causes."],[7868,"You need both to enter the live room, where recording happens, even if you turn them off afterwards. The **Continue to Green Room** button stays greyed out until the room detects them."],[4277,"You open the attendee view on a phone, or in a narrow browser window. This catches slides and shared screens that are too small to read. It matters because attendees on phones, and in browser windows narrower than 768 pixels, can't zoom in on what you share."],[4274,"You open the room on your own and look at your camera, your microphone level and your speakers. This catches the most common failure, a device the browser can't use. In Chrome, for example, a site that isn't allowed your camera or microphone won't work for video calls, and on a work computer your network administrator may control those settings. It misses everything attendees see."],[1566,"You pass webinar activity to a connected CRM as tags, and that CRM's automation sends the sequence. This catches everything your CRM can act on. The cost is a second tool to set up, and a sequence that only works if the mapping is right."],[9115,"You pick the registration page's look on **Step 4 of 4 — Registration Template** when you create a webinar manually."],[2347,"You present in real time and attendees join at the scheduled moment. Choose live when the value is in you being there, such as a launch or a workshop. You can create and schedule a live webinar in four steps, and recording can be switched on so you keep the session."],[1924,"You present in real time at a set date and time. Attendees join at that moment, and chat and Q&A happen as they happen. Choose a live webinar when people come for the interaction: a launch, a workshop or a question session."],[7141,"You present in real time at a specific date and time. Chat and Q&A happen as they happen. Choose Live when people come for the interaction: a launch, a workshop, a session where you answer real questions."],[5328,"You present in real time, and attendees join at the scheduled moment. Chat and Q&A happen as they happen, and you can react to the room. This catches everything a live audience brings, such as a sharp question that changes the talk. It misses the point of a repeated session, because you pay the full presenting time again for content that hasn't changed."],[2486,"You present in real time, and attendees join at the scheduled moment. Chat and questions happen as they happen. A live webinar is best for a launch, a workshop or any session where people come for the conversation. It is not the right fit when nobody can be there at the set time, every time."],[5139,"You present the session again to an empty room and use that recording. This removes live mistakes, dated remarks and long waits at the start. It misses the energy and real audience questions that made the live version work, and the new take is untested."],[5119,"You ran a live webinar last month, the pitch landed and people bought. Now new visitors arrive on your page every week, and the next live date is weeks away."],[2487,"You record the session in advance, and it plays at set times. Everyone in a session watches together, and the recording plays while you and your moderators answer in the chat live. Simulive suits a talk you give again and again, or a session in a time zone you'd rather sleep through. It is not for a session you haven't recorded yet."],[408,"You reuse the previous sequence and change the details. It's quick and keeps a format that already worked. It misses stale details: an old topic, date or offer left in one email is easy to overlook when the rest looks right."],[4275,"You run the session end to end, with a colleague registered as an ordinary attendee on a second device. This catches registration, reminders, the attendee view, polls and the redirect at the end. It takes the most time, so do it a day or two before, not on the day."],[7759,"You see the recording status before you broadcast anything. When you enter the room you land in the green room, where nothing reaches your audience. Its header shows **Not Live** and whether recording is on."],[2620,"You sell access elsewhere and post the join link inside it. This suits hosts who already sell there. It misses the workshop's own numbers, because sales and attendance live in different places."],[2619,"You sell on a payment page and send the link afterwards. This works with any payment tool. It misses timing: anyone who pays late, or whose email is filtered, depends on you noticing in time."],[4276,"You send each reminder or text message to yourself before any registrant gets it. This catches wrong times, broken links and messages that read badly on a phone. It misses whether the room itself works."],[1018,"You set the number of slides and the duration, and the AI writes the script in named sections, each with timing and speaker notes. **Download PDF** lets you present from it. This is the part to read most carefully, because the pages and emails are built from it."],[9507,"You set the price in US dollars. Stripe may show attendees outside the US the price in their local currency."],[67,"You set their password and nothing is emailed to them. There is no invitation to accept, so pass the login details on yourself and ask them to change the password once they are in."],[4883,"You set this with the **Interval** setting on the schedule step: every 2, 15, 30, 45 or 60 minutes. **Exclude specific time** keeps chosen hours free of just-in-time sessions."],[8027,"You shouldn't. The webinar plays that video, so removing it leaves the webinar without its content."],[7172,"You show polls and offers in the room."],[4161,"You still set the schedule yourself, and a simulive or evergreen webinar needs a video chosen afterwards."],[2353,"You teach and mention the offer at natural points, then close with a clear invitation. People who leave halfway have still heard what you sell. The risk is that each mention interrupts the teaching if it isn't tied to what you just showed."],[2352,"You teach for most of the session and introduce the offer in the last few minutes. It feels least pushy and builds trust with a cold audience. It misses anyone who leaves early, and on a first webinar that can be a large share of the room."],[407,"You write every email yourself, in the platform's editor. It catches tone and detail, because you know the audience. It misses time: the work repeats for every webinar, and the later emails in the sequence are the ones that get rushed or skipped."],[830,"You write every word yourself, in your own voice. This catches tone problems early, because the script already sounds like you. It misses timing, since you rarely know how long a page of text takes to say until you read it aloud. It is also the slowest route, and it is where most webinar prep stalls."],[3689,"You'll find fuller guidance on these options in setting up a replay page, and the same link also sits in the webinar's Share dialog. Anyone with that link can watch while the page is on, so treat it as public. A replay isn't the same as instant viewing on demand, which lets someone watch as soon as they register. Our post on using replays to increase sales goes further on the selling side."],[3028,"You'll need a recording. Upload it and EasyWebinar hosts it (MP4, up to 4 GB), or play one already on YouTube, Vimeo or Wistia. You can swap the recording later from the **Webinar Video** panel on the Content tab without disturbing your schedule or registrations."],[230,"You're asked for the number of **slides** and the **duration**, then the script is written slide by slide."],[672,"You're asked for the number of slides and the duration, then the script is written slide by slide. Read it now. Change the slides or duration and click **Regenerate** if it's off, then click **Continue to landing pages**."],[7385,"You're asked to choose your **camera, microphone and speakers**. Pick each one, then click **Continue to Green Room**."],[5917,"You're on a live webinar. Third-party chat is only available on simulive and evergreen webinars."],[2333,"You've got a service or product that people buy once they understand it, and explaining it one call at a time is eating your week. A sales webinar lets you explain it once to a room of interested people, then make the offer while their interest is high."],[5309,"You've run the same 45-minute session on a Tuesday evening for months, and the questions are always the same. Presenting it again costs you the evening, and a bad connection or a cold can still spoil it. A simulated live webinar, often shortened to simulive, swaps the presenting for a recording and keeps the shared room."],[2962,"Your brand"],[2154,"Your client doesn't have to change systems to work with you, because it's your CRM or ours. Keep HubSpot, Salesforce Pardot (Account Engagement), ActiveCampaign, Mailchimp or Kit (formerly ConvertKit), or use EasyCRM natively."],[8778,"Your CRM"],[9237,"Your CRM"],[9239,"Your domain"],[9065,"Your domain and branding"],[2960,"Your email list"],[4114,"Your filled-in outline becomes this prompt, so the audience, the three teaching points and the offer are all decided before anything is generated."],[3910,"Your invitation is sent from this screen, and its rates join the averages at the top. The figures shown are sample data for a demo brand."],[2365,"Your offer is a card with a headline and a button, shown in the room while people watch. Build it before the session on the **CTAs** tab, and keep its toggle off until you're ready to show it. This video shows an offer being made to attendees during a webinar, without sending them away first."],[24,"Your plan"],[458,"Your prompt is the source for the script and every drafted email, so describe the audience, topics and offer here before you generate."],[7448,"Your recording appears in **Library → Recordings**. With the replay page set to **Webinar Replica**, the replay shows the same video, offers, polls and chat replay as the live session. See Replay pages and The Library."],[3040,"Your registrants stay with the original, so the evergreen version starts fresh, and the original is untouched. Cloning is also the safe way to test a new registration page or offer on a webinar that's already selling: change the copy, not the original."],[9152,"Your registration link doesn't work until the page is published. Creating the webinar doesn't make it public either. Each of the five pages has its own **Publish** button."],[2951,"Your registration page"],[6869,"Your registration page can then be edited in the drag-and-drop builder. It doesn't go public until you click **Publish**. See Registration page builder."],[2359,"Your registration page is the link you promote, so it carries most of the work before the session. It needs a headline that names the result, three or four things people will learn, the date and time, and a form."],[9165,"Your registration page lives on your subdomain, ending in `.easywebinar.live`, set in your profile. The **C-Name** add-on, with a CNAME record at your DNS provider, puts the pages on your own domain. The **White Label** add-on removes EasyWebinar branding. Changing your subdomain changes your existing links, so set it before you promote anything."],[2844,"Your replay is part of the pitch, so give it the same closing date as your offer. On the **Replay** tab, **Expire after days** closes the replay a set number of days after the session. **Specific date** closes it at a date and time you choose, which suits a cohort with a fixed start."],[1808,"Your rules appear under **Current mappings**."],[3592,"Your session has just ended. Some people attended and some never made it, and each group needs a different next message."],[2170,"Your webinar pages normally sit on an EasyWebinar subdomain. With the C-Name add-on, links read like `webinars.clientcompany.com` instead. A link that doesn't look like the client's own business invites hesitation at the moment someone decides to register."],[4201,"Your webinar setup checklist"],[7592,"Zooming in helps people who read small text with difficulty. The GOV.UK Service Manual notes that some users use a screen magn